      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>CHAT and GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>CHAT and GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>By NIARJORIE.</p>
        <p>The Debut of Princess Mary. Although Court festivities of all kinds will be tabooed as long as the war lasts, the Sing and Queen at present intend to hold the usual number of Courts, at the first of which Princess Mary, who will then be eighteen, will take her place in the Royal circle. That the only daughter of Their Majesties should make her debut when the country is overshadowed by a great crisis, is a matter for regret. Very few private balls will be given for the Princess this year, and it is quite doubtful if a Court Ball will take place, unless it should be a cele- bration of the peace everyone so ardently desires. The Sing will hold levies, but not the usual number. The coming out of the Princess will always be associated with the greatest of wars, which, however, has united all classes, as no other event could have done, and from which we confidently hope our Empire will emerge, stronger and greater than at any previous period. Austrat'an Women and the War. The omen of Australia have not been behind other Oversea Dominions in the help they have sent to the Mother Country. The work of the Red Cross was but vaguely understood in the ' Island Continent before the war. Lady Helen Munro Fergussen had, however, gained experi- ence in this country, and has been magnificently helped by voluntary workers, who rapidly entered into the spirit of the organisation. The ball-room of the Federal Government House at Melbourne has been turned into a workroom. Numerous cases containing articles made by members of the society have been forwarded to Sir George Reid, the High Commissioner for Australia in London. Over fifty-two thousand pounds in money has been contributed, and men and money are still being raised. The unit equipped by Melbourne and sent to the seat of war comprises a medical service of a field ambulance, with 100 beds; one clearing hospital, with 200 beds; two stationary hospitals, each containing 200 beds; and two general hospitals, providing accommodation for 520 patients; making a grand total of nearly 1,800 beds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>HOME NOTES. Profitable Recipes and Useful</head>
        <p>HOME NOTES. Profitable Recipes and Useful</p>
        <p>,E CHILDREN'S- CORNER. irtz C onducted by 'UNCLE NED. A HEARTY INVITATION to join this COVROT extended to all children who read it regu- larly. Application for membership, stating name, address and age, should be made to: " UNCLE NED, 'Examiner" Office, Bewsey- street, Warrington." Every girl or boy, whose name is in the Big Book, is entitled to enter the competitions held from time to time, to the winners of which useful prizes are awarded.</p>
        <p>ANOTHER NEW MEMBER. MY DEAR CHILDREN,— This week we welcome another new member to our Corner: ROBERT I. CROOK, Blue Coat School. Robert sends me a poem or two, and as I am sore you all like to see what your fellow-mem- bers can write, I give below one of these poems. Robert also wrote nice sets of verses upon "British Heroes of the War" and "Hail, Christmastide," and you will be as sorry as I am that space does not permit these to appear in our Corner.</p>
        <p>Your loving UNCLE NED.</p>
        <p>THOUGHT FOR THE WEEK. We can all go straight forward and do our 'cluty.—Duke of Wellington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>Suggestions.</head>
        <p>Suggestions.</p>
        <p>Fur. To clean white fur rugs lay the fur on a table and rub with bran, moistened with warm water. Rub until dry, then rub with dry bran. Use flannel for rubbing with the wet bran and book muslin for the dry bran. After doing this rub with magnesia. Rub against the way of the fur.</p>
        <p>, t , ts !',.. ' ,-,,,,1,;,4 ' 5' 4 , ,-</p>
        <p>',:" 4.: i'4', ' :' i',-. .•:,..,!: ',i.x/,.: - '''.* .. - ~,,,,, ~. ~,:,,,,, ~W</p>
        <p>Brass. Brass can be silver-plated without the use of a battery. The first essential is that the metal be chemically clean, which is best done by the use of dilute nitric acid, followed by a wash with clean water and then with dilute aqua ammonia, drying in sawdust. If the metal be then rubbed with chloride of silver dissolved in water and washed and again dried in sawdust the result will be fine. It should, however, be immediately lacquered in order to preserve the surface.</p>
        <p>,</p>
        <p>Beef Steak With Onions. Cut one dozen onions into slices; fry lb. of salt pork or bacon until all the fat is fried out, then take out the crackling; into this hot fat put the onions. Fry and stir for twenty minutes over a good fire, then add a teaspoon- ful of salt, a dash of black pepper, and one cup of boiling water; place over a more moder- ate fire to simmer for half an hour. By this time the water should have entirely evaporated, and the onions should be a nice brown. Have ready a broiled steak, place it in the pan with the onions, cover it over the top with some of them, and stand in the oven for five minutes. Then place the steak on a hot dish, heap the onions over and around, and serve.</p>
        <p>Roast Tongue. Properly roasted, a fresh ox or calf's tongue is a delicious meat, of good flavour and of „melt- ing tenderness. Select a tongue 'weighing 31b. Wash and trim it thoroughly, and rub it with salt and pepper. Wrap around it a paste made of a pint of flour and a cupful of water. Roll out this paste thin, place it around the tongue. Lay it on a meat rack in a dripping-10, pour</p>
