      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>WOMAN'S --'";..7.,:'.1 lILD.</head>
        <p>WOMAN'S --'";..7.,:'.1 lILD.</p>
        <p>used as fringe for tunics. Broad-tail is much more expensive, but certainly very distinguished looking. All fur coats and capes have gorgeous linings of brocade in brilliant colourings, and a beautiful contrast is afforded by ermine of the tail-less variety, and tangerine red brocade. Long coats in velvet or other rich materials, made in one piece, are much worn for evening, and these also have very handsome and brightly- coloured linings.</p>
        <p>Blouses to Buy. The white satin blouse shirt is as popular as at any period of its history, for it has many attractions and is quite as warm as those made of woollen materials. Therefore short lengths of white satin should be bought during the sales, for there is no doubt that clothes will get dearer rather than cheaper if the war continues throughout the year. Delicate .blouses in well- covered patterns have pretty touches of black, which gives them a distinction-. Those in winter 'weight crepon are particularly attractive when thus finished, and during the sales are sold very cheaply. The baggy sleeves that have been so characteristic of last year's blouses and cor- sages have had their day, and will be replaced by a comfortably loose, but more reasonable shape. All collars are high, and those with fur edging are the highest of all. Although the waists to coats get longer every day, there are many indications that ,the shapeless waist is, like the baggy sleeve, on the wane. The fash-. ions prevailing in 1860-70 will be -revived, and the slightly defined waist, if nothing else, will prove an inevitable result of the revival. Fashionable Footwear.</p>
        <p>With shorter skirts, footgear and hosiery be- come much more important. Unfortunately, leather is much dearer than twelve months ago, and will be still dearer; therefore, bargains during the sales should be secured. Lower heels both to boots and shoes have been tried, but they find no favour with modish women. Tbe newest shoes have square toes, and boots lave cloth tops. Wooden heels are now covered with celluloid, which is an improvement on leatiler. Glace boots with fawn tops are very generally worn, as are also black patent leatil..?r with white tops, and next summer these styles will be still more popular. Leather laces are a novelty. Jet is combined with pearls to form buckles for evening shoes, many of which are made of rich Oriental brocade. Parisian women are taking very little interest in clothes, which are severely simple in character, but their footgear absorbs their attention, and is more ornate than any other item of dress.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>American Women and tha War.</head>
        <p>American Women and tha War.</p>
        <p>American women wt.() have married English- men are doing a great work for the British soldiers. Six hundred patients, many very seriously wounded, have passed through the hospital in Mr. Singer's house at Paignton. Only one has died, although several have suf- fered from telanus. American women presented the War Office with six motor ambulances that have done excellent work, a seventh being sent by the women of Boston. In addition, they have started an Economic Relief Fund for assisting their countrywomen in Germany and Austria to return to the United States. Other committees have rendered valuable help, more especially those in New York and Panama, and all gifts have been most judiciously distributed. The Duchess of Roxburgh is the , president of the base hospital of the Allied troops at Boulogne. The New Skirt.</p>
        <p>. _ There is little doubt that the war will have a sobering effect on women's and men's clothes, for a very long time to come. Nor will this prove a drawback, for dress had become almost an infatuation with many women, whose one ambition seemed to be,to appear in a new and ' startling frock at every function they attended. There is now so little in the way of entertain- ment and so much ,sadness that there is little demand for variety in dress. Still there will be drastic changes in styles as the spring advances, and the full skirt will be seen in all thin, soft and pliable fabrics. Its fulness is gathered at the waist and falls in flutes round the ankles, the hips being slender as in the old silhouette. Flounces will be the predominant note in even- ing gowns, for they are a change from the tunic which has reigned an unusually long time. At present flounces cling to the figure, and they are usually three in number; liter on they, too, will, in. all probability, flare out round the ankles. There will, however, be less change in dresses and suits made of heavy cloths and tweeds, and these will be very short:</p>
        <p>Bargains in Furs. Furs are some of the best bargains in this year's sales, and among those to be recom- mended are the whole fox skins, which, at the reduced price, are very cheap, and they wear well. Another very good-looking and low-priced pelt is the long-haired goat, which takes a black dye remarkably well. Nutria is worn very much this year that also is inexpensive, and is largely used for toques and turbans of the Russian type, for which it is quite suitable. Monkey furs of the finer sorts are extensively to the fore,</p>
        <p>in a pint of boiling water. Roast it for two hours. Keep the, surface of the paste from drying too hard, or from burning, by basting it with the boiling dripping. At the end .of this time remove the paste. It is of no further value. Loosen the skin of the tongue and peel it off. Lay it back in the pan, and rub it over with butter, about one tablespoonful, or more if necessary; dredge it lightly with flour, and pour a cupful of rich brown stock in the pan under it to baste it with. Roast it, basting it often, until it is well browned. Take it up, and if mushrooms are procurable add a dozen, chopped fine, to the brown gravy in the pan, thicken, it, and serve with the tongue.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>HERE AND THERE.</head>
        <p>HERE AND THERE.</p>
        <p>The Testing Time. You can't tell anything about the language a man has in reserve until he has lost his last collar button and is trying to catch the next train.</p>
        <p>His Discovery. "Their honeymoon is about over." "What's the matter?" "He's come to the conclusion that it really isn't fun to help her wash the dishes!!</p>
        <p>Forced to Keep House. "Thought you were going to quit keeping house and live in a hotel?" "We did intend to." "What made you change your mind F" "The cook wouldn't leave."</p>
        <p>Holding Back the News. "I suppose your wife was tickled to death at your rise in salary?" "She will be." "Haven't you told her yet?" "No; I thought I would enjoy myself for a couple of weeks first."</p>
        <p>The Perfect Wife.</p>
        <p>"Why do you feed every tramp who comes along? They never do any work for you." "No," said the wife, "but it is quite a satis- faction to me to see a man eat a meal without finding fault with the cooking."</p>
        <p>tall man replied in a kindly tone: "Why do you want a guilder so much, my boy? Are you hungry?" "It's not that," panted the boy. "Mother's very ill, and the doctor won't come to see her unless I give him a guilder first, and I haven't got as much as a cent., and perhaps mother may die in the meantime."</p>
        <p>The stranger paused, and looked keenly at the boy, his large eyes seeming to glow like live coals under the dark shadow of his slouch hat. Such tales were told every day by the knaves and beggars of the town, but the poor little beggar looked thin and miserable enough to make his assertion that he had no money only too probable, and the earnestness of his voice and look could not be mistaken.</p>
        <p>"Here's a guilder for you, my lad. Go quick and fetch the doctor, and I'll look after your mother in the meantime if you'll tell me where she lives."</p>
        <p>The boy hastily gave him the address, and muttering a few broken words of thanks, darted away like an arrow from a bow. "So here's an adventure already," mused the unknown, "and a strange one it seems likely to be. There are doctors in this town it would seem who would let a sick woman die rather than lose a guilder. And yet people call me hard-hearted. Bah 1"</p>
        <p>Just then he passed a baker's store, and a sudden thought appeared to strike him. He went in and bought a small brown loaf, taking care to choose the freshest he could find. In this part of the town most men were fond of keeping their faces hidden, so that the baker was not surprised to see his customer pull his hat over his eyes as he came in; but it struck him that the hand which took the loaf was far too white and well-shaped to suit the greasy, threadbare cloak that hung over it. The stranger pushed on at a brisk pace through the maze of crooked, gloomy alleys, and soon reached the house to which the boy had directed him.</p>
        <p>A dismal den it was, much more like a wild beast lair than the abode of a human being. Not without more than one awkward stumble did the visitor make his way up the pitch-dark and tumble-down stairs to the small, bare, chilly garret, in which the sick woman was lying with nothing but an old cloak between her and the 'hard plank floor. He greeted her kindly, and she, supposing him to be the doctor whom her son had gone to fetch, told him all about her illness readily enough. But he • soon saw that whit she was suffering from was simply starva- tion, and breaking up the loaf which he had brought he proceeded to feed her with the soft- est morsels of it.</p>
        <p>'"Now," said he, seeing she was beginning to revive a little, "I'll write down the name of a medicine, which I think will do you good. Send your son with this paper to-morrow morning "to 23, Ferdinand-street, and they'll give you what you want, and not cli,arge you anything forit." •</p>
        <p>;re,.</p>
        <p>_</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>HINTS ON HEALTH. Useful Information in Popular Language.</head>
        <p>HINTS ON HEALTH. Useful Information in Popular Language.</p>
        <p>Rheumatic Gout.</p>
        <p>Very often patients who have rheumatic gout have eczema, psoriasis, and other forms of skin disease. Very Often the skin is ,sallow. In many cases there are brown spots on the hands and other parts of the body, unfailing signs of chronic" intestinal intoxication. To, get rid of these poisons one must adopt an antitoxic diet consisting chiefly of fruits and vegetables. Cereals should be sparingly eaten, largely be- cause they contain an excess of acids. A diet of all kinds of fruits and vegetables is indi- cated. A person who has rheumatism of any sort has an excessively acid state of the body. Seasonable Dont's.</p>
        <p>Don't wear more clothing than is really neces- sary for comfort. Many people render them- selves sensitive to cold by wearing too much _clothing. Don't forget, on' going out of doors, to slip on an outer garment of some kind if the temperature is considerably lower than the in- door temperature; especially protect the head and the feet. Don't dress the neck too warm when going out in cold weather. A little extra protection is required for the ears, but it is not necessary to muffle up the neck with thick furs to protect the ears. A light scarf or ear muffs are all that is needed. Warm wrappings about the neck cause the skin of the neck to become moistened with perspiratiort. When the wrappings are removed indoors, the slow cooling which takes place in consequence 'of the evaporation chills the parts, and may produce sore throat or nasal c-atarrh. Don't forget, on going to bed at night, to hang up the under- clothing in some place where it will air over- night. It is a good plan to lay the clothing over a warm steam coil when it is convenient to do so. Persons who perspire freely should employ two suits of underclothing, wearing each every other day, allowing one day for air- ing and drying. Don't wear thin-soled shoes. One may take cold from chilling of the feet as the result of wearing thin-soled shoes in walk- ing over a cold pavement, even wben the pave- ment is perfectly dry.</p>
        <p>"May God bless you," said the poor woman faintly.</p>
        <p>At that moment the rickety stairs creaked beneath a heavy tread, and into the room strode the doctor with the boy at his heels. " Hello !" said he roughly, "what sort of a dog's-hole is this that you've brought me to?" "If it be a dog-hole," said the stranger eyeing him sternly, "it's all the fitter for you."</p>
        <p>"What a.; you mean by that, you rascal?" roared the burly doctor, stalking up to him, "Do you know who I am?" "Yes, I do," retorted the other with crushing contempt. "You are a doctor without brains, and a man without heart."</p>
        <p>\ The answer to this taunt was a blow, which would have ended the dispute at once had not the 1 unknown leaped nimbly aside. But as he did so his hat fell off, disclosing features at the sight of which the bullying doctor started back as if he had seen a ghost, his coarse red face growing as pale as death.</p>
        <p>- - " Do ym know who I am?" asked the stranger, "you look as if you did." He did, indeed, for he slunk out of the room like a beaten dog without daring to utter a word. Meanwhile the sick woman, who had been listening in amazement, opened the paper given by the stranger and read, "Pay to bearer the sum of forty ducats, Joseph." "The Emperor," cried she, clasping her hands. "The Emperor," echoed the son. "Is this kind gentleman really the Emperor himself?" "Yes, my boy," said Joseph 11., laying his hand tenderly upon the curly head, "and as you have been such a good son to your sick mother I promise you I won't lose sight or either her or you." And the Emperor kept his promise.</p>
        <p>A CHRISTMAS STORY. (BY ROBERT I. CROOK!) 'Twas ,Christmas Eve; deep was the snow; A waif, wrapt in a shawl, With face and feet turned blue with cold. Crouched, shiv'ring, 'gainst a wall. She had no home, poor little child, Her parents'both were dead; She thought of children who were rich— Then of herself—and sighed. Of Father Christmas then she thought, He seemed to speak to her— "I'll take you where you want to go, I'll take you anywhere!" "Oh! take me back to mother, dear, Please, Daddy Christmas, do; Pvet always loved my mother dear, And now I will love you." Old Daddy Christmas took her up, He carried her away; She'd gone tc; meet her "Heavenly Dad," To spend her Christmas Day! The. people found her corpse next morn, "She's dead, poor thing," said they; They never dreamed how happily She'd spent- her Christittei Pay 4„,</p>
