      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 16, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 16, 1915.</p>
        <p>41:6+404042.0.0,0 000 000 4)4:":' 4":64"C+,44".),+ •:,•:•+,+•:"0":":'•)+4,•:"&gt;•:. +46 00 4•+ 04&gt;</p>
        <p>CONSCRIPTION?</p>
        <p>An Old Man's Reasons Why It Is</p>
        <p>Not Needed.</p>
        <p>FREEDOM VERSUS PRESSURE.</p>
        <p>Rain,! Rain ! ! Rain! I ! And it was raining, :oo !</p>
        <p>My fireside friend, the veteran, gazed thought- fully out of the window, accompanying his evi- dent meditation with a frequent shake of the head.</p>
        <p>"Now, then," I said, startling him out of his reverie. "What are your thoughts worth?" "Very little, my lad," he said, rousing him- self up; "just an old man's gaze into the past. It is a sort of habit we old folk have." Habit or no habit, I knew my old friend better than that; when he did gaze into the past there was a reason for it. So settling back in my chair with an evident expectation of something good, I was just on the point of questioning him again, but he forestalled me.</p>
        <p>"Guess you seem anxious to know, eh? Well, I was just thinking of the boys in the trenches at the Front on a day like this. It make me wish I was young again." Here he paused, as if in contemplation of the days long gone by, and then suddenly he ex- claimed, "It has been magnificent, my lad, magnificent !" "What has been magnificent?" I queried;</p>
        <p>" the courage of the soldiers in the trenches?" "Well, yes, that has been very good," he re- ptied ; "but what I was thinking about then was the way in which the young men of our country have answered the call to arms! It has been splendid !" Here I scented an opportunity of drawing the old man out on a favourite topic, and asked him whether he thought the response had fully justi- fied the retention of the voluntary. system; or, if not, was he in favour of conscription?</p>
        <p>, He looked at me savagely, as he replied, "Con- scription, did you say? I hate that word. But," he said, in a calmer tone, "there is no need to fall out about it; we never will have conscrip- tion."</p>
        <p>"We never will?" I exclaimed, bearing in mind the attempt that is being made by some to force the question of conscription to the front. "I wish you could fully assure me on that point." _ _ _</p>
        <p>"Why, lad," and his eyes sparkled as he spoke; "you have no better proof than the way Lord Kitchener touchedosupon the subject the other day. He fairly gave the conscriptionists a cold douche when, in the House of Lords, he told them he was fully satisfied with the progress of the recruiting." _ _</p>
        <p>"But," I argued, "in some places there has been a very poor response, and, maybe, to save our honour, the conscriptionists think the ex- treme step should be taken."</p>
        <p>- - "Yes, yes;" the old man said, eagerly', "hut then you must not forget that in these districts it is quite possible that the men are at work on Government contracts, which, as Lord Kitch- ener, said, was quite as important as being at the Front, but, in other places, why, bless you, I read that in one town they have sent 10,000 men, and have promised more. Ah, yes, Lord Haldane was right when he said that the volun- tary system was the best, and would always remain so." The old man laughed as I asked, "Has the voluntary system always stood the test?- Have we never been in the position of bringing in</p>
        <p>conscription?" "There may have been one or two instances where the press gang have been sent out in</p>
        <p>order to impress m- en- into the service," he an-</p>
        <p>swered. "The Crimean War was one, I believe. But as for conscription, well, the voluntary</p>
        <p>system has nearly always succeeded. But, ah wait a moment, I believe, if it has not been</p>
        <p>taken from the Statute Book, and I have never heard that it has been, there is a species of con- scription law in existence, but few people are aware of it. It has alwaye been suspended by an Act of Parliament which is passed in the expiring hours of every annual session of Par- liament." "And what would happen if the passing of this Act were omitted?" I queried anxioirsly-.</p>
        <p>" Ah ! no need to be afraid, my lad," he said; "of course, if the passage of the Suspension Act were omitted any year, and if the Act still re- mained on the Statute Book, the Militia ballot system of conscription would once more come into legal force."</p>
        <p>Asked whether the canseriptionists had ever</p>
        <p>tried to put this, Act into operation, the veteran smiled. "Oh, yes," he said, "several times, but you see the reigning Government has always turned a deaf ear, becauSe, in the first place, ex-</p>
        <p>perience has shown that conscription rarely fur- nishes an efficient fighting force, as you know one volunteer is worth three pressed men; and it cannot be looked upon as a very valuable factor in the system of military defence of any ' country. And, again, the idea of compelling 'men to fight has always been repugnant to Eng- lish-speaking races." "So that at no time had England suffered from lack of men?" He paused. "There may have been odd in- stances, but very rare," he continued, "espe- cially when men have properly understood the nature of the war they were engaged in. The Crimean War, as I gained -from my reading of it, was such an instance. It was because men did not understand to the full extent for what they were 'fighting, or for whose sake the war had been undertaken, that England had to turn to other sources. But in the Napoleonic wars every Briton knew that the chief aim of the first Napoleon was to crush England, and con- sequently every Briton was prepayed to do his utmost, as he is doing to-day, in a somewhat similar case, to destroy the power of 'Bony,' and thus preserve his native country from inva- sion." "And so, since the Crimean War, the volun- tary system has stood the test?" 'T asked. "Stood the test?" said the veteran. "I should think 'so. Look at the South African War. The War Offices were literally overwhelmed with offers of service. Why, my lad, men who volun- teer of their own accord to fight constitute tar better fighting material than those who are forced to bear arms against their will." He paused, as if in meditation, and then con- tinued, "Why, yes, I remember reading about the time of the South African War, in one of our leading journals, a statement that we had 4,000,000 men capable of bearing arms in this country. I was surprised, but it helped me in my thought that conscription would not be needed." "I have noticed," I said, "that our soldiers at the Front display more -indomitable courage than their German foes." "Of course they do," he replied, 'and it all goes to prove that so long as we; as a nation, are not intent upon a course of conquest and mili- tary aggression, conscription is not, nor never will be, required. "And so you are perfectly satisfied with our present system?" "Satisfied?" he said, "eh ! more than satis- fied. If our lads continue to enrol as they are doing at the present time the compulsory system will, as Lord Haldane and his colleagues pointed out, never be needed.". , "Are you satisfied?" he asked, as be rose to take his leave, the rain having by this time abated. "Quite," I replied, as I shook his hand; "What with men like you to cheer us on, and lads ready, eh! and Willing, to sacrifice their all for King and country, how could I be any other way?"</p>
        <p>"Just so," he answered, as he turned to go, on his journey homewards, "and," he paused as if forgetting he had left something out, "this is a righteous cause in which we are engaged, and one in which we are called upon to uphold our honikr, and that in itself is a sufficient reason why our boys respond." "Do you think we shall win?" I queried,•as prone to let him go. - "Win? There is no doubt about it," he an- swered. But I must go, good-night !"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>'THE MAN OF DESTINY."</head>
        <p>'THE MAN OF DESTINY."</p>
        <p>Napoleon, the Maker of Widows and Orphans.</p>
        <p>"LIT. AND PHIL." LECTURE.</p>
        <p>"A little while ago I stood by the grave of the old Napoleon—a magnificent tomb of gilt and gold fit almost for a deity dead—and gazed upon the sarcophagus of rare and nameless marbles, where rest at least the ashes of that restless man. . . . I thought of the widows and orphans he had made, of the rears that had been shed for his glory, and the only woman who ever loved him pushed from his heart by the cold hand of ambition. And I said I would rather have been a French peasant and worn wooden shoes. I would rather have lived in a hut with a vine growing over the door, and the grapes growing purple in the kisses of the amorous sun; I would rather have been that poor peasant, with my wife knitting by my side as the day died out of the sky, with my children upon my knees, and their arms about me; I would rather have been this man and gone down to the tongueless silence of the dreamless dust, than to have been that imperial personification of force and murder known as Napoleon the Great."</p>
        <p>With these words, quoted from the writings or the great American Free-thinker, Colonel Ingersoll, Mr. Hedley H. Taylor drew to a dose</p>
        <p>the excellent lecture which, under the title of "Stones of Waterloo," he delivered before the members of the Literary and Philosophical Society in the Cairo-street Hall on Monday evening.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>_ CENTRAL FIGURE AT WATERLOO.</head>
        <p>_ CENTRAL FIGURE AT WATERLOO.</p>
        <p>The central figure at Waterloo, said Mr. Taylor, in the course of his lecture, was Napo- leon Bonaparte. The destruction of the power of that central figure was the object of the col- lecting of the masses of European forces in Bel- gium in that memorable month of June, 1815, and, to destroy the power of one, surrounded, as he was, by brave men, meant the destruction of thousands. The casus belli between France and the other European Powers was the French Revolution. It started, according to Carlyle, by the execution of Louis the Sixteenth in January, 1793. In his history of the French Revolution the Sage of Chelsea said: "At home this killing ' of a king has divided all friends; abroad it has united all enemies. England declares war. Spain declares war. They all declare war." When the, wars of the Revolution ended, Chose of Napoleon began; and thus the European Powers commenced a war against France, and ended in a war against Napoleon. Napoleon, on the other hand, commenced a war against the other European Powers, but later concentrated his energies more especially against Britain. Britain seemed to be the obstacle that pre- vented him from becoming master of all Europe, and to overcome her was his aim, and had that aim been realised, "England," to quote his own words, "would have finally been no more than an appendix to my France." The invasion of England was Napoleon's great idea, and so cer-</p>
        <p>thin- was he of being able to realise his idea that he had, in anticipation, a medal struck to com- memorate the victory that never cane off. Hav- ing found that England could not be invaded without a very strong navy—this point being emphasised in his mind all the more by the result of the Battle of Trafalgar in 1805—Napo- leon' tried to subdue her by instituting what was known as the European blockade, thereby clos- ing the ports of Europe to British and Colonial wares.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>O'ER-REACHING ACT.</head>
        <p>THE O'ER-REACHING ACT.</p>
        <p>This was the beginning of the end. In order to make Russia obey the rules of the European blockade, Napoleon went on that terrible expe- dition to Moscow, from whence the lack of food and shelter forced him to retreat through the bitter winter weather, so that only ,a shattered remnant of his great army ever returned home. In 1814 Napoleon's ambition of founding a vast empire to dominate the destinies of the world reached its climax. With that masterly spirit which characterised his whole life, he had placed himself, his three brothers, his brother- in-law, and his stepson on thrones, and had treated the boundaries of countries like elastic —the extension of one meaning the diminution of another—the result being that in 1814 the map of Europe was in a disordered state. To achieve the object of his ambition blood had been shed with indifference, whilst the disor- ganisation of trade, caused by the European blockade, had reduced many people, especially in France, to the verge of starvation. After Wellington had driven Napoleon's brother, Joseph, from the throne of Spain, and the French had been defeated at Leipsic, Napoleon was compelled to sign the Treaty of Paris, in which it was set forth that he renounced for himself\and for his heirs the thrones of France and Italy, and that "faithful to his oath" there was no personal sacrifice, even of life itself,", that he was not ready to make in the interests of France. This treaty was signed on April 6th, and five days later Napoleon left Fontainebleau for Elba. The representatives of the European Powers then assembled at Vienna and there endeavoured to rearrange the disordered state of Europe. NAPOLEON WINS BACK HIS ARMY. . Such changes could not be made without causing dissatisfaction in some quarters, and Napoleon followed the course of events with interest. Being truly a military man, he felt he could win the army over to his side, and, being tired of inactivity, he determined to try his fortune once more—the fortune which, he often stated, never deserted him on great occa- sions. In March, 1815, he landed again in France. As soon as the news of his landing reached Paris, Marshal Ney went out with a body of troops to oppose his old master, promis- ing the king to bring him back in an Von cage. That promise was, not kept, for neither he nor the soldiers under him could resist that old affection for Napoleon which had in the past, united them as comrades in arms, and joining the forces they had been sent out to resist, they marched back to Paris, conquered by a force more powerful than that of arms. The turning of Ney was only typical of what happened all 'along the route on the march back to Paris, until practically the whole army had joined the ranks of Napoleon. On March 11th the news reached the Congress at Vienna; on the 12th the Congress drew up a declaration that, as a com- mon enemy, a disturber of the peace of the world, Napoleon had delivered himself over to public justice, and France, by receiving him back, had declared war against Europe. On March 25th Great Britain, Russia, Austria and Prussia promised to provide 150,000 men each in order to maintain the treaty of Paris—a promise that sealed the doom, of 'Napoleon's power, the crushing defeat at and flight from Waterloo, following some three months later.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>CRUSHING BLOW</head>
        <p>THE CRUSHING BLOW</p>
        <p>The lectuier then went on to describe in dp- tail the various phases of the historic battle, of which Scott. wrote:</p>
        <p>Agincourt may be forgot, And Cressy be an unknown spot, And Blenheim's name be new; But still in story and in song, For many an age remember'd long. Shall live the towers of Hougomont. And field of Waterlooo</p>
        <p>When it was quite clear th`at the issue of the day had gone against him, Napoleon, the lec- turer said, left the battlefield in the carriage which accompanied him to Moscow, and was used by him at Elba. About fifteen miles from the field he was overtaken by some Prussians, who ,captured tho carriage, though he himself escaped.- The carriage was presented to the Prince Regent, who afterwards 'sold it for &lt;22,500, and to-day it may be seen in Madame Taussaud's well-known exhibition in 'London. How Napo- leon reached Fronde, and how, realising that the people had, turned against him, and that there was no hope of his ever regaining his position in that country again, he delivered himselr up to the captain of the British frigate "Bellerophon,"</p>
        <p>romtinusd bi m 4 Mum,</p>
        <p>4. POULTRY PEN. $ 44 Hints and Interesting Facts and .14 Chat for Amateurs. .14 .14 3.4444•14+44.14.14.14.14+++++++.14.14.14.14 .14 DUTY OF ALLOTMENT HOLDERS</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>FARM AND GARDEN.</head>
        <p>FARM AND GARDEN.</p>
        <p>LOSSES CAUSED BY ACORNS.</p>
        <p>"After running the 400 sheep in front of cattle, barb wiring off oak• trees where possible and taking every possible precaution," writes A farmer, "I lost six calves (four months), due to a rough wind in the night, when acorns were green, blowing a quantity down, and they were eaten early in the morning before being noticed. We also lost one steer two years old, two beasts one and a half year old, and we have had a dairy cow, twelve young beasts, and seven calves ill, but have pulled them through. "I put pickers on all over the estate, and have now collected in the granary 16 tons of acorns for winter use, and as the prices for foodstuffs are ruling they will be valuable for pigs and sheep, and help against the losses sustained."</p>
        <p>NON-IMPRESSMENT OF BROOD MARES.</p>
        <p>The Board of Agriculture and Fisheries give notice to farmers and owners of horses that in order to encourage the breeding of horses, the War Office have issued instructions to their pur- chasing officers to avoid, as far as possible, the purchase of mares, and of-no account to pur- chase by impressment any mare whose owner signs a declaration that he will use her for breeding neat service season.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>ROTATION BENEFITS.</head>
        <p>ROTATION BENEFITS.</p>
        <p>The necessity of the rotation of crops is well recognised among modern farmers, and now it appears that in India nature is seen practising the same thing in the forests. The soil becom- ing exhausted after a long period of one kind of forest, seedlings of other trees gradually replace the old trees as they die out. On the Indian soil, the deodar tree has been observed taking the place of the blue pine, pine and oak slowly exchange places, and spruce and silver fir have been noted gradually extending into a forest of failing oaks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>POTASH AND DEEP PLOUGHING.</head>
        <p>POTASH AND DEEP PLOUGHING.</p>
        <p>There is a good deal of talk in advanced farm circles as to what we are going to do for a supply of potash now since the importation from Germany is cut off. So far as ordinary soils are concerned, that need can be readily sup- plied if the farmer will but resolutely face the problem of deep ploughing. All of the .lower soils contain a large supply of potash. All that is needed to bring it into Use and profitable con- tact with growing plants is to go down after it. ,Plough the land 12 inches to 16 inches deep, bring up that stiff, impenetrable subsoil and give it contact with the air, mix it with the top soil, and give it contact with the manure and other forms of humus. That will set the potash free, put it in shape so that the roots of plants can utilise it. We do not need to do this deep ploughing every year. Once in five or six years will do. But we shall know a good deal more about it, and the benefits that come from it in various ways, if we once set about it in a practical, earnest manner. Most farmers dread to start in this work. They dislike to incur the expense of a deep tilling plough, and they are sceptical as to the result. But they always will be in dread and always will be sceptical until they go ahead and put themselves where they can see and learn.</p>
        <p>One benefit is that you can get at your deeply- ploughed land several days earlier in the spring. Clayey soils lose time in the spring. Every farmer is afraid of ploughing them until they are well dried out for fear of their being lumpy all the summer long. But deep ploughing helps a great deal in this respect.—," Hoard's Dairy- man."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>AN OLD FLORAL FAVOURITE.</p>
        <p>Those who-love the magnificent Cottage and Darwin Tulips which make our gardens so brilliant in May'will give a special eye to Ver- benas, because the bed from which the tulips are taken in early June need only be forked over and raked down to form a suitable home for Verbenas. Here they can be planted about 18 inches apart all ways. Closer planting is tempting, because in June the plants are very small, and they have a somewhat lost look when planted wide apart. Nevertheless, when estab- lished they grow very vigorously, and throw out a large number of horizontal shoots, so that close planting must be avoided. With a little care in watering for a few days after planting the seedlings will soon be safe. After this stag they will grow rapidly. Long before midsum- mer the bed will be full of growth, and the plants will begin to bloom, and they will re- main in beauty until October.—" Agricultural Economist and Horticultural Review."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>FISH MANURE.</head>
        <p>FISH MANURE.</p>
        <p>This is commonly known in commerce as fish guano, though really the refuse of fish dried and ground up fine. It contains about 14 per cent. of phosphates, 7 to 8 per cent. of nitrogen, and 2to 3 per cent. of potash. It may be used at the rate of from 101 b. to 121 b. per square rod of ground, and is available for /either fruit or vegetables. Because of its strong odour it should be dug in deeply as soon as applied. It is well to apply it a few weeks previous to crop- ping, as some time has to elapse before it is fitted to become plant food.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>PRUNING GOOSEBERRIES.</head>
        <p>PRUNING GOOSEBERRIES.</p>
        <p>No greater error prevails in the culture of this popular and wholesome fruit than the practice of cutting back the individual shoots to within a bud or two of their base, with the result that only a thicket of weakly shoots and small fruits is forthcoming. The way to obtain large fruits and heavy crops is to thin out the shoots freely and shorten back the long and weak shoots so as to promote a balance of growth in the bushes. Another advantage of this mode of pruning is that when the fruit is being gathered this can be done without scratching the hands.—" Farm and Ilome"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>ABOUT YOUR DAUGHTER. No Longer a Child.</head>
        <p>ABOUT YOUR DAUGHTER. No Longer a Child.</p>
        <p>Perhaps you have already noticed that your daughter in her " teens " has developed a fitful temper, is restless and excitable, and often in need of gentle reproof. In that case, remember that the march of years is leading her on to womanhood, and at this time a great responsi- bility rests upon you, as parents. If your daughter is pale, complains of weak- ness•and depression, feels tired-out after a little exertion; if she tells you of headaches or back- aches, or pain in the side, do not disregard these warnings. Your daughter needs help, for she is most probably anEemic—that is, bloodless. Should you notice any of these disturbing signs, lose no time, but procure for her Dr. Williams' Pink Pills, or her unhealthy girlhood is bound to lead to unhealthy womanhood. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People en- rich the impoverished blood of girls and women, and „by doing so they repair waste and prevent disease. They give to sickly drooping girls, health, brightness and charm, with colour in the cheeks, sparkling eyes, a light step and high spirits. Let your daughter begin to-day; any dealer supplies these Pills (but never accept substitutes), or send 2s. 9d. for one box, or 13s. 9d. for six, to Dr. Williams' Medicine Co., 46, Holborn-viaduct, London. FREE.—Of interest t• all girls and women is the little book "Plain Talks to Women" offered free to lady readers who send postcard for a copy to Hints Department, 46, Holborn-viaduct, Lon- don.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>, • , – . bre – • . ' • Jf..? • ; elift..s9" "t 9 0 0, sr • a ' ' – t,' ,‘.A f -11 ' • ••• At,„ , , en, ,* 4. . .•</head>
        <p>, • , - . bre - • . ' • Jf..? • ; elift..s9" "t 9 0 0, sr • a ' ' - t,' ,‘.A f -11 ' • ••• At,„ , , en, ,* 4. . .•</p>
