      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>AND GARDENERS.</head>
        <p>AND GARDENERS.</p>
        <p>Occripiers of allotments and gardens should endeavour to keep fowls. Ducks are not likely to be in great demand for some time. They are, moreover, expensive to rear, and do not pay unless prices are good. Residents near open land such as commons should breed geese. Where there is plenty or rough grazing these birds are profitable, as they forage for the greater part of their food, and are naturally grazers. Geese and turkeys are unsuitable for allotments and gardens, and it is in relation to fowls that de- velopment should be encouraged.</p>
        <p>AREA OF LAND REQUIRED FOR POULTRY. The number of fowls. to be kept must be deter- mined by the area of the ground available. If the birds are suitable, are properly managed:. are ensured plenty of exercise by the provision of scratching material in which grain is fed, and i are used for egg production and not as breeding stock, as many as 400 may be kept on an acre of land for a period of twelve months. To state this in another way, twelve square yards of pasture land should be allowed for every fowl. Where it is ploughed or dug land, then eight square yards for each bird will be enough. For permanent occupation fifty fowls per acre may be regarded as sufficient. The occupier of an allotment, if a capable manager of poultry, may keep 100 fowls on a quarter of an acre for a year, at the end of which time the ground would have to be vacated and cropped. Larger holdings may be stocked on the same basis. The garden poultry-keeper who can give a space of, say, 16 yards by 10 yards•to the fowls, may keel) twenty birds on dug ground, or even more if the position is changed every six months. Half a dozen hens can be kept on a garden plot 20 feet by 6 feet, provided the soil is dug over frequently and en- tirely renewed by fresh earth once every three 'months. Cottagers can frequently obtain range for their birds on adjacent land, in which case the renewal of the soil is not required so often.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>HOW TO USE THE GROUND.</head>
        <p>HOW TO USE THE GROUND.</p>
        <p>The great danger of poultry-keeping on these intensive lines is from tainted soil, due to the accumulation of manure. Sanitary and hygienic conditions must, therefore, be maintained, other- wise disease and loss will result. Where a mod- erate number of poultry is distributed over a larger area, as on farms, this difficulty is not present. The safe plan is to run fowls over one- fourth of the land at a time, fully cultivating that not occupied by the birds. By such an arrangement much of the food for the fowls can be grown, serving the double purpose of keep- ing the soil sweet and clean and reducing the cost of feeding. The manure will largely in- crease the crops if the land is properly culti- vated. Under the conditions described an allot- ment should be divided into four parts, each to be used in succession. Upon that devoted ex- clusively to pOultry laying hens should be kept. As chickens are hatched they may be put out on to the other sections during the growing stages. It is important to use houses which can epily be removed from place to place.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>MOST PROFITABLE.</head>
        <p>MOST PROFITABLE.</p>
        <p>As a rule egg production would be found most profitable, for two reasons—(l) because the shortage is more likely to be felt in regard to eggs than to chickens, as the country is more dependent upon imported eggs than upon im- ported poultry, and (2) because the demand for table poultry is not likely to be so large as it would be under normal conditions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>PROFIT IN POULTRY.</head>
        <p>PROFIT IN POULTRY.</p>
        <p>There are two fundamental facts upon which the elementary training of every' poultry keeper should be based. One, ,says the "Agricultural Economist and Horticultural Review," is that hens are not all alike; which emphasises the need for discrimination; and the other is that as you do by a hen so she will 'do by you; which points to the importance of management. It fs little use remembering one of these facts and ignoring the other. You may obtain the best stock in the country—pullets descended from parents which for generations have neyer failed to average 200 eggs in a year, and except per- haps for a few weeks in spring and summer you will never detect the difference if you leave them to their own devices and neglect common-sense rules of managemeAt. Or, On the other hand, you will be merely wasting time, energy, and opportunities by lavishing good management upon stock of a naturally unproductive class.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>RUSSIAN EGGS.</head>
        <p>RUSSIAN EGGS.</p>
        <p>The Russian Government has again permitted a number of large consignments of eggs to leave Russia for Sweden. The Government has fur- ther decided to abolish all the restrictions that were laid on this trade at the beginning of the war, and the export trade in eggs will shortly be again in full swing. Last year 50,000,000 eggs were imported into Sweden and some 55,000,000 exported.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>"THE MAN OF DESTINY "—continued.</head>
        <p>"THE MAN OF DESTINY "—continued.</p>
        <p>was next told by the lecturer, who then pro- ceeded to describe the closing days of the fallen Emperor's career—" Trie Last Phase "—spent in captivity on the inhospitable island of St. Helena. As to this, the lecturer said that he thought most people woula be inclined to agree with Lord Rosebery, that although the 'only place for Napoleon was St. Helena, it was a pity that the ungracious responsibility of confining him *there should have devolved on Great Britain, and that that responsibility was not discharged with more consideration and less crudeness. Napoleon himself regretted that he had not been killed at Waterloo—a much better death for a soldier than to die in captivity on a lonely island.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>LITTLE JOKE.</head>
        <p>A LITTLE JOKE.</p>
        <p>At the close of' his lecture, which was illus- trated by beautiful lantern slides—many of them being taken from world-famous paintings of the happenings of a world-famous period— Mr. Taylor was accorded a hearty vote or thanks, on the proposition of Mr. Rymer Young, seconded by the Deputy-Mayor (Dr. Joseph), and supported by Miss Broadbent, who con- vulsed the audience by recounting a recent con- versation overheard between two working men: " Wot'll they do with these 'ere Kayser," asked the first. "Oh, Lguess they'll send him to St. Helens, like they did the other bloke," replied the second.</p>
        <p>Mr. Young, in theicourse of his brief speech, said that he could never succeed in putting away from his Mind the fact that Napoleon was one of the greatest demons that ever cursed the earth, and was to be ranked with the Neros of former times and the Kaisers of to-day. He was not in a position to shed one particle of admiration upon human vermin. The Chairman (the Rev. H. V. Pigot) added to the merriment by expressing the hope that when "the consummation so devoutly to be wished" came about and the Kaiser was in the hands of the Allies, that they would realise that St. Helens was too good for him. He (the speaker) had a great affection for St. Helens, and he would be sorry to see it put to such a base use. (Laughter.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD</p>
        <p>"Scenes."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>The little display of temper at last week's meeting of the Warrington Town Council set me mentally contrasting my varied experience of the doings of public bodies in different parts of the country in which I have lived and worked. When I was in Halifax I had regularly to attend the monthly performances of a then newly-formed School Board for the Stainland district. The two parties constituting that body were ferociously antagonistic and absolutely ir- reconcilable. In those days they met in a vil- lager's best room, and the stout gentleman who presided was accommodated in a rocking chair, which he was wont violently to oscillate when his ruffled feelings required soothing. We re- porters were always sure of a series of "scenes," and the Board obtained a wide and most unenvi- able notoriety. From that district I went into East Lancashire, and I found things at the opposite extreme. Both the Town Council and the School Board did all their business in com- mittee, and the proceedings at their monthly meetings consisted simply and solely in the con- firmation of the minutes. This was tn a sense as farcical as the other, and really more objectionable in every way. The Press resented it in the public interest, and eventually the con- ditions were modified. Hat Trick.</p>
        <p>Meanwhile the reporters took care to make the most of every incident that came their way. The old School Board in question was composed all but wholly of ministers—four clergymen of the Church of England, one Roman Catholic, and the remainder Nonconformist. One of the clergy was a young "sport." He was great at cricket and athletics generally, and about the period to which I allude had won increased ad- miration by dashing from his study, which over- looked the canal, and gallantly rescuing a child that had fallen into the water. At the meeting to which I now refer particularly, the Catholic priest was tipping his chair back when his hat dropped therefrom to the floor. The young par- son, who sat next to him, found the temptation too great, and, undignified as it was, made a football of the "tile." Another member "re- turned," and the lot became boys again, the priest taking it all in good part. No more was thought of it until the appearance of the papers, with a detailed account of the "serum" and all the rest of it. Readers may be sure that the story did not suffer in the telling, and of course the participants in the "game," then oblivious or careless of consequences and now dreadfully annoyed and scandalised, declared it had been grossly exaggerated. They hastened 'to pass a characteristic but futile resolution to the effect that thenceforth the reporters take note only of such matters and things as they were specifically told were for publication. As one of our number promptly pointed out, in the first place there were no such matters and things, all business of public interest being transacted in committee, and, further, if there happened at any time to be an item out of the ordinary when the representatives of the Press were present, they would exercise their own discretion, and not submit to dictation or "gag." A Cynical Carter.</p>
        <p>Let me go back for atmoment to the case of the Stainland School Board. Once they fixed a special meeting for the eve of publication, and, as the village was a few miles from our office and had then no railway, I hired a trap from the livery stables. The beast they put in the shafts for me turned out a fearful "roarer." I made the painful discovery when we came to mount the first hill on the outward journey, and a cart driver who was riding on. in front awak- ened to the fact simultaneously and favoured me with his view of the situation. Lying in the bottom of his vehicle was a sheep tethered ty a small cord, and, looking round suddenly, he shouted derisively, "Well, I'm blowed if I didn't think it 'er my sheep 'at wor hanging itsen ! Wheerivvor have yo picked that up, mate?" And passers-by laughed at the clumsy joker. Macnamara's Band.</p>
        <p>I see that this humorous item has lately figured in a Grappenhall Independent Methodist Church entertainment programme. It is really much older than any of those who took an active part on that occasion. I first heard it in the early '7o's, and I well remember how John Bull, as conductor, made sympathetic reference to France and her lost provinces. I hardly ex- pected to live to see Alsace-Lorraine restored to our gallant Ally; now it is reasonable to hope that I may, for "the day" cannot now be very far distant.</p>
        <p>Dental Objection. The send-off at Lymm at the end of the local soldiers' Christmas furlough reminded me of a somewhat similar one which I lately witnessed. A party of young men associated with the St. John Ambulance organisation who were under- stood to have been accepted for service with the military had gathered on the platform of a way- side station in readiness for the train in which I was travelling, and their friends were present in force to wish them God-speed. They told me they had first to report themselves at York for the final medical examination. Returning that way the same night, I fell into the company of seven of them on their way back home, I had some difficulty in identifying them as the same, so crestfallen and woebegone were they. Their keen disappointnient was natural, for all seven had been rejected for dental deficiency; bad teeth was all that was amiss with 'them, but the inexorable weeding-out process left them n‘ alternative but to take the next train home- ward, while their more fortunate fellows were sent to London, en route for the Crystal Palace.</p>
        <p>Hades. While in those parts I took the opportunity to look up a friend who happens at present to be in a military hospital thereabout, and as we chatted a local vicar popped in. My friend told me this parson had two sons who wished to join the Army, and that he had refused consent and positively forbidden them to mention it again. I was interested, and paid special attention to what followed. "Well, boys," exclaimed his reverence cheerfully, "still going on all right, eh?" "Yes, sir, thank you," was the hearty re- sponse from all parts of the ward. "And you're all anxious to get away to the front again as soon as possible," he pursued, and was plainly a bit surprised when this was met by a vigorous and emphatic "No." "Dear me," said he, "you're not turning cowards, are you?" One of the soldiers limped forward as spokesman, and said with dignity, "I don't think anyone has ha 4 reason to doubt our bravery; but, unlike your sons, if I may say so, we have been to hell once,- and we are not keen on going again—all the same we shall do our duty when we are certified as fit. Go thou and do likewise." I dii not envy that parson at the moment.</p>
        <p>Tommy and Mary. It served him right; still, one seldom catches Mr. Atkins in any such mood as that—on the contrary, he is in most circumstances a wonder- fully cheery, happy-go-lucky fellow,' always ready with a joke and a laugh that is infectious and irresistible. The other day I was watch- ing some recruits in. course of being licked into shape, 41 they were bubbling over with good humour all the while. Presently a nursemaid in charge of a little chap of not more than four joined our group. The youngster wore a khaki cap and coat of military cut, and prattled of puttees that were to be ready or him on the morrow. It turned out that he was well known on the parade ground, and on intimate terms with at least one squad of the recruits. A member of the latter put out his hand, crying "Now, then, kiddie; quick march," and the de- lighted little chap sprang forward and took it just is they moved away. lie was on the trot as he passed, and, looking back at nursie, he ex- claimed "Come on, Mary." Needless to say, this was quickly taken up, and every man of the company, casting an eye over his shoulder in our direction, repeated laughingly, " Yes, come on, Mary." When, a few moments later, the flnshed and breathless boy rejoined the maid, one said to him, "You mean to be a soldier. don't you?". and lie 'replied proudly, " That is a fad."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 16, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 16, 1915.</p>
        <p>"LIFE IN OUR BELGIAN HOME."</p>
        <p>Reflections by a Refugee at Hall Nook.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>ADVERSITY THAT HAS CEMENTED FRIENDSHIP.</head>
        <p>ADVERSITY THAT HAS CEMENTED FRIENDSHIP.</p>
        <p>SPECIAL TO "THE EXA</p>
        <p>I have been asked to write something about life in one of the homes provided for those of my countrymen who have been driven out of Belgium. I might reasonably suggest that England, with her Allies, has something to thank Belgium for in challenging with sword the right of passage in the attack upon France. The quarrel was not ours; we have been drawn into it much against our will. But I do not want to emphasise what some call "a debt of honour." I recognise that even if this debt—if debt it be—did not exist, your country, which has ever been the refuge for all that are oppressed, would have provided succour for the afflicted. Belgian people, just as in our small way we, forty years ago, succoured both French and German wounded in the Franco-German war. I would therefore think of your hospi- tality as springing from the kindness of your hearts, and not as the acknowledgment of an obligation.</p>
        <p>The position is peculiar for both hosts and visitors. From your standpoint we are foreign- ers from a foreign land; personally, we are an unknown quantity; we have no personal or direct claim upon those who have so freely provided for us in our need. On the other hand, before these awful happenings we were dependent upon no one; we were taking our share as self-respecting, self-supporting citizens in our own homeland. When the war overwhelmed our country and brought disrister into our national and home life, we were helpless to avert personal ruin and incapable of providing against the catastrophe. I and my compatriots homed here mostly come from Malines, one of the many Belgian towns which are now, in a large measure, noth- ing but heaps of stones and ashes. We arrived in England at the end of September, and after spending some time at what might be consid- ered clearing or sorting houses, first near Lon- don and then at Liverpool, a resting-place was found for us in Hall Nook, Penketh, and it has proved a real haven of rest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>SOFTENING THE FEELING OF DEPEND-</head>
        <p>SOFTENING THE FEELING OF DEPEND-</p>
        <p>EN CE.</p>
        <p>The local committee Under whose care we live is composed of ladies and gentlemen from the neighbourhood, augmented by representa- tives from the Catholic Churches of Warring- ton. These friends have shown us every kind- ness, and this kindness has been manifested with all possible consideration for our sense of self-respect; we have been treated as honoured visitors. This has done much to soften the feeling of dependence upon those whom we had no personal claim; and we have tried to accept the hospitality in the spirit in which it has been offered, and to be worthy of it. There are twenty-three Belgians sheltered under this roof. The work of the house was, from the beginning, arranged on a systematic plan. Each one is given the task for which he or she is best fitted. To the women naturally fall the housework, the washing, cooking, cleaning, ex. The men, in addition to assisting in the rougher housework, help in the outdoor work of the estate. So far, this has consisted mostly in re-arranging the shrubberies and thinning out the trees, and this work is full of new interest to us. Your tools, appliances, and ways of doing things differ a good deal from \those in Belgium. We will no doubt learn a good deal, and possibly some of our agricultur- ists will be Ole to teach your people some- thing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>PIG-KEEPING.</head>
        <p>PIG-KEEPING.</p>
        <p>Anticipating that our stay may be long, the committee have thought we should grow our own bacon, and have started us in pig-rearing. One of our number, although totally unatcust tomed to the task, has taken over, with zest and apparent success, the care of these pigs, and we are looking with pleasurable anticipation to a good supply of pork and English sausages in the spring. The one who looks ,after the porkers takes great pride in their growth, and frequently puts a tape round them to measure their increasing girth. The scraps from the table go a long way to provide the food, and they need to go a long way, for the capacity of these pigs seems to me to have no limit. They appear daily to gorge themselves and to be no worse, but rather the better for it. They are turned out for a constitutional every day, and although I do not understand their language, the sounds produced seem to indicate satisfac- tion. Our caretaker has christened his charges, respectively, Jean, Jacque and Boy, although which is which no one but he, and possibly the pigs themselves, know, for all are brethren, and alike in colour, shape and size.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>LEARNING AND TEACHING.</head>
        <p>LEARNING AND TEACHING.</p>
        <p>Another of our colony has undertaken the tuition of two French classes—one elementary, and one advanced. There has been an attend- ance of about thirty students from the neigh- bourhood.</p>
        <p>On the other hand, our people are picking up your language, and this is all to the good. We find the words in your language that have a Saxon origin are closely akin to our Flemish. No doubt when order takes the place of the present terrible chaos, the links which connect the two people will be much closer than in the past, and the English tongue will count for far more in Belgium than before.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>RECEPTION DAYS.</head>
        <p>RECEPTION DAYS.</p>
        <p>A feature of interest is our weekly Reception Day—Saturday afternoon—when tea is provided for visitors at a charge of sixpence. These are to be continued as long as our friends, and this means everybody in the district, care to visit the home. Our hostess has provided special</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>RUNCORN GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The War and the Butchers' Bill.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Board of Guardians was held at Dutton Work- house on Tuesday, Mr. C. E. Linaker, jun., pre- siding. AN APPOINTMENT SANCTIONLD.</p>
        <p>The Local Government Board wrote sanction- ing the appointment of Miss H. Rase as assist- ant nurse at the Workhouse, at a salary of ..t25 per -annum.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>WAR AND MEAT PRICES.</head>
        <p>WAR AND MEAT PRICES.</p>
        <p>It was reported that at a _meeting of the House Committee the meat contractor, Mr. Hancock, asked the Guardians to reconsider the existing prices owing to the increases on account of the war. The matter was left in the hands of Messrs. Morris, Spruce and Baxter. Mr. Woodward said he hoped the sub-commit- tee would deal sympathetically with Mr. Han- cock, who had shown the Guardians every con- sideration.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>EVERYTHING SATISFACTORY.</head>
        <p>EVERYTHING SATISFACTORY.</p>
        <p>In compliance with anew Local Government Order, the Master presented his half-yearly report, which stated that the staff was incom- plete owing to the assistant master and cook being away. The vacancies were, however, about to be filled. The Master had no com- plaints to make about the staff and everything was satisfactorily conducted. The Chairman: The administration is highly satisfactory, for the Master does his utmost to satisfy us and the people under his charge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>ANOTHER TESTIMONIAL!</head>
        <p>ANOTHER TESTIMONIAL!</p>
        <p>The Chaplain reported that the services were well, attended, and the congregations uniform. Excepting when it had been wet, the children had attended every Sunday. The inmates joined willingly in the services. The children</p>
        <p>seemed happy, contented, and well cared for. Mr. Hewitt: Another testimony! The reports of the Master and Chaplain were adopted.</p>
        <p>'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>INER " BY A BELGIAN.</head>
        <p>INER " BY A BELGIAN.</p>
        <p>dresses in the Belgian colours for our little girls, who act as waitresses. After tea a little musical entertainment is given. Our children provide the programme with the help of one of the mothers, assisted by Madame Whyte, the matron. The committee have lindly arranged that the profits from these teas are to go to provide a sum for our personal necessities, and the balance from this, or any like source, is banked in our name to help us to gather to- gether something to furnish the new homes we hope to build when the time comes to return to Belgium.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>RETURN TO BELGIUM.</head>
        <p>RETURN TO BELGIUM.</p>
        <p>Speaking of returning, although the date may be far off, we are sustaining our hopes by mak- ing preparations. Some of our men are bpsy making boxes, one for each family, in which to take our belongings back. These we mostly brought over in bundles—bundles of all shapes and sizes. We seem to have been adding to our stock since we came to England, and it appears we will return in a better condition than when we arrived; anyway, our committee have en- couraged us to make preparations by provid;ti,g wood and tools to make the wherewithal to ( on- tain our little all.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>IN PRAISE OF CLOGS</head>
        <p>IN PRAISE OF CLOGS</p>
        <p>I would like to say a word of appreciation or your English clogs. We have been shod with these—men, women and children—and always wear them out of doors in wet weather; and in England, if we are to judge by the last two months, 'you have more than your share of this. Our women find clogs specially useful in the wash-house, and, wearing these, the chil- dren's feet are always dry. These clogs, have the advantage shared by our sabots of keeping the feet out of the wet, but are not so clumsy as our wooden shoes. They are, of course, more rigid than ordinary boots, and the wearer has to accommodate himself to them in his walk, but he soon gets accustomed to this.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>FINDING FRIENDS AND RELATIVES.</head>
        <p>FINDING FRIENDS AND RELATIVES.</p>
        <p>A pleasant feature which has brought so much comfort during the last week or two is the discovery of the whereabouts and well-being of many of our friends and relatives. In the terrible confusion which occurred in Belgium, families and friends got widely scattered. Some came to England, others went to Holland and France, others remained in their own country. Of those who came here the Refugee Commit- tees in London and elsewhere had their hands full in "placing" our people and making room for fresh arrivals, and no attention could be paid to the grouping of friends. In course of time a more or less complete register was pre- pared, not only in London, but in the different centres; also in Holland and France. Through the help of these we have been able to trace the location of many of our friends. We have found that the father of a little boy with us—a soldier in the Belgian Army—is interned in Holland, and is safe and well; and we have been able to correspond with him and send him knitted comforts; while this boy's mother, whom we were very anxious about, as she re- mained in Malines with an invalid relative, is, we have learnt, safe and well in that town. We have also been able to correspond with several of our friends, whom we have located through the English register. Some of these live quite near and visits have been exchanged. Some of. our relatives were in a lonely country district, and in consequence were very depressed. These, through the kind consideration of the commit- tees in charge, have been transferred to one of the Warrington homes to be near us. I have tried to write the foregoing in a spirit of cheerfulness. If, however, we are not arivays happy in our temporary homes, it is not the result of any want of consideration on the part of our good hosts. Is it any wonder that some of our people have found it difficult to adjust themselves to the peculiar conditions in which they are placed? And I doubt not that the difficulty of adjustment has not been altogether on one side.</p>
        <p>CONSIDER!</p>
        <p>On behalf of the Belgian visitors who have sought refuge here, I put forward a plea for consideration. At times our spirits droop, and the strain of separation from friends, home, country, and all that these suggest, is as a heavy cloud which often darkens our outlook, lowers our vitality, and frays our spirits. Some of our Belgian people may have disappointed their hosts, but you English people must be for- bearing for the conditions are not normal. I might go further and say that as a result of what they have passed through, many Belgians who have come to England are not themselves normal. The world to them seems to have sud- denly been turned wrong way up. They feel that the wrong is not of their doing, and ask them- selves—Why should they and their country be the ones to suffer so terribly? Whatever the future may have in store for our two countries; English people may rest assured that the Belgians, who have 'partaken of your kindness, will never forget what has been done for us. We have broken bread with you, we have made friends with you, we have shared your Christmas cheer, and taken part in your Christmas joys. The ties which in the past have linked our little country with yours will, by the strange visit of ours, be greatly strengthened., While we perhaps have suffered most by this evil war, we do not forget you have suffered also. It was because of your plighted word to us, which 'another country dishonoured, you bared your sword, and to-day you are making the costliest sacrifices any people can offer to re-establish the integrity and the independence of our beloved country.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>THANKS FOR GENEROUS GIFTS.</head>
        <p>THANKS FOR GENEROUS GIFTS.</p>
        <p>It was decided, on the recommendation of the Children's Home Committee to forward a hearty vote of thanks to Lady Banner and Mrs. P. E. Secker for their generous gifts to the children.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>SAFEGUARDING ST. PAUL'S CATHEDRAL.</head>
        <p>SAFEGUARDING ST. PAUL'S CATHEDRAL.</p>
        <p>Apart from the danger to the principal Lon- don buildings, which might result from a pos- sible Zeppelin raid, there is, also the ever present fire risk to be considered, and in build- ings of the height and magnitude of St. Paul's Cathedral the problem of adequately safe- guarding all parts against outbreaks of fire is not a simple one. As a matter of fact, the fire- extinguishing arrangements at St. Paul's were put on a sound footing about two years ago, the contractors being Messrs. hierryweather and Sons, of Greenwich. So elaborate were the pre- cautions taken that by means of a system of fire-mains and hydrants, accompanied by special</p>
        <p>apparatus, it as found possible to -thiow a jet of wdter on any part of the vast building from the ground to the summit of the dome. The large rain water tanks in the stone gallery have recently been removed, and for an alter- native supply for the hydrants on the roof level a Merryweather electrically-driven "Hatfield" pump has just been installed with very satis- factory. results. It takes its supply from the Water Board's main and is arranged to start automatically as soon as a hydrant is opened.</p>
        <p>How To Reduce Your Meat Bill! Make delicious and nutritious puddings with apples, figs, dates, golden syrup, jam, &amp;e, and Shredded ATORA Beef Suet. Always ready for use and saves all the trouble of chopping. Auk your grocer for it; refuse substitutes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>TIE TERRITORIALS.</head>
        <p>TIE TERRITORIALS.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTONIANS FEASTED AND FETED IN THE SOUTH.</p>
        <p>Writing to a friend in Warrington, one of the lads at present training with the 4th South Lancashires in the South of England, says that now that the Christmas and New Year festi- vities are over he and his comrades are settling down to work harder than ever.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>CROSFIELD'S CONTINGENT DINE TOGETHER. Jobs Back When the War Is Over.</head>
        <p>CROSFIELD'S CONTINGENT DINE TOGETHER. Jobs Back When the War Is Over.</p>
        <p>Recently such of the men as formerly worked at Messrs. Crosfield's Soap Works were enter- tained to dinner, the bill being footed, to the extent of £23, by their old workmates in War- rington. The dinner party took place in the finest assembly room in the town, and the com, pany numbered about 170, Lieutenant Dickson presiding and being supported by Major G. R. Crosfield, Captain Layton, Captain McClure, Lieut. Jones, and Second-Lieut. Preedy. All these officers are connected with Messrs. Cros- field and Sons. The guests of the evening were Lieut.-Colonel Brereton Fairclough (command- ing the Battalion) and Captain and Adjutant Izod. _ _</p>
        <p>Everything passed off swimmingly„ and did great credit to the small committee which had all the arrangements in hand, and which con- sisted of Colour-Sergeant Mannion (hon. sec.), Colour-Sergeant Tynan, and Corporal Wright. The men were regaled with excellent fare, which included roast beef and Yorkshire pud- ding, boiled leg of mutton and caper sauce, potatoes, sprouts, cabbage, huge plum puddings, mince pies, beer and, smokes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>OLD WORKMATES AS HOSTS.</head>
        <p>OLD WORKMATES AS HOSTS.</p>
        <p>After the toast of "The King,"</p>
        <p>Major Crosfield proposed the health of "Our Comrades at Bank Quay, Warrington," and announced that he proposed to send the follow- ing telegram to Mr. Taylor, the foreman :-- " Crosfield Territorials of 4th South Lancs. send hearty greetings to comrades at the works and sincere thanks for most excellent New Year's dinner." (Applause.) His brother, John, continued the Major, in- formed him that the works were very busy, and so the men need not be afraid that when the war was over there would .be no work for them to return to ;* for the -works would be there, and everybody connected with them would be willing and anxious to welcome the men back. (Cheers.) He could assure them that not one of them need have the least anxiety as to his civilian life, and they could therefore give their whole attention to the great task they had in hand. (Renewed cheers.) Crosfield's had supplied ten officers and over six hundred non-commissioned officers and men, which meant that one man in every- three eligible men employed by the firm had volunteered his services, and he •doubted whether there was a business concern in the whole country which could show a better record. (More cheers.) •</p>
        <p>A capital musical programme was subse- quently rendered by Colonel Fairclough (who played the regimental march), Corporal Ingram (accompanist), Corporal Wright (vocalist), Col.- Sergeant Tynan (whose rendering of "The Rosary" evoked enthusiastic applause), and Private Clemo (humorous songs).</p>
        <p>A flashlight photograph of the diners was aken.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>BIG TEA PARTY.</head>
        <p>A BIG TEA PARTY.</p>
        <p>Many of Crosfield's men, as well as other War- ringtonians, were among a numerous /company entertained (a, dkty or so before the dinner Party) by the congregation of the Emmanuel Church, whose lecture hall is thrown open for the use of the "boys in khaki," quartered in the town, as a reading and writing room. The guests were regaled with tea and all the good things usually associated in the mind with a Christmas-cum-New-Year party, and before they separated Lieutenant Armstrong voiced thanks, on their behalf, to the kindly hosts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>CIGARETTES AND SONG.</head>
        <p>CIGARETTES AND SONG.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday of last week the men of the South Lancashire and other regiments were in- vited to a smoking concert in the biggest hall in the town, and spent a right merry evening, among the contributors being some of the best- known amateur artistes in the fashionable town in which the " Tommies" are billeted, as well as several of the Lancashire fighting men them- selves, among the latter Lieutenant Richard- son, Lance-Corporal Knowles, and Privates Walton and Smith. Besides the music, the guests were regaled with coffee, cakes, cigar- ettes and tobacco. Arrangements are being made by a strong committee of ladies and gentlemen to hold a series of similar concerts between now and the time fixed for the depar- turt of the men.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>IN THE NORTH.</head>
        <p>IN THE NORTH.</p>
        <p>Boxing Tourneys to Amuse "Tommy."</p>
        <p>The Warrington lads, and their comrades of other regiments, who are billeted in the North of England have discovered an additional joy during their sojourn by the sea. Boxing tour- naments are their newest recreation, and arrangements have been made to hold them weekly from now on to the end of their stay, one of the largest buildings in the town having been placed at the disposal of the organisers wherein to hold the fistic competitions. The first tournament, held on Friday of last week, was a great success, and among those who at- tended it was no less a personage than the Vrigadier-General himself. The various exhibi- tions of the art of self-defence given proved just to "Tommy's" liking, and the many matches made, for settleinent on future Friday even- ings, afford unquestionable proof of the popu- larity of the innovation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>"ARE WE ALL HERE':"</head>
        <p>"ARE WE ALL HERE':"</p>
        <p>In between the bouts, Colonel Cooney, the officer commanding the Bth (Irish) Battalion of the King's (Liverpool) Regiment, made an appeal for more recruits. He referred to the fact that many present had not yet donned the King's uniform and said that every man who was in uniform had the right to ask the ques- tion: "Are we all here?" with the addition: "If not, why not?" If he studied his own interests he would not be there. There was no officer in Blackpool at the present time who was losing more by being there than he was. But it was his duty to be there to help the country. (Hear, hear.) The mere fact of fatally 'ties and business considerations were not sufficient to keep them out of the Army to-day, when the country was engaged in a war the like of which , was never known before. Everyone was wanted, and if they did not join his battalion, let them join something. (Loud applause.) TOWNSPEOPLE ENTERTAIN THE MEN. Various sections of the people of the town vie with each other in entertaining the " Tommies." Whist drives galore have been, or are to be, held and there is not a section in the place that has not a concert arranged for it every Saturday evening, whilst many of them also receive invi- tations to sacred concerts on Sundays. Severar of the Churches arrange special services for the men on Sundays, whilst one or two have insti- tuted what they call Social Hours, open invita- tions to attend which are extended to the men of all regiments. These social hours are given over to sacred music—some of the best-known local amateurs being amongst the singers and performers—and short addresses.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>OLYMPIA CIRCUS, LIVERPOOL.</head>
        <p>OLYMPIA CIRCUS, LIVERPOOL.</p>
        <p>Next week, commencing January 18th, is positively the last week of the enormously suc- cessful circus, which has been witnessed by 150,000 people. For the last week the prices have been specially reduced, with half-price for children to all performances, Saturday . night excepted. Monday, January 25th, "The Whirl of the Town," from the Palate TheatrA, Man- chester, has been specially engaged at fabuloue, expense for two weeks. The cast is a magnifi- cent one, including Wilkie Bard, Daisy Wood, Charles Hart, Nellie Stratton, Thelma Raye, Walter Williams, &amp;c. All wishing to see or re-visit the wonderful circus should book early so as to secure seats some time during the last week, during which— as last year—much money will inevitably be turned away.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>IN THE COURTS.</head>
        <p>IN THE COURTS.</p>
        <p>FALSE PRETENCES AND BURGLARY</p>
        <p>Alleged Against A Salford Girl.</p>
        <p>Before Councillor H. Roberts (in the chair) and Mr. C. Jackson Holmes, at the Borough Police Court on Tuesday morning, Annie May Vickers, a young woman giving an address in Williams-street, Salford, was charged with obtaining by means of false pretences board and lodging to the value of 12s. from Lena Bostock, of 4, Poachers-lane, between Christ- mas Day and January 3rd. She was further charged with stealing 3s. 3d. in cash and a num- ber of articles belonging to Mrs. Bostock, and also with burglarously entering the same woman's house, on January sth, and stealink therefrom 2s. 9d. in cash and articles to , the total value of £3 15s.</p>
        <p>Prosecutrix the Wife of a Soldier.</p>
        <p>Superintendent Williamson said that it would be necessary for the police to make further inquiries concerning the prisoner, and, in order that they might have time to do so, he applied for a remand for seven days. Et aPpeaTed that on Christmas Day the prisoner turned up at Mrs. Bostock's house in Poachers-lbne, Latchford, and asked for lodgings. Mrs. Bos- tock was the wife of a soldier, who happened to be spending a short Christmas leave at home after having come back from the Front. Prisoner said she had come from Manchester on holiday, and added that stic worked in a mill, and that she had come to Warrington with three other gills, who had fixed. up lodg- ings at' a house in Knutsford-road—a house which, by the way, Mrs. Bostock knew very well. On the strength of what she said, Mrs. Bostock agreed to board and lodge the prisoner for nine days. On January 3rd the girl dis- appeared and Mrs. Bostock missed 3s. 3d. i cash.</p>
        <p>How the House Was Burgled.</p>
        <p>On the night of January sth—after the hu band had gone away—somebody broke into th house, and, as Mrs. Bostock discovered the fol- lowing morning, stole quite a lot of things, including 2s. 9d. in cash. Entry had been ob- tained through the back door, which had been opened from the outside, in a manner explained to prisoner whilst she was staying in the house. The prisoner was apprehended at Latchford on Monday, and she at once admitted all the offences. Some of the stolen property was found in her possession, and the remainder, she told the police, she had either pawned or had given away. Two of the missing articles had been found pawned in the Old Trafford district of Manchester. Prisoner was remanded for a week.</p>
        <p>in .s..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>ON SICK FURLOUGH.</head>
        <p>ON SICK FURLOUGH.</p>
        <p>low a Soldier Mis-spent Part of His</p>
        <p>Leave.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court on Tuesday, before Councillor Roberts (in the chair) and Mr. C. Jackson Holmes, Thomas Mitchell, a private in the Loyal North Lancashire Regi- ment, was charged with having been drunk and disorderly and with having aksaulted P.C. John McDonald whilst in the execution of his duty, on January 11th. P.C. McDonald deposed that at 12.45 a.m. on Monday he received information as to a dis- turbance at Friars' Green., He went there and saw prisoner, who was accompanied by another soldier and several women, causing a disturb- ance. They were •quarrelling and shouting among themselves. Witness spoke to them, whereupon the prisoner and the other man made use of very bad language. After a while the prisoner ran at witness with the idea of striking him. He was then taken into custody.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>Kicked the Constable.</head>
        <p>Kicked the Constable.</p>
        <p>On the way to the Police Station he was very violent and kicked' witness and butted him in the stomach, besides striking him across the face s with his fist. Two bystanders and P.C. Wise got the man to the lock-up. Prisoner denied striking the officer. P.C. Wise, who did not see anything of the assault, spoke to helping to get prisoner to the Police Station.</p>
        <p>Prisoner regretted that he got drunk and ex- plained that it was due to the fact that he had had no drink for a long while, so that it only took "a little drop" to• get hold of him. He had been invalided home from the Front.</p>
        <p>The Chairman (Councillor H. Roberts) : Were you wounded? Prisoner: No; it was bronchitis—chronic bronchitis.</p>
        <p>Have you got all right now?—Nearly. I am on 18 days' sick furlough. The 18 days are up on January 28th.</p>
        <p>Thirty-six Police Court Appearances.</p>
        <p>Superintendent Williamson said prisoner was rather well-known at that Court; in fact, he had been up 36 times in all, for such offences as drunkenness, thieving and assaults on the police. He had told the police that he went out to the Front on August 14th and came back on 'September 22nd. He had been in Chelsea Hos- pital and had a leave-pass until January 28th. He (the Superintendent) was very much afraid it would not do the man much good to be about Warrington. • The Chairman (to prisoner) : One does not like</p>
        <p>the idea of sending you—a soldier—to prison under circumstances like this. Can you find anybody who will become security for your good behaviour? _ _</p>
        <p>Prisoner said his sister would, and she having consented, the Bench accepted her as surety, and then formally bound Mitchell over in his own recognisances, admonishing him not to mis- conduct himself again. Prisoner:. No, sir; I won't. I'll be teetotal for the rest of the time I am on furlough. The Chairman: Remember you are a soldier and you have the reputation of Warrington to keep good, and, whilst you are doing your duty abroad, make up your mind that when you come home you will keep your character clean. Prisoner saluted and left the dock.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>BOROUGH POLICE COURT</head>
        <p>BOROUGH POLICE COURT</p>
        <p>SATURDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Alderman' Tinnion (in the chair), and Messrs. W. Furness, J. Upson and T. Pember- ton.</p>
        <p>A Female " Shop-lifter " Caught.—Lilian Wakefield, 8, Laira-street, was charged on re- mand with stealing articles of children's cloth- ing from two stalls in the Market.—Supt. Wil- liamson said that at one stall the attendant be- came suspicious of the woman, and found a child's pinafore under her skirt, and a kilted skirt, pinafore and woollen jeisey in her bag. Prisoner struggled and got away in the crowd.— Detective Skelton said that he made inquiries and arrested the woman. The woman's husband said she as a good wife and did not drink. She had a very good home, and he could not account for what she had done.—ln fining her 215., in- .cluding costs, the Chairman warned the woman —who had stated that her child had pulled the articles off the stall and that she made the mis- take of putting them in her bag—to be careful what she did when in the Market. It was only because she had looked after her home that they were dealing leniently with her.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>WEDNESDAY.</head>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Messrs. J. W. Hayward (in the chair),</p>
        <p>C. Jackson Holmes anCl W. T. Williams.</p>
        <p>Working An Unfit Horse.—Asked to plead to a summons for working a horse whilst in an unfit state, William Rutter, a lad, of 21, Ches- ter-street, said: "I am very sorry. The horse fell down and hurt its back." This was inter- preted as being a plea of guilt.—P.C. Rolls deposed to being at Bridge Foot at 11.30 on Wednesday, December 30th, and to seeing the defendant in charge of a bay colt, attached to a coal cart. He noticed that the animal was uneasy, and had the cart stopped, whilst he made an examination. He found a sore on the off-side shoulder and another underneath the saddle, the latter appearing to be old and very much inflamed. Defendant said that the horse was all right when he took it out. The animal was quite unfit for work.—lnspector Dee, of the R.S.P.C.A., said that the owner was away with the Army, and defendant did not draw the manager's attention to the condition of the horse.—The Chairman (to defendant) : Why didn't you tell the manager?—Defendant The horse fell down and " catched" itself.—Admon- ishing the lad to be kind in future to horses , and all other dumb animals, as they could not speak for themselves, the Chairman announced that a fine of 10s., with 6s. costs, would be imposed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>%%5iMW•%%bE%%%%%M%5fi%%%%%%%%T%!.Eni%F1%%%L5YM</head>
        <p>%%5iMW•%%bE%%%%%M%5fi%%%%%%%%T%!.Eni%F1%%%L5YM</p>
        <p>1 WARRINGTON MAN Kept Well 8 Years.</p>
        <p>On September 11th, 1905, Mr. T. Rigby, of 121, Church-street, near the Training College for Girls, Warrington, said: "Through hav- ing to work out in all weathers I became subject to kidney trouble. For several years I was a sufferer from acute pains in my back, which were sometimes so bad that I could scarcely get about. I was almost afraid to stoop, for it was a diffi- cult matter to straighten myself</p>
        <p>suits. It was different, however, when I started with Doan's Back- ache Kidney Pills; they did me good from the first, and brought</p>
        <p>me back to health. I am thankful for the benefit I derived from Doan's Pills, and shall do all I can to make them wider known. (Signed) "P. Rion." On November 21st, 1913—OVER. EIGHT YEARS LATER—Mr. Rigby said: "I "Say still keep very well indeed. I have 'Thank often recommended Doan's Pills. -D°6"'s" They have dont me lasting good."</p>
