      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>t.O-•</head>
        <p>t.O-•</p>
        <p>Price 20, six loxes 1319, of all dealers or Foster-McClellan Co., 8, Wells Street, Oxford Street, London, W. 51 21</p>
        <p>when reverses and sufferings came. They had had their special day of prayer and thanksgiv- ing; and they did well to thank God for the mercies He had vouchsafed to them, if only in/ saving them -from so many of the horrors their Allies had suffered. The Germa`ns were having their day of humiliation and prayer that day, and they, as English people, felt sure that their enemies had much cause to humble them- selves, for they, and they alone, were the aggressors in this great war. Let them hope that the German humiliation will be sincere. WILL SPIRITUAL AWAKENING RESULT? The "call to arms," said the preacher, in conclusion, had been responded to splendidly in this country, and everyone had showed a readi- ness to help in any way they could. Had they, however, the courage to deal with themselves as to their sins, failings, and lack of devotion to God? The war had made a deep impression upon their men at the Front. It had also made a deep impression upon their Allies. They must now ask themselves what impression it had made upon them at home? Were the masses of the people really impressed from the spiritual point of view? It, was a well-known fact that the Bible was not read to-day in Eng- land half as much as it was read in days gone by. Happily, however, a view of the more serious side of life had deeply impressed a large number of the people, and a real spiritual awakening might result from that; but whether it was so in regard to the masses of tilt people he was doubtful. Judgment must begin with the people of God, and the example must be set by those who came to church. A revival of spi-itual life must take place throughout the country—a revival the like of which this gener- ation had not seen.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>WHAT PRAYER WEEK SHOWED.</head>
        <p>WHAT PRAYER WEEK SHOWED.</p>
        <p>A united communion service, held on Sunday evening in the Golborne-street Baptist. Church, concluded the Warrington Free Church Coun- cil's special Week of prayer. Considering the inclemency of the weather, there was an excel- lent attendance. The Rev. H. Ogden conducted the service, in which the following ministers also took part: The Revs. William Reid (who gave the address), T. T. James, W. Thompson Derry, A. J. Chessum (who read the lesson), C. P. Pitt and George Curtis. The Rev. H. Ogden drew the attention of the Churches to the forth- coming " Come-to-Church " campaign, and said there were those who were sceptical of the re- sult, saying that they might succeed in securing a few to attend church on the two specified Sundays, but that they would lose sight of them afterwards. Yet if not a single individual was secured permanently for the Churches, the Churches would still have accomplished their necessary duty, in that every householder had been invited to join in the worship of God's House.—The Rev. William Reid said that the paucity of the attendance at the previous week's special prayer meetings revealed the fact that the people did not believe that "more things are wrought by prayer than this world dreams of."</p>
        <p>0 4,4</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>BANE OF WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>THE BANE OF WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Lnkewarmness and Indifference in</p>
        <p>Things Religious.</p>
        <p>In connection with the "Come-to-Church" campaign now being organised by the Warring- ton Free Church Council, the members of the following churches will convey invitations to attend divine service, during the week from the 17th to the 24th of this month, to all the householders of the town: Wycliffe and Elm- wood-avenue Congregational; Bold-street, Bew- sey-road, Stockton Heath, Latchford, Padgate, Sankey Bridges, Penketh, Winwick, and Moore Wesleyan; St. John's Presbyterian; Golborne- street and Latchford Baptist; Bold-street Em- manuel; Legh-street and Knutsford-road Primi- tive Methodist; Friars' Green, Stockton Heath, and Grappenhall Independent Methodist; and Bank Quay Mission. 16,000 POST-CARD INVITATIONS. Sixteen thousand post-cads urging thAlaims of public worship have b&amp;n secured from the %National Free Church Council, and it is hoped tHat, as a result of the personal visits and invi- tations, a large number who have almost uncon- sciously drifted away from the churches may be induced again to join in Sabbath worship. Interviewed by " The Examiner" representa- tive, the Secretary of the local Free Church Council stated that their National Council had at first deemed it wise, in view of the war, to drop the cainpaign for the time being. It was, however, decided to go on with it, as there was a feeling that the nation had become more serious, sober and inclined to hear about God. WARRINGTON AN AVERAGE TOWN IN MATTERS RELIGIOUS.</p>
        <p>Warrington; in matters religious, was just an average town. With most Lancashire towns, she shared a strange aversion to Sunday morning worship, and regarded the Sabbath as a day for domestic tea meetings and friendly visits. The bane of the town was essentially luke-warmness and indifference. Probably the town was no worse—and certainly no better—for flagrant wickedness than any other town in the county, but the Churches were very really and very truly anxious to bring the people into touch with active expression of God's will and pur- pose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>(Mirth grim.</head>
        <p>(Mirth grim.</p>
        <p>ELMWOOD CONGREGATIONALISTS Form A Brotherhood On Adult School</p>
        <p>Lines.</p>
        <p>More than thirty men assembled in the Guild Room at the Elmwood-avenue Congregational Church on Monday evening, when a meeting was held for the purpose of forming a Brother- hood—a step which it had been decided to take some weeks before. After some discussion, it was resolved to run the new organisation on the lines of an Adult School, and that, although it is to be distinctly connected with the Elmwood- avenue Church, the members shall be allowed to bring their friends if they so desired.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>Main Purpose.</head>
        <p>The Main Purpose.</p>
        <p>The Pastor, the Rev. C. P. Pitt (who presided), said the chief purpose of the Brotherhood would be to bring the men of the Church together in social intercourse.</p>
        <p>A short form of service was decided upon for</p>
        <p>the meetings, which are to be held in the Guild Room every Monday evening from 8 to 10 o'clock. For the first hour of each meeting there is to be given a paper dealing with any subject— religious, historical, or scientific—except poli- tics. This will be followed by a discussion on the subject raised by the taper, after which a "free and easy" discussimN will take place, the men being allowed to talk in groups and smoke. Each meeting will close with a good, rousin hymn.</p>
        <p>President and Secretary Elected.</p>
        <p>No charge is to be made for membership, but there will be a box into which each one attending may drop a penny if he so desires— the proceeds going towards defraying expenses. The Rev. C. P. Pitt has been appointed presi- dent, and Mr. W. Evans secretary; but as yet no other officials have been decided upon. As the, Brotherhood develops, it is hoped to form different committees—including a Visiting Com- mittee, which, it is thought, could do much towards increasing the membership on the well- known " Catch-my-pal " lines. During the meeting one or two hymns were</p>
        <p>heartily sung. Mr: H. Farrar 'Allying the piano.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>WARNING VOICES UNHEARD.</head>
        <p>WARNING VOICES UNHEARD.</p>
        <p>Rev. B. Percival and Nation's Heed- lessness ot War Sifmns. WHAT GERMANY WANTS.</p>
        <p>At St. Peter's Church on Sunday the Vicar, the Rev. B. Percival, preached his 22nd anni- versary sermon. He should have done so the previous Sunday, but, owing to illness, was unable to attend. The preacher, who took for his subject the Church motto for the year—"Be strong and of a good courage, for God is with you "—said that his reason for choosing those words was that he felt that they needed strength for the coming year. The past year had been an eventful one—full of surprises and anxieties, and, also, of great encouragements. The war came upon them like a bolt from the blue at the beginning of August; though, in a sense, they were not without warning, for, just as Nature held• out danger-signals in regard to , their bodies, so God raised up warning voices with regard to this war years ago—voices which, alas! for the most part, were unheeded. GERMANY'S GRUDGE AGAINST BRITAIN. "Fourteen years ago," continued the preacher, "I spent nine months in Switzerland, where a large number of the visitors were Germans. There I had an opportunity of finding out their real feelings towards this country, and found that they had no ill-feeling against an Eng- lishman, as an Englishman, but their grudge against us as a nation was very bitter, The fact is that Germany woke up too late in regard to colonisation, and found that the most desir- able parts of the world were in the hands of others. England has the most, and so they want to grapple with Us and crush us, and when they meet in ilublic they drink to what they call "The Event." But, whatever Ger- many's ambitions are, they are mistaken. They think that Might can triumph over Right, and forget that the world can only be conquered by love and simplicity, and that it is 'the meek who shall inherit the earth.' HAS GERMAN POWER REACHED ITS</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>LIMIT?</head>
        <p>LIMIT?</p>
        <p>"There have been days of great anxiety both for us and our Allies, and the New Year may have more such days in store for us. But I think that German power has reached its limit, that it has already failed in its object, and that it will, in due time, be completely over- thrown."</p>
        <p>Much, continued the preacher, had been said about the right and wrong of war. Some held that wars were wrong. There were few people who believed in war for war's sake,and, surely, nobody in this country believed in war when methods were used to crush everything that stood in the way. Yet such methods were being used by Germany, and they had to oppose them for honour's sake—oppose them with the same weapons, but not with the same methods. This country's cause *as a righteous one.</p>
        <p>FIGHTING FOR FREEDOM AND TRUTH. Freedom and truth were the things they were fighting for. Courage helped them to endure</p>
        <p>The Coffee Maid, of Symington's, " Symington's of course. No other making coffee is good enough, quick enough, or easy enough for me." LILO&amp; SY AUNGION &amp;CO.. E.iislrregh &amp; Lo.den.</p>
