      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>SINGLE</head>
        <p>SINGLE</p>
        <p>TUNINGS</p>
        <p>DAVtir ON S 9 59, Sankey St., Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>EXAMINER,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, JANUARY 16, 1915.</p>
        <p>THE SIN OF TREATING SOLDIERS.</p>
        <p>If we were not the most unemotional people on the face of the earth, the Police Court 'case heard at Stockton Heath and repotted in the local Press last week 'would have roused us to a state of revolt and produced such a volume of indignation that we should have been within thea-surable distance of taking some step to end a most dis- graceful state of things. But we remain dumb, like those prepared to accept the inevitable. Just befoh s LORD ROBERTS died he addressed an appeal to the nation—his last—upon a subject that was causing him "great uneasi- ness." "I feel it my duty to point out to the civil population," said this great soldier, standing almost beside his open grave, "that putting temptation in the way of our soldiers by injudi- ciously treating them to drink is injurious to them and prejudicial to our chances of victory." It was the knowledge that vodka was prejudicial to the Russian chances of victory that caused the CZAR to stop the sale of drink in his great Empire, and it is because the -results of stopping it have been so satisfactory that the CZAR has decided to prohibit the sale "for ever." LORD KITCHENER has " beiged " everyone to "avoid treating soldiers. Medical scientists, including BARLOW, TREVES, EVATT, HORS,LEY and WOODHEAD have sent out a manifesto to His Majesty's Naval and Military Forces, stating that alcoholic drtink slows the power to see signals, confuses prompt judgment, spoils accuiPate shooting, hastens fatigue, lessens resistance to disease and exposure, and increases shock from -wounds. These are the estab- lished facts which made LORD ROBERTS "uneasy," and caused him to declare that treating was "prejudicial to our chances of victory." In some places the warning has been taken seriously to heart; in Warrington we have gone on very much as usual. There is, we are, told, nothing abnormal in our drunkenness statistics. There was nothing abnormal, we may be sure, in the drunkenness statistics of Russia. They are always much the same and are never pleasant reading. The dif- ference in Russia and here is that we are now engaged in a life-and-death struggle for national existence.. There is no doubt about that; and the ques- tion is—Are we so well provided with men that we can afford voluntarily and recklessly to make ten per cert. of them inefficient in honour of our normal drinking habits?</p>
        <p>In those drinking habits "treating" lakes high rank; the Stockton Heath cage to which we referred reveals IriNtins in its most ViCiftliS. form. Two</p>
        <p>privates of the Manchesfier Special Re- serve were placed on tie highly impor- tant. work of guare.ting the Walton Bridge over the Shipytanal. A slight slip from the strict pact), of duty might mean the destruction, 'of the bridge and the dislocation ofAhe trade of one of the most vital crincerns in the country. But one of the men was so under the influence of drink that he assaulted a constable on his beat, and the other was so befogged that he came to his fellow-segtry's assistance, and we had the edifying spectacle of military guards loading jup their rifles as a menace to the representative of civil law and order. The policeman had to arrest the guard! JORDAN told the magistrates that</p>
        <p>a man gave him a bottle of whisky and a woman gave him a drink Of beer. He then lost his senses altogether. He left the Army with a good character after eight years' service and was sorry for what had happened. It was the first time he had been in trouble. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Keenan said he was sorry, as it was his first offence. The whisky caused the trouble. A military officer said the men had an abso- lutely clean record. Perhaps the holiday had got over them. The people about were really more to blame than the soldiers, for "one brought one thing to them and another some- thing else."</p>
        <p>It was stated that the prisoners, who were fined, would also be court- martialled. We can only regret that the beer woman and the whisky man who/ brought disgrace upon ,them, at 'a time when they Were engaged in the noblest ocupation of serving their country, are not to. be court-martialled, too. They are., by far,- the greatest sinners.</p>
        <p>It may be well to inform those who are disposed to allure men by drink from the proper discharge of their duty and, incidentally, cause them to endanger the safety of their country, that the selling and giving of drink do nof raise only moral consider- ations. There are legal penalties also— penalties Of the severest type. The Naval ana ,Military Authorities may make Orders closing licensed premises in certain areas for certain times, and breaches of those Orders involve trial by courts-martial, whose penalties may range up to penal servitude for life. For ordinary treating,, such as in the case under review, the offender is tried by the magistrates. "If any person," runs a:paragraph in the Defence of the Realm Act, •</p>
        <p>gives or sells to a member of any of His Majesty's Forces any intoxicants when not on duty, with intent to make him drunk, or less capable of the efficient discharge of his duties, or when on sentry or other duty either with or without such intent, he shall be guilty of an offence against these regulations.</p>
        <p>And the punishment on the offender being found guilty may be six months, with hard labour, or a fine of £lOO, or both. If these penalties were ever richly earnedlhey were earned by the treaters in this case ; and it is fortuna:te for them that the police have been unable, though theyl have tried hard, to discover 'their identity.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>TO A FALSE PATRIOI.</head>
        <p>TO A FALSE PATRIOI.</p>
        <p>He caine obedient to the Call; He might have shirked like half his mates Who, while their comrades fight and fall,</p>
        <p>go to swell the football gates. And you, a patriot in your prime, You waved a flag above his head, And hoped he'd have a high old time, And slapped him on the back and said:</p>
        <p>You'll show 'em what we British are! give us your hand, old pal, to shake"; And took him round from bar to bar And made him drunk—for England's sake.</p>
        <p>That's how you helped him. Yesterday, Clear-eyed and earnest, keen and hard, He held himself the soldier's way— And now they've got him under guard.</p>
        <p>That doesn't hurt you; you're all right; Your easy conscience takes no blame; But he, poor boy, with morning's light, He eat's his heart out, sick with shame.</p>
        <p>'What's that to you? You understand Nothing of all his bitter pain; You have no regiment to brand; You have no uniform to stain;</p>
        <p>No vow of service to abuse, No pledge to King and country due; But he had something dear to lose, And he has lost it—thanks ,to you. —O.S7 in "</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>ROLL OF HONOUR.</head>
        <p>ROLL OF HONOUR.</p>
        <p>KILLED IN A CHARGE.</p>
        <p>Comrade's Story of Gallant Private's</p>
        <p>End.</p>
        <p>Mrs. J. S. Thompson, formerly of 19, Portland- street, but now residing with relatives at 60, Bostock - street, has received news from the War office, of the death of her hus- band, Private John Silas Thompson, of the 3rd Battalion of the South Lancashire Regiment, who was killed in action at Neuve Chappelle, Belgium, on October 27th. According to a com- rade, who is on furlough recovering from wounds, Private Thompson was struck in the neck by a piece of shrapnel, whilst taking part in a charge, and was killed instantaneously, his body remaining in the trench for three days, the fighting in the locality being so fierce and unremitting that nobody dare venture out of cover to bury it. Private Thompson was called up with the first batch of Reservists. Prior to receiving the call, he was working for Messrs. J. Crosfield and Sons.</p>
        <p>Whilst at the Front he wrote a number of affectionate letters to his wife. In one of them he told of the capture of a band of 250 Germans, and mentioned, as evidence of the fact that the British treat their prisoners as well as cir- cumstances permit, the fact that they were being fed exactly the same as the British " Tommies," viz., on biscuits and bully beef. In a subsequent letter he penned the phrase: "I hope and trust I shall be a man, and that I shall get to see dear old England again." An- other time he wrote he mentioned the fact that tobacco is served out to the "boys," but that he, like the majority of them, preferred eigB.l.ll Mrs. Thompson is left with four young chil- dren.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>HOW TO MAKE YOUR OWN BREAD.</head>
        <p>HOW TO MAKE YOUR OWN BREAD.</p>
        <p>Small delicious loaves and rolls can be made easily and quickly by using BORWICK'S BAKING POWDER. Full directions on every pactaze.l Olgoiltai* a ail Oractrs.</p>
