      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 16, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 16, 1915.</p>
        <p>EVERYWHERE,</p>
        <p>The linssians invented wood paving for streets.</p>
        <p>Pleasure always knocks louder tham Opportu- nity.</p>
        <p>Food for reflection is a poor substitute for a square' meal.</p>
        <p>Many a liroman makes up everything except her mind.</p>
        <p>One child has more faith than a thousand grown-ups.</p>
        <p>There are no dead ones like those who are killed with kindness.</p>
        <p>Why are we supposed to have more respect for grey hairs than for a bald head?</p>
        <p>About the most expensive thing a man car do is to associate with cheap people.</p>
        <p>It isn't what your enemies say about you•tha' urts; it's what your friends say.</p>
        <p>Lots of people are bubbling over with enthu siasm, but bubbles don't accomplish much.</p>
        <p>. A man takes almost as much pleasure in keep. ing a secret as a woman does in telling one.</p>
        <p>Some people spend half their time wondering low they are going to spend the other .half.</p>
        <p>It's a sign of love when a young man squan- ders a whole month's salary On an engagement ring.</p>
        <p>Sorne,men are not satisfied to lie the architects of their own fortunes unless they are constantly adding extensions.</p>
        <p>When you hear a man remark that accidents will happen you may be pretty sure he has been doing something he shouldn't.</p>
        <p>If the good die young, must we suppose the bad become oldest inhabitants and lie about their ages, the weather and sundry other things?</p>
        <p>The largest tenement house in the world is Freilhaus, in a suburb of Vienna, containing 1,500 rooms, divided into upwards of 400 separ- ate apartments.</p>
        <p>There are five capital offences under British law—murder, high treason, piracy, arson in the port of London, and attempts to destroy Public arsenals.</p>
        <p>Gas Bills. Hixon "Who invented gas meters?" Dixon: "Judging from my gas bills I should say Ananias."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>Not Yet.</head>
        <p>Not Yet.</p>
        <p>"The enemy is not yet subdued," announced the Kaiser in his New Year's address to his troops. It is gratifying to have this rumour confirmed from a source so unimpeachable.— "Punch."</p>
        <p>Self-Denial.</p>
        <p>Teacher: "What do you understand by the word ` self-denial ' ? " Pupil: "It is when some one comes to borrow money from father and he says he is not at home."</p>
        <p>Certainly the Rule. "Young man," said the stern parent, "I de- voted my life to business. You are devoting yours to pleasure." "Well," replied the flippant . youth, "that's the good old rule; business before pleasure."</p>
        <p>The Way With Most of Us. Sunday School Teacher: "Elmer; which would you rather have hurt—your feelings or your finger?" Little Elmer: "my finger." Sunday School Teacher: "Why?" Elmer: "Cause I can't tie a rag around my feelings."</p>
        <p>Economy. McTavish (to convalescent soldier): "I was hearin' ye had a bullet in ye yet. Are ye no gown ta hae it taen cot?" Soldier: "No, the noo. Ye see, I'll be gawn back tae the fr-ront in a wee while, an' when I come back just hae them a' oot thegither !" —"Punch."</p>
        <p>A Suggestion. A wag suggests that the real cause of the in- vasion of Belgium is that the Germans have recently discovered that there are more public- houses there in proportion to the population than in any other part of the world. What German could resist a beerhouse?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>Grim Kitchener.</head>
        <p>Grim Kitchener.</p>
        <p>A story is told which illustrates Lord Kitch- ener's grim manner. The Prince of Wales was very anxious ,to go to the front? but his Lord- ship barred the way. H.R.H. called at the War office. "I must go on active service," he said. "I will go; I don't care if I. am killed." "Neither do " (!) replied the Secretary for War, "but I can't have you taken prisoner !"</p>
        <p>Excitement in the Village. The Tourist (passing a week-end in the vil- lage) to the Oldest Inhabitant: "Well, I don't know what you do here. It's certainly the most dead-and-alive show I was ever in." The Oldest Inhabitant: " Ah, 'e ought to wait till next wek, zur, an' you'll see 'ow the place 'nil be stirred up then." The Tourist: "Why, what's on next week?" The Oldest Inhabitant: "Ploughin'."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>Corrected.</head>
        <p>Corrected.</p>
        <p>Two Highlanders in London for the first time were greatly amazed at the heavy street traffic While they were standing awestruck on the pavement a watercart passed spraying the dusty streets.</p>
        <p>" lley, mon," yelled Donald, running after the cart, " ye're login' a' yer water." "Come back, ye old fule," called his com- panion after him. " Dae ye no' ken that's tae keep the bairns frae sittin' on the back o' the cairt?"</p>
        <p>Couldn't Fool Him." A Clergyman who made a study of antiques was riding on the outside of a coach in the West of England, when the driver said to him: "I've had a coin giv' to me to-day 200 years old. Did you ever see a coin 200 years old?" "Oh, yes; I have one myself 2,000 years old." "Ah !" said the driver, "have ye?" and spoke no more during the rest of the journey. When the coach arrived at its destination the driver turned to the clergyman with an in- tensely self-satisfied air and said: •"I told you as we druv' along that I had a coin 200 years old." "Yes."</p>
        <p>"And you said you had one 2,090 'years old?" "Yes, so I have." "That's not true." "What do you mean by that?" "What do I mean? Why, it's only 1908 now."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>KAISER'S JOURNEY.</head>
        <p>KAISER'S JOURNEY.</p>
        <p>It's a long way to get to Paree, It's a long way to go; It's the wrong way through little Belgium, The wrongest way we know. Good bye, Kaiser Billy; Farewell, 0 mein Herr; It's a long, long way to St. Helena. But your home's right there.</p>
        <p>Pu u]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>INDISPENSABLE HORSE.</head>
        <p>INDISPENSABLE HORSE.</p>
        <p>One of the innovations of this war, writes a correspondent, has been the substitution of the motor for the horse. A horse is a rare sight now except at the stabling stations near the front. Almost the only branch of activity in which the horse is still indispensable is for rushing field artillery into action, for motors cannot smash through hedges and over broken ground as our splendid horses can.</p>
        <p>MiliftMV.l</p>
        <p>Int</p>
        <p>'SM&amp;,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>BANK REPORTS.</head>
        <p>BANK REPORTS.</p>
        <p>PARR'S BANK, LTD.</p>
        <p>The 50th ordinary general meeting of share- holders of Parr's Bank, Ltd., will be held at the Cannon-street Hotel, London, E.C., on Thurs- day, january 28th. The transfer books of the bank will be closed from ,Wednesday, January 20th, to Thursday, February 11th, both days inclusive.</p>
        <p>The directors, after writing down investaie.pts to below market value of 27th July, 1914 (the date of the latest official making up prices before the closing of the Stock Exchange owing to the war), providing for all bad and doubtful debts, and contributing to staff widows' fund, will recommend a •dividend for the past half- year at the rate of 18 per cent. per annum, sub- ject to deductioa of income tax, Taking 19 per cent. for the year 1914, leaving a balance of £225,670 to be carried forward to the credit of next account. _ _</p>
        <p>The distribution for 1913 was at the rate of 21 per cent. per annum, with allocations to bank premises account of £30,000, and officers' pension and provident fund £25,000, carrying forward £109,606.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>LANCASHIRE AND YORKSHIRE.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE AND YORKSHIRE.</p>
        <p>At the forty-second annual general meeting, to be held January 29th, the directors of the Lan- cashire and Yorkshire Bank, Ltd., will report that, notwithstanding war complications, the bank has continued to make progress, the num- ber of customers showing a gratifying increase. Net profits were £153,059 2s. 7d., increased by the balance in hand to £168,387 is. 9d. The directors, says the report, feel that at such a time they will have the approval of the shareholders in adopting a cautious policy, and they therefore recommend, in addition to the interim dividend of 17s. per share, paid in July. amounting to £73,326 25., a further dividend of 16s. per share for the half-year ended 31st December, 1914, subject to deduction of income tax, £69,012 165.; making a total distribution of 161 per cent. for the year, less income tax; ap- propriating to reserve fund (raising it to £660,000), £5,000; and carrying forward to profit and loss new account, £21,048 3s. 9d. The valuation of the investments of the bank , is based on the prices of July 27th, 1914.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>UNION BANK OF MANCIIESTER.</head>
        <p>UNION BANK OF MANCIIESTER.</p>
        <p>The net profits of the Union Bank or Man- chester, Limited, for the half-year ended Decem- ber 31st, 1914, including £17,018 brought for- ward, amount to £68,534. The directors have declared a dividend of 121 per cent., less income tax (being the same as last year), absorbing £31,152; have provided .k 1,200 for income tax; .0,000 for premises reduction; £2,000 for pen- sion fund; and have appropriated £20,000 against depreciation of the investments held b;\ the Bank, thereby writing them down to the prices current on the 31st December, 1914; carry- ing forward £8,182.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>LONDON,, CITY AND MIDLAND.</head>
        <p>LONDON,, CITY AND MIDLAND.</p>
        <p>The directors of the London, City and Mid- land Bank, Ltd., report that the profits for the year ending 31st December last are £1,106,809, which, with the balance brought forward of £147,993, amount to £1,254,802. They recom- mend a dividend for the last six months at the rate of 18 per cent. per annum, less income tax, payable on the Ist February next, which, with the interim dividend paid in July last, amounts to £785,794, appropriating £27,723 to pay in- come tax for shareholders for the half-year ending 30th June last, £20,000 to officers' pen- sion fund, and carrying forward. £421,285. The dividend for the year 1913 was at the same rate, with £43,774 for income tax for share- holders, £326,000 for writing down investments, £50,000 for building redemption fund, £20,900 for officers' pension fund, £30,000 bonus for the staff, and £147,993 carried forward.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>WAR ITEMS.</head>
        <p>WAR ITEMS.</p>
        <p>LEAVE FOR WARRINGTON "TERRIERS."</p>
        <p>During next week the whole of the members of the .4th Battalion of the South Lancashire Regiment Will be given four days' leave.</p>
        <p>MORE WARRINGTONIANS FOR THE WAR.</p>
        <p>A further 275 men have enlisted in all branches of the Service at the Police Scation during the week. From Warrington alone 175 men have joined, and the other 100 men have come from St. Helens and the outlying dis- tricts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>MORE RECRUITS FROM THE WORKS.</head>
        <p>MORE RECRUITS FROM THE WORKS.</p>
        <p>The recruits from the larger Warrington Works this week number 32. Fifteen have gone from Messrs. Rylands Bros.', ten from Messrs. Pearson and Knowles', four from Messrs. Monks, Hall and Co.'s, and three from Messrs. Cros- field's. Since the war broke out 531 men have joined from Messrs. Pearson and Knowles' works. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>PATRIOTIC BANKERS.</head>
        <p>PATRIOTIC BANKERS.</p>
        <p>The report of the directors of the Lancashire and Yorkshire Bank, Ltd., for 1914 states that the staff have loyally responded to the King's call, and 92 have joined the colours. They will be gladly received back into the service after the termination of the war. The remain- ing members of the staff have acted a loyal part in dealing with the extra work thrown on them. Among those of the staff who have joined the colours are J. T. Picton, Warrington; and R. Balshaw, Broadheath. The Bank has sub- scribed £l,OOO to the Pridc'e of Wales' Nation'al Relief Fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>NATIONAL RESERVE COMPANY OF THE 4th SOUTH LANCS. FORMED.</head>
        <p>NATIONAL RESERVE COMPANY OF THE 4th SOUTH LANCS. FORMED.</p>
        <p>About a week ago a(full company of 120— namely, the Ist National Reserve Company, 4th South Lancashire Regiment—was started at the Drill Hall, since when recruiting has pro- ceeded busily; with a result that only 20 more men are now' required for the company, which will doubtless be completed by Tuesday. Per- mission has been applied for, however, to form another half-company. The following officers have already been ap- pointed: .Captain A. C. Wright, Lieutenants P. R. Arrowsmith and F. A. Klipsch; whilst the N.C.O.'s are: Colour-Sergeant G. Fern,, Sergt. D. Gray, and Corporals C. T. Cox, W. H. Kelly, P. Kilgallon, B. H. Naylor and H. Warburton. A short time ago the half-company lefft for the South of England, where they are guarding five miles of railway. The remainder will fol- low as soon as their uniforms are ready.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>GUARDIANS ANSWER "THE CALL."</head>
        <p>GUARDIANS ANSWER "THE CALL."</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Runcorn Board of Guar- dians on Tuesday a letter was read from one of the members, Mr. Henry Talbot, of Aston Ledge, stating that he had been appointed to command the Cheshire Infantry (Territorial) Reserve Brigade, and was taking up his duties at once. He would, therefore, be unable to attend meetings for the time being. Ralph Gibbs, one of the Runcorn mem.: hers, also wrote regretting his' inability to attend the Central Poor Law Conference at London, owing to his having joined the Ist City Battalion of the King's (Liverpool) Regiment. It was decided that the Clerk write to the two members expressing the Board's good wishes and hopes for their safe return. The Chairman: Of course, they will be immune from the six months' limit?</p>
        <p>Mr. Mclntyre: Oh, yes; that is provided for in the event of members serving with the Forces.</p>
        <p>_ Mr.J.Hewitt, of Stretton, was appointe dele- gate to the Poor Law Conference, in the place of Mr. Gibbs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>CLAY DRESSING FOR LIGHT LAND.</head>
        <p>CLAY DRESSING FOR LIGHT LAND.</p>
        <p>Where the soil is hot and sandy a good dress- ing of clay is very often more valuable than manure. These clay dressings are best wheeled on to the ground during the, winter so that the frost may break them down and mellow them. Such a dressing is as good for beds and borders as for the kitchen garden in all cases where the soil is very light and sandy, and ,therefore wanting in body.—Wick in "Farm and Home."</p>
        <p>lINN&amp;MKr</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH "KING."</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH "KING."</p>
        <p>Mr. Benjamin Bennett's Connection With Hatten. PROMINENT WESLEYAN FAMILY.</p>
        <p>Mr. Benjamin Bennett, who was crowned lastl week at Stockton Health as the oldest resident , of the village who 'was able to attend the annual aged folks' treat, has had a tran- quil but useful career. He is, in fact, one of that large army of yeomen which has from time immemorial been the backbone of our national life, and the foundation upon which • our Imperial expansion has rested. He has lived in Stockton Heath for eleven years only. Prior to his retirement he had "lived on a farm" for seventy-fhree years, and had done his duty to the community in every respect during the whole of that long period. Born at Pillmoss Farm, Hatton, in the occu- pation of his father, Mr. Bennett first worked for his father and then succeeded him, later taking Greenside Farm in the same township. Before bhanging farms, Mr. Bennett mar•ied in 1859, going to Macclesfield for his bride. It was a happy union, and almost as long as happy, for the couple were not only able to celebrate their Golden Wedding Day, in 15309, but they succeeded in rearing six daughters and four sons, the first death in the home being that of Mrs. Bennett, who, two years ago, died full of , years and honours. Those sons and daughters</p>
        <p>Photograph by Birtles,</p>
        <p>MR. BENNETT AT EIGHTY-FOUR.</p>
        <p>are mostly married, and in their respective spheres are following careers in Warrington and elsewhere quite in keeping with the example set them by their inankdiate and in- dustrious forebears. There are 24 grand- children. . _</p>
        <p>The Bennett family have always been stal- warts in the Wesleyan toey. When at Hatton they were the leading O.:its in the erection of the Wesleyan School, which was also used as a chapel under the Warrington Circuit. A heir- loom, treasUred and well preserved, in the shape of a silver trowel, accompanied by a miniature mallet, bears eloquent testimony to this fact in the following inscription : Presented to Mrs. Benjamin Bennett on laying he foundation-stone of Hatton Wesleyan Schools, .ILine 10th, 1875.</p>
        <p>For several reasons, upon which it is needless to en'cer here, Nonconformity does not thrive at Hatton, and this combined school and chapel was, in the course of years, sold. It became the property of the C,I) nrch, and is now a mis- sion to Daresbury- Chure.h. /t is likely that the withdrawal of s'ach supporters as the Bennett fanily of twelve and their labourers had something to do with th collapse of Wesleyisn; at Hatton; but. by a strange stroke of good luck, the proceeds of the sale, £6OO, became the nucleus for the buildirg, of - the Wesleyan, Church at Stockton Heath, at which Mrs. Ben- nett frequently worshipped in the last days of her life. In an indirect 'way, therefore, the funds she had helped to raise in the 'Seventies became available for Stockton ,lleaq, where she ultimately settled.. When the Bennetts left the country for the town they took a milk business in Scheel Brow, and then moved to Clapgate Farm, Lovely-lane, where for many years they carried on a thriving -dairy business.. One of Mr. Bennett's .sons re- mained on the farm when the "old folk" re- tired to Stockton Heath, and is still there. - Mr. Bennett has a. grand constitution as the state of his health at 84 proves. When he was seen by our representative this week, he owned to having nothing the matter with him but hunger, which, as he is living in much comfort with a daughter at Victoria-road, was his humorous way of .saying that he has still a very good and ready appetite. He also pleaded guilty to a little "rheumatics," but for this he blamed the weather., During the groat cattle plague Mr. Bennett, when at Hatton, lost fifteen milking cows, a loss which was aggravated by • the factithat it came before the Compensation Fund was estabf- lished. He represented Hatton on the Runcorn Board of Guardians, and his other activities included the post of special village constable.</p>
        <p>r k</p>
        <p>- - • ,••-, - =',?.,0•,-,;,l•Y:'-' :•,'-' ' , , ) • . ' , , , , •• , / ... 4 , 'a,',li' 1? v4W .108' "*.%1,1: „&lt; .., , -, , • • ' ' ' "liiii*; • :'''''' -: ,' ' - " I , ~,t; 7'.• 3'4, I • 4 ~,,, , I ,• , •,. • , . • , , , ~ ' , 4i,, `,,,';;›,, • '',.'4 ' *-'14,,, • ,'' .r..l.''' v`,".4fy., •• "", ',;',.'''''''''''':'.,,,:', '. ' . i ',- ''•',4- , ',,, ''''''74,",e ',.- , ' 1 ",;///,?.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>"QUEEN."</head>
        <p>THE "QUEEN."</p>
        <p>St4ry of Her 11,1jesty's Chreer.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Boddie, who, like the "King," is also 84 years of ,age, was, last week, " orowned " with mob cap and shawl as the "Queen." She is the widow of the late Mr. James ,Fred G. Boddie, who died in the village two years ago. Mrs. Boddie was born in Dover, and it was from that town that, along with her father and mother, Mr. and Mrs. Thompson, and other members of the family, she went to Ber- muda, where her father was a tailor. Mr. Boddie, who was an engineer, worked there for some time, and after marrying Miss Thompson, as the "Queen" then was, he settled, with his wife, in Portsmouth, the couple afterwards going to Sheffield, where he was emplpyed as an engineer. Mr. Boddie retired about twenty years ago, and.; in 1900, he, along with his wife, came to Stockton Heath to live with their daughter and son-in-law, Mr. and Mrs. L. Batey, formerly of Portsmouth, and now of 113, London-road.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Boddie still enjoys good health, and, in the words of her daughter, in conversation with an " Examiner " reporter, "she has not been in bed a single day since coming to the village. She uses spectacles, but can thread a needle without their aid. She likes Stockton Heath and Warrington very much." Mrs. Boddie is a worshipper at St. Thomas' Church and attends the services regularly when the weather permits.' Her only son, Mr. Hugh Fred Boddie, resides in Portsmouth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>VALUE OF EXERCISE.</head>
        <p>THE VALUE OF EXERCISE.</p>
        <p>It is necessary to maintain laying hens in a lean, hard condition. Fat hens are rarely pro- fitable, for not only do these produce fewer eggs, but a larger proportion is infertile. It is a grave error to feed too liberal&amp; or upon too stimulating foods. Exercise is a very important factor towards health and prolificacy,' but it is not always an easy matter to persuade hens to take much exercise during the wilAter months. A great help towards this end is to have a scratching shed attached to the roosting com- partment, littering the floor with straw or chaff. The afternoonl'grain should be thrown down in the litter, affording the birds a considerable amount of exercise. Unless hens have some- thing with which to occupy their time during the winter months they are very liable to get into mischief, and contract suchf bad habits as feather-picking and egg-eating.—E. T. Brown in "Farm and Homo,"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>BEQUESTS TO THE TOWN.</head>
        <p>BEQUESTS TO THE TOWN.</p>
        <p>£l,OOO for the Infirmary and a House for the Poor.</p>
        <p>LATE MR. HITCHINGMAN'S WILL.</p>
        <p>The late Mr. JOseph Kitchingman, of "Sea- bank Nook," . Egremont Promenade, Liscard, Liverpool, bequeathed the sum of £l,OOO to/the Warrington Infirmary, and his house at t gre- mont to the Warrington Corporation "as .a place of quiet rest and vista for the poor in- habitants of Warrington." Mr. Kitchingman, who was 70 years of age, was found dead in bed on Sunday morning, and the funeral took place on Thursday. His father and mother were well-known in the, town as owners -of property. The property at the lower end of Buttermarket-street was theirs, and when the Corporation acquired it for the purpose of widening the street, Mr. Kitohing- man conducted the negotiations. He was so pleased with the manner in which the Cor— poration had dealt with him that in a letter to the Town Clerk lan December 24th, 1910, he said: "I desire to express my satisfaction and thanks for the courtesy. kindness and absolutely fair dealing shown on the part of the committee and yourself towards me in this transaction."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>Life-Governor of the Infirmary.</head>
        <p>A Life-Governor of the Infirmary.</p>
        <p>In September, 1911, Mr. Kitchingman for- warded £2l to the Infirmary, as a donation to constitute him a life-governor. He also wrote "1 have, by my will, as at present drawn, be- queathed the sum of .21,000 to the Infirmary for the purpose of the-endoWment of a bed in the institution to be called "The Mary Ann Kitch- ingman Endowment,' such being the name of My ,mother, who was born in Buttermarket- street."</p>
        <p>The bequest is, we, understand, in fulfilment of a promise made" to the Town Clerk at the tithe of the Butter Market-street negotiations: .. In addition to giving his residence to his trustees upon trust, to allow the Corporation the use of the same for the purpose stated abO4, he has further directed that his trustees may raise and pay to the corporation a capital sum for altering and improving the, house, and an annual sum for its complete maintenance, up- keep and support. Mr. ICitehingman was a retired solicitor and had, since his retirement, devoted' himself to art, his works being frequently hung at the Liverpool Autumn Exhibition. About eight months ago he visited the Infirm- ary and seconded an expression of satisfaclion with all he saw in the institution by presenting it: with an invalid chair.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Lyon Whittle (town clerk) is one of he trustees and executors of the will.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>COMFORTS FOR OUR SOLDIERS.</head>
        <p>COMFORTS FOR OUR SOLDIERS.</p>
        <p>" TOIIAIY'S " THANK YOU!</p>
        <p>Busy Cockitedge Lassies Receive Appreciative Letters.</p>
        <p>Work in Warrington for the Grand Duke Michael's Gloves and Mittens Scheme is going on huite satisfactorily. rrn many pairs of the gloves and mittens and other comforts which hay- been forwarded to all parts of this coun- try, as well as abroad, the lassies from the different works have stitched their names and addresses—to prevent the permanent loss of the comforts should they go astray! This has given the recipients the idea of writing to thank the kind workers whose names they have thus found, and the following are? four letters typical ,of the many which have been received this week by girls employed at Messrs. Armitage and Rigby's Cockhedge Works :</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>PHILOSOPHIC "TOMMY."</head>
        <p>PHILOSOPHIC "TOMMY."</p>
        <p>"Dear Friends,—Just a few lines tot.let you know how pleased I was to hear from you again, and to thank you both very much for the parcel. It came in at a nice time, as we get some awful wettings, and have to be always changing—so the clothes came very handy. I was hit at the Battle of the Aisne on the right shoulder, and got over. that. Perhaps my good luck will hold until the end of the 'war. I am very glad to have been reMembered so well by girls .at home. I was. telling the boys about the 200 girls, and I believe most of us would rather be helping theta to knit! "We Take the Bad with the Good."</p>
        <p>"But we are easy, and take the bad with the good; and if we have the luck to come back all the better. I have had a, sample of all the parcel now, and we enjoyed it very much. We get awful wet weather bere, but I think it is worth all the discomfort when we know it is for people such as you and your sister.—Yours truly, ROBERT AIRMAN.—P.S.-: All the Germans got of the cigarettes was a smell, and we don't love them !"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>AN OPPORTUNE PRESENT.</head>
        <p>AN OPPORTUNE PRESENT.</p>
        <p>"Just a line," writes Private J. C. Lindsay, of the Ist Division British Expeditionary Force, "to say that I have become the proud possessor of the mittens you so kindly knitted for the sol- diers at the front. They arrived at a very opportune moment, as I am leaving to-night for. the trenches, where everything that keeps one warm is a blessing. I can assure you that the Tommies ' out here most -highly appreciate.the work of the women folk at home, who spend their time, &amp;c., in knitting the articles that will keep them comfortable. You, a Lancashire, lassie, might be interested when I tell you that: I am Scotch and a Dundee laddie. I belong to, the Black Watch, which is a kilted regiment, and one which used to have plenty of Lanca-. shire ladies in it. My greatest chum . . . belonged to Lancashire. Wounded, But All Right Again.</p>
        <p>"I have now been five months in this country —I was unfortunate enpugh to get wounded at Ypres on the 27th October, but I am • quite all right again now—and, as I said before, going, to try my luck , a second time. I didn't have the luck to get home, but if I get home when, it is all over I shall be satisfied. With best wishes to you and all others."' "GOOD LUCK TO YOU," SAYS GRATEFUL</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>"TOMMY."</head>
        <p>"TOMMY."</p>
        <p>"I am very grateful," declares Private Wm... Dixon, of the 3rd Lancashire Regiment, Expe- ditionary Force, for the 'woollen mittens that I have just received. They come in handy,. because it has been freezing and snowing in the. trenches, and they are warm. All I can do now is to wish, you and yours the best luck and a• happy New Year. Good luck to you . "I wish you a bright New Year" is the sentiment of Private J. J. Phcenix, of the D.A.C., British Expeditionary Force, Northern, France. "I received the mittens which you have knitted for the boys at the front, and send' my kindest regards to you and,yours</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>FOR OUR 'WOUNDED GUESTS. Alio!her Successful Pound Day At</head>
        <p>FOR OUR 'WOUNDED GUESTS. Alio!her Successful Pound Day At</p>
        <p>Raddon Court.</p>
        <p>Thursday was "Pound lAy " at Raddcm Court Hospital, and a great success it proved to be, the. gifts brought 'by a large number of visitors being many and varied. Good supplies of note- paper, fruit, green vegetables and. groceries, which were specially asked for, were received, and the Matron wishes to thank all who so generously contributed. _\</p>
        <p>- Eight Belgians having 'left on Thursday for London, there ;are now 26 patrerets left in the hospital. These may be visited on Mondays and Saturdays, from 2 to 3 o'clock, and on Thurs- days from 6 'to 7 o'clock.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>LIBERAL WOMEN'S LARGE CONSIGNMENT.</head>
        <p>LIBERAL WOMEN'S LARGE CONSIGNMENT.</p>
        <p>The Women's--Liberal Sewing Class continues to make good progress, and this week has for- warded a large consignment of body-belts, wool- len helmets and bandages to Miss Truda. Cros- field and Mrs. Eva McLaren, for ,distribution amongst the soldiers at the Eva McLaren Hos- pital, France. It will be remembered that Mrs. McLaren belongs to the Lancashire .and Che- shire Women's Suffrage Union, of which Miss Crosfield is secretary.</p>
        <p>Mrs. G. S. Pendfebury and Miss Short are also sending 150 packets.of cigarettes to Miss Crosfield, who will distribute them amongst the wounded warriors at tit hospital.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
