        <p>, , • - . . ' . . . .. _ gi, ..., -,. , r, ; •,,g; .;;41P74,1!6„..5t ' , .(",.• ...- .:" "'"," ' ..r.''' ~..; Ni,:t...,..,.."1.0*'''',', ;41• 4..:,t`.7.7 ... ; -.:* • •,..s. '...- •:' . vi.ta,oro. IL. -,..0..........- . ~...k.0,116,,,i4;4" -., ~. - . . • .„ 4,, .., 1 • :1, ~. ~ . . , ~-• . , . l• #. . ....-,„. ~:"....etteLfr -V., '' •, - , ' --.. ' -1, --.- ------.1.-.--,</p>
        <p>PUBLIC AMUSERTIITS.</p>
        <p>SP tRKLING MUSICAL COMEDY AT THE ROYAL COURT.</p>
        <p>This week Royal Court patrons are afforded the opportunity of renewing acquaintance with that charming and demure maiden, "The Quaker Girl." Or, of course, if they missed the pleasure of an introduction—quite an informal affair; all the preliminaries necessary consist- ing in "parting-up" at the pay box or the booking office—on the occasion of her last visit to Warrington, they have now got the chance of getting to know her. And hers is a case in which to get to know is to learn to like—the learning being an easy matter, for "The Qttaker Girl" is quite one of the most likeable and winsome of the many "girls" who have been introduced to lovers of musical plays at the points of the pens and tuning-forks of our brightest librettists and most tuneful com- posers.</p>
        <p>- It is only necessary to state that Adrian Ross waS responsible for most of the lyrics in this enormously successful musical play, and that Lionel Monckton. strung together the major portion of the melodies, in order to guarantee the wit and the tunefulness of the production, and, if still further recommendation be neces- sary, it may be writ, right here and now, that both Adrian and Lionel were at their best when they collaborated in this delightful irrespon- sibility—and they, at their best, are unbeatable in their own particular lines. The piece is well played by a company sent on the road by Eric Burton. Miss Georgie Corlass makes an engaging Quaker girl, singing artistically and acting with great charm. The part of the breezy Tony Chute—from the land of the almighty dollar—is breezily played—just as it should be—by Mr. A. Scott Cullen; whilst Mr. Reginald Matthews makes the most of the oart of Prince Carlo, and positively revels in the lilting Waltz-song, "Come to the ball," which is one of the features of the piece, and is by way of being Britain's reply to the "Merry Widow" and. "Chocolate Soldier" waltzes, imported from Austria. As Jeremiah, the Quaker, who "tires of Quakering" and drops his "thees" and "thous" and sets out .to "see life," Mr. Kennedy Allen is always amusing, and Mr. Gerard Wynne makes an entertaining "Chief of Police." Messrs. Vernon Booth and Tom Squire are highly successful as Captain Charteris and Monsieur Duhamel respectively. Miss Maud Denny is a charming Phcebe—just striking the right note of archness—and the: Madame I3lum of Miss Jennie Richards is a de- lightful study, whilst Miss Nellie Vancourt is excellent as Princess Alathilda, who, tiring of the life of the Court,- kicks over the traces and decides to sample the gaieties of the every-day whilligig, and finds the experiment good. It Duly remains to add that the chorus is up to its work, that the stage-effects are elaborate, and the dresses .gorgeous. Also that you have ;till a chance of seeing " The Quaker Girl"— if you hurry up!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>Fine Fare for Next Week.</head>
        <p>Fine Fare for Next Week.</p>
        <p>The popular actor, Mr. Raynor, and his trst- class company, including Miss Cecilia Dare, will appear in four • plays at the Royal .Court Theatre next week. On Monday, Tuesday and Thursday "Moths" will be presented, and on Wednesday and Saturday "The Scarlet Band," the great American detective play; will be staged. The attraction for the first house on Friday will be the famous old comedy, "Still Waters Run Deep," and for the _second house the great French Romance, "</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>MAGIC AT THE HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>MAGIC AT THE HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>"The W,izard of the Nile "—Amasis, the man cf mystery—presents a marvellous exhibition of his magic and skill at the Hippodrome this week and, it is hardly necessary to state, packed " houses " have been the rule nightly. His tricks are bewildering, but one derives a feeling of satisfaction in being bewildered and mystified in the Amasis way, because it is all done so cleverly and with the utmost nonchalance. An empty cage is -placed on a stand, three pigeons are put into a cannon, and when it is fired there is a flurry, and without, the audience laving seen .;:he birds in transit they appear instan- taneously with the .firing of the "piece" in the cage. A toy " golliwog " is put into a chest, the Sides, top and bottom of which are sounded and closed -up. Amasis fires a revolver- and out of the box trips a human "golliwog." A young lady is placed in a cage in the centre of the 'stage and disappears in a flash, her 'place being taken by ,the, aforementioned "golliwog." 'c.ril.;!Ous articles are produced, apparently from "nowhere," and returned to the smile place in a most baffling fashion, and, as a finale, Amasis is suspended in the middle of the stage, way from all "props," and instantly vanishes into space " before the very Oyes of the astounded audience: These are only a few of the acts of magic performed by this Oriental magician. Australia's leading comedian, Fred Bluett, is a , popular .-.ltirtli provoker, and, in his inimitable style, convulses the audience with his " For he's a jolly good fellow" burlesque and other equally diverting numbers. As an encore, which he is being accorded nightly, he. gives a realistic ' character study of a typical Australian , sun- downer. The Columbia Trio of humorous vocal- ists are being accorded a hearty reception, which they thoroughly deserve, for their sing- ing is rich and their humour of the best variety. "The Story of Liege" is the title of a poem recited with much feeling by one member of the trio. Comedy acrobatics of a very clever' and eccentric character are performed by Great and Good, who know ," the business" in somer- saults, tumbling and balancing, and get through it in a decidedly entertaining Manner. The Brothers Leo are bright comedians, :whose patter, gags, songs and witticisms please im- mensely. Their "Porter and Professional" number is very smart. On a variety of instru- ments the Raymond Trio are experts, and they give a bright drawing-room entertainment, into which they introduce a series of step-dances. The Fitzroys are smart gymnasts and they per- form the most daring tricks on the trapeze. Donna Barrington, soprano vocalist, also 'ap- pears. The "war , pictures" are topical and interesting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>Pantomime Next Week.</head>
        <p>Pantomime Next Week.</p>
        <p>Messrs. E. C. Jazon and M. Montgomery will present the gorgeous comic pantomime, "Dick Whittington" next week. Miss Jennie Reeve will appear as Dick, and other talented come- dians and comediennes are included in the cast. The songs and wheezes will be up-to-date and of the most tuneful and witty variety, and the scenery and dresses will be on a lavish scale. There will be a full chorus and an augmented orchestra under the direction of Mr. E. Edgar Varley. A matinee performance will he given at 2.30 on Saturday afternoon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>" STAR " FILMS AT 'STAR KINEMA.</head>
        <p>" STAR " FILMS AT 'STAR KINEMA.</p>
        <p>The all-star programmes secured for the Star Kinema have this week continued te attract very large audiences—which will doubtless con- tinue so long as the quality of the fare main- tains its high standard. The first fine bill of fare commenced with 'The' Warrior. Maid " the tenth instalment of that thrilling serial, "The Adventures of Kathlyn, Queen of :Allaha." " Pathe's Animated Gazette' supplied an abundance of up-to-date war news from differ- ent parts of the world. "Mr. Barnes of New York" was a film which could only be classed amongst the finest of its kind. A pictured ver- sion of the famous book .whose title -it bears, the film presented a most 'stirring drama woven around life and love in the Navy. Especially exciting were the duelling scenes, and it was very evident the film had only been produced at great expense. "Nan in Fairyland" was a most touching picture dealing with an episode --or was it an epoch?—in the life of a poor orphan; and the scenes in Fairyland about which she dreamed were beautifully screened. The comedy, "The Fatal High C," concluded an excellent programme. Thii 'evening the second programme—quite equal to its forerunner—concludes. No lover of living pictures should miss it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>Next Week's Big Films.</head>
        <p>Next Week's Big Films.</p>
        <p>"Cherry Blossom" is the title title of the prin- cipal film for Monday, Tuesday and Wednes- day, and for the remainder of the week "Called Back" will occupy premier place on a good bill.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>"TOMMIES" INSURANCE CARDS.</head>
        <p>"TOMMIES" INSURANCE CARDS.</p>
        <p>Holders of State Insurance cards who have joined the Army are, it was reported to the Warrington Insurance Committee on Thursday evening, not, unless and until definitely dis- charged from the service, entitled to medical benefit under the Insurance Act, and, local panel doctors am advised, should be referred to the Army doctor at Orford Barracks.</p>
        <p>/*A,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>Christianity and War.</head>
        <p>Christianity and War.</p>
        <p>At the iring House, Buttermar- ket-street at 6.30 on Sunday evening, an address on "Christiallity and War" will be given by Mr. J. J. Garnett, of Manchester.</p>
        <p>Primitive Methodist Anniversary. The anniversary of the foundation of the Knutaford-road Primitive .Methodist Chapel will be celebrated to-morrow, when the preacher —morning and evening—will be Mr. A. E. Hoyle.</p>
        <p>Local Lady's Estate. The will of the late Mrs. Mary Ann Chrimes —widow of a former Mayor of Warrington, and who, up till her death recentlj,, kept the Roe- buck Hotel, Bridge-street—has been proven at .t 4,160 gross. -</p>
        <p>Belgian Organist's Visit. , On Thursday evening, January 28th, an organ recital will be given in the Bold-street Wesleyan Church by Professor Firmin Swin- nem, organist of St. Walpurgiss and, deputy- organist of Ar.twerp Cathedral.</p>
        <p>Mayor to Help War Funds. - in aid of the Warrington Infirmary Special War Fund, the Mayor and Mayoress have arranged a whist drive and dance. This will be held in the Parr Hall on Friday, February 12th.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>Week's Births and Deaths.</head>
        <p>A Week's Births and Deaths.</p>
        <p>During the week ended January 9th 46 births were registered- in Warrington and thirty deaths, the totals for the corresponding period Of last year being forty and twenty-six respec- tl.7ely.</p>
        <p>Infectious Diseases. Twenty-five fresh 'cases of scarlet fever were reported in Warrington during the week ended January 9th, three of diphtheria and one each of ,1.-lichisis and erysipelas. There were on the Oth inst. 90 scarlet fever and five diphtheria patients in the Borough Hospital, in aVition to 22 cases of phthisis, &amp;c.</p>
        <p>Warmer Weather.</p>
        <p>The mean temperature during the week ended January 9th was 40 degrees, or two degrees higher than in the preceding week. The coldest day recently was Friday of last week, when the glass dropped to 30 degrees, whilst the warmest day was Saturday, the 9th inst., when 48 degrees were registered.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>Gone—Left No Addresses.</head>
        <p>Gone—Left No Addresses.</p>
        <p>It was reported to the Warrington Insurance Committee on Thursday that during the early part of the year no fewer than 1,400 medical cards had been returned through the post, the postal officials being unable to find the persons whose names they bore. Eight hundred of the insured persons had, however, been traced.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>On the Insurance Panel.</head>
        <p>On the Insurance Panel.</p>
        <p>It was reported to the Insurance Committee on Thursday night that inasmuch as no notices had been received from any doctors or chemists signifying their intention to cancel their agree- ments with the committee at the end of the year, the panel for 1915 would remain the same as for 1914, with one exception, viz., Mr. C. J. Chamberlain, Church-stre‘t (removed).</p>
        <p>Changing Their Doctor. It was reported to the Insurance Committee on Thursday night that 268 persons had given notice of their intention to change doctors at the end of the year, also that two doctors had asked for seven persons to be removed from their lists at the end of the year. These per- sons, .the report added; would be supplied with new medical cards to enable them to make fresh choice of doctor as from the Ist January.</p>
        <p>Doctors Kept Waiting. The Finance Sub-committee reported to the Insurance Committee on Thursday night that they had instructed their Clerk to write the Insurance Commissioners protesting against their instructions to delay the payments to Panel doctors, and approved institutions, pend- ing the receipt of the record cards. The sub- committee also reported passing the following; resolution, which, with the foregoing, the com- mittee approved :—" That the Clerk write the Commissioners, asking when they intend'to re- mit the balance of the money due in respect of 1913, to enable a final settlement to be effected with the doctors."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>Musical Successes.</head>
        <p>Musical Successes.</p>
        <p>At a recent examination, held in Manchester, the following pupils of Miss Clarice Daly, Diploma of V.C.M. and Medallist, 70, The Causeway, passed successfully . Junior Grade.—Nora Williamson, Wildelspoo-- road, 92 marks, first class honours; Winifred Carter, Cairo-street, 90 marks, first class honours; Amy Jolley, ,St. Austin's-lane, 88 marks, first class honours. Primary Grade.—Doris Massey, Lower Walton, 96 marks, first class honours. Preparatory Grade.—Alice Higham, Thynne- street, 95 marks, first class honours; Lavinia Farrington, Arpley-street, 93 marks, first class honours.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. "Prayer; its use, its power, and its helP- fulness," was the title of the address which Mr. Willie Street gave on Sunday afternoon to the members of the Wycliffe P.S.A. Society. The speaker emphasised the fact that Christ prayed often, earnestly and pointedly, and urged the importance of following the Divine Example, pointing out that prayer could Lot be effectual unless it were offered in sincerity.—The Rev. T. T. Jenes, who-presided, referred to the pre- sence at the meeting of three members who had offered their services to King and country and were on furlough. They were Privates Gagan and Hilson, who have now gone back to their regiment, and Private Thornley,t who has yet some three weeks of his leave to expire.— During the afternoon Mr. Street sang "Un- answered 'yet !" and Mr. Payton played organ solos. The lesson was read by Mr. Yearsley, and the service closed with the sing- ing of the hymn "Abide with me."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>Dilatory Approved Societies.</head>
        <p>Dilatory Approved Societies.</p>
        <p>• At Thursday's meeting of the Warrington Insurance Committee the Medical Benefit Sub- , committee reported that at their last meeting the Clerk called attention to the delay on the part of many Approved Societies in notifying the committee of persons going out of benefit, ceasing to be insured, &amp;c. A large number of cases were instanced of persons who had been out of benefit for periods between 12 and 20 months, in respect of whom cancellation slips had only been received from Approved Socie- ties during the past few days. The majority of such persons had been on doctors' lists for a considerable length .of time. It was resolved that the Commissioners' attention be drawn tci the unreasonable delay on the part of many Approved Societies in notifying the committee of persons who have gone out of medical and sanatorium benefits, and that this committee suggests that, inasmuch as doctors and chem- ists are liable fore and do in many instances give, treatment to such persons (although out of benefit), the regulations should be amended SO as to enable the Cbmmissioners to penalise those Approved Societies who are proved to be negligent in this respect.—The committee ap- proved the subcommittee's recommendation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>Workers' Gift for Fellow-Workman.</head>
        <p>Workers' Gift for Fellow-Workman.</p>
        <p>There was an interesting little gathering of heads of departments in the time-office of Messrs. 'Whyman's Foundry on Saturday, the occasion being the making of a presentation to Mr. George Marshall, who is leaving to take up an appointment elsewhere in the town. The presentation, which took the form of a pipe in a case and a well-filled tobacco pouch, was made on behalf of the subscribers by Mr. E. Wil- liams, who said that during the nine and a halt years he had known Mr. Marshall he hhd always found him willing and ready to help in any good cause.—Mr. Marshall, who; by the way, is secretary to the Liberal Club Bowling Green Committee, suitably returned thanks, re- marking that whenever he smoked their pipe— which he hoped would, indeed, prove a pipe of peace—it would bring back to his memory pleas- ant recollections of the times he had spent in Whyman's Foundry.—Mr. William Mitchell, who acted as chairman over the brief, but pleasant, proceedings, also spoke, referring to the keen interest Mr. Marshall had taken in everything concerning tlie welfare if his fellow- -4131/Wits</p>
        <p>S.J.A.B. Hospital in France. The St. John Ambulance Brigade appeal for funds for the equipment of a brigade hospital in France. The sum of .227 18s. 6d. has already been received locally.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>A.O.F. Burial Society.</head>
        <p>A.O.F. Burial Society.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of memliers of the Ancient Order of Foresters' Burial Society will be held in the Roebuck Hotel, Bridge-street, on Tuesday, January 26th, at 7.30 p.m.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>Junior Liiierals.</head>
        <p>Junior Liiierals.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Warrington Junior Liberal Association and Club will take place in the Crosfield Memorial Hall on Tuesaay even- ing, when Mr. A. H. Crosfield will be present.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>Shorthand Writers.</head>
        <p>Shorthand Writers.</p>
        <p>Considerable progress is being made by the members of the Shorthand Writers' Association, there being a highly satisfactory increase in the membership. Miss Farrington and Miss Buckley had charge of the arrangements for the con- ducting of the speed class at the last meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>Messrs. Crosfield's Dividend.</head>
        <p>Messrs. Crosfield's Dividend.</p>
        <p>The transfer books of Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Limited, relative to the five per cent. cumulative pre-preference shares will be closed from the 18th to the .31st January (both days inclusive) for the purpose of preptiring and issuing dividend warrants due on February Ist.,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>Insurance Delegates' Expenses.</head>
        <p>Insurance Delegates' Expenses.</p>
        <p>The Insurance Commissioners recently wrote to the Insurance Committees of the country, suggesting a scale of expenses that should be paid to delegates attending meetings of the National Association of Insurance Committees, but at their meeting on Thursday night the Warrington Committee decided to adhere to their old acaleoviz., third-class railway fare and £1 is. per day.</p>
        <p>Impatient Patients. To the Insurance Committee on Thursday evening, Dr. Joseph reported on his visit, as Warrington's delegate, to a recent conference of the Association of Insurance Committees, and said that one of the recommendations put for- ward by the Medical Sub-section was that Ap- proved Societies should in all cases be informed, through the medical superintendent or other- wise, when patients left sanatoriums before their term of treatment had expired, so that suitable action might be taken, the idea 'being that Insurance Committees required some penal powers under which they cbula act in cases where insured persons left sanatoria before the time originally arranged.</p>
        <p>Bold-street Cripples' Guild. The annual Christmas tree and concert in connection with the above was held in the Bold- street Wesleyan Schoolroom on 'Wednesday evening, when about 160 cripples 1.% ere enter- tained to tea at 6.30 p.m., after which a very interesting programme was given by Mr. Rougl-isedge and friends from Stockton Heath, Miss Eva Tanner, and scholars of the Working Men's Mission, Bank Quay, the scholars of the Bold-street Wesleyan Sunday School (who were trained by Mis.s Gladys. Barlow), and Messrs. Scarisbrick and Bibby. At the close of the con- cert each cripple received a useful present from the Christmas tree, which had been kindly lent for the occasion by the Warrington Co-opera- tive Society, Limited. Thanks are due to the Secretary (Mrs. Booth) and the members of the committee for the manner in which the arrange- ments were carried out, and also to Mr. F. Hickman for presiding.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>Ebenezer Band of Hope.</head>
        <p>Ebenezer Band of Hope.</p>
        <p>A successful Band of Hope entertainment was held in connection with the Ebenezer Church, Buttermarket-street, on Monday evening, the chair being taken by Mr. A. E. Reece. The Band of Hope Choir rendered choruses, the Misses Rose Hasler, Beatrice Houghton' and Ida Shaw, and Master George Hasler recited, whilst Mrs. Cook and Mr. W. Ward contributed songs. Features of the programme were two dialogues, "A Funny Mistake" and "The Con- quest of Flowers," Miss Lily Ribbert appearing as a fairy in the last named. The action song, "There's an Isle 'Mid the Silver Sea," proved a popular item, and much amusement was caused by Miss Lilian Shaw's song "I shan't want to go to school any more," in which she hail the assistance of a numerous chorus. One of the items rendered by the choir was the Rus- sian National Anthem, which, together with "God save the King," brought the programme to a close.</p>
        <p>-4,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>Belgian Architecture.</head>
        <p>Belgian Architecture.</p>
        <p>The architecture of Belgium formed the sub- ject of the third lecture of the session at the School of Art, given by Mr. C. Mawson on , Tuesday evening. The lecture was illustrated by lantern slides depicting the architectural features of the chief buildings in the principal towns—Antwerp, Brussels Bruges, Ghent, Ypres, Louvain, Tournai, and Courtrai. The lecturer showed .the varying characteristics in the designs Of the buildings, resulting from the political changes through which the country has passed, and the influences brought to hear during its history, from the times of Ancient Rome. The character of the buildings—civic, ecclesiastical and domestic—showed evidences of the country's relations with France in Gothic times, and with Germany and Spain in the Renaissance period. Quite unique, as viewed through English eyes, were the picturesque roofs and towers and important belfries that were continually being met with in Belgian architecture; many of them suggestive of Vene- tian or Italian derivation.</p>
        <p>Warrington Agricultural Society. At the annual meeting of the Warrington and District Agricultural Society, to be held in the Masonic Rooms, Bold-street, next Wednesday afternoon, the financial statement will, among other matters, be brought before the members. It slows that the past year's receipts totalled £476 Bs. 10d., of which £233 1k came from subscriptions, £B9 18s. 6d. from entrance fees, and £63 11s. 6d. from admission charges, to which may be added a further £l7 2s. for ad- mission charges to the grand-stand. The pay- ments totalled £7lB 48. 5d., the largest item being one of £452 for prizes. Fitting, up the showyard cost •£93 18s. 3d., printing and adver- tising £95 9s. 10d., and judges' fees and salaries £5l 17s. 6d. There is, therefore, a debit bal- ance on the year's working of £241 15s. 7d., which has had to be met out of the general balance fund, made up of a balance in hand from 1913 of £56 155., and a special donation of £2OO from Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart. The general balance has, therefore, been reduced to one of £l4 19s. sd. The principal business on the agenda for next Wednesdays meeting is the reception of the accounts and the Council's annual report, and the election of a President and Vice-president for the ensuing year. • • •"'</p>
        <p>Local Leather Trade In Full Swing. "Trade," says the Warrington correspondent of the "Leather Trades Review," "appears to be in full swing again, and numerous buyers were in force last week without, however, greatly affecting the net result, as there was only slight evidence of any accumulation of leather. Naturally, business was chiefly confined to goods suitable for Government orders, and good quan- tities were booked, buyers generally contriving to keep their contracts constant. This works out well for both sides, as it checks excessive fluctuations of prices and the producer has no rear of stock. The home trade seems quiet, judging by the inquiries received, and although there is a strong demand for the lighter run of bends they are practically all going out for purposes other than boots. The prices of leather have lately shown little change; certain classes in particular demand, and therefore schrcer than others, have made advances, but generally the position is unchanged; there is no expectation in any quarter of any weakening while the war lasts, and buyers as a whole make very little complaint about the high prices, I -recognising that under the circumstances they cannot well be otherwise. Of more vital import at the moment is the possibility of their being higher, and the increased pressure on the market caused by the new Government orders, together with the quick advances being made on all classes of suitable hides, are indications that this is an early probability. Locally, everyone seems very busy and turning out leather as quickly and —abundantly as poesible, the distribution of which is greatly hindered by the restricted and 'uncertain railway facili- Ulm".</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>TO-DAY'S MATCH</head>
        <p>TO-DAY'S MATCH</p>
        <p>WILL BE PLAYED AT</p>
        <p>WILDERSPOOL.</p>
        <p>The rumour in town last night that the match Warrington v. Leeds would not be played is ill- founded.</p>
        <p>The water on the Warrington ground will be pumped off by the Fire Brigade this morning, and so the turf will be made fit for play.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>Red Cross Lectures.</head>
        <p>Red Cross Lectures.</p>
        <p>In connection with the British Red hpross Society, lectures on nursing will be given in the Parr Hall every Thursday at 6 p.m., com- mencing on January 28th. Lectures on first aid will be given at 8 p.m. on the same nights, and the fee for either course of lectures is 2s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>Photographic Society.</head>
        <p>Photographic Society.</p>
        <p>With the aid of 140 beautiful lantern pictures Mr. Callon, of Widnes, lectured to the members of the Warrington Photographic Society, this week, on "Winter in, the Engadine." His photographs of the gloriotts scenery in -anti, around San Maritz and his clever snap-shots of the winter sports Were a source of great delight, and at the close of his chatty lecture Mr.. Callon was accorded a hearty vote of thanks, on the proposition of Mr. Fox, seconded' by: Mr. Bishop.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>Stole Picture Palace Uniforms.</head>
        <p>Stole Picture Palace Uniforms.</p>
        <p>At Saturday's Borough Police Court John Wal- lace (lodging-housg) was charged with stealing four coats, valued'at is., the property of Mr. Griffin, of the Central Picture Palace.—lt was stated that the prisoner was employed in cart- ing goods from the Pidure Palace to Mr. Griffin's house. Ile took the uniforms and sold them for threepence.—Prisoner said a man at the Picture Palace gave them to him. He had been in a Bible Class for eight years, and had not done anything wrong to anyone.—A fine of 78. was impoged. '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>IVarrington Musical Society.</head>
        <p>IVarrington Musical Society.</p>
        <p>It will be remembered that the Musical Society's first concert of the season—" Elijah," given in the Parish Church—resulted in the 'sub- stantial sum of £29 odd being realised for the benefit of the new ward in the Infirmary for soldiers wounded in the war. Practices of vocal and orchestral members have been resumed this week with a view to a further concert, this time in Parr Hall, and again, we understand, for the laudable purpose of benefiting one or other of the local war funds. The forthcoming con- cert will, we are informed, be of a miscellaneous character, and among the principal artistes who will take part in the programme it is possible we shall make the acquaintance of a Belgian solo 'cellist of high rank in the profession. Needless to say, we wish the Musical Society all success in its efforts to cater for our edifica- tion and amusement and, incidentally, to assist deserving movements to help the helpless.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>1, The Mayor Opens Municipal Rifle</head>
        <p>1, The Mayor Opens Municipal Rifle</p>
        <p>-Range. , A rifle range, 50 yards long, which has been made At a 'cost of £lOO, alongside the baths in Legh-street, was formally opened by the Mayor on Thursday, his Worship and Alderman Bolton (chairman of the Baths Committee) both being credited with having scored a eye, although the targets looked suspiciously like having been pierced by a blackhead pencil. The range is for the use of the • Preparatory Training Corps and kindred organisations.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>Two _Tragedies.</head>
        <p>Two _Tragedies.</p>
        <p>At an inquest held on he body of Ann. Ma- rshall, 58, wife of Joseph Marshall, lock keeper, Lock Villa, Grappenhall, a verdict of "Acci- dental death" was returned. Mrs. Marshall, when in the Royal Oak Hotel with her hus- band, opened the cellar door in mistake for the kitchen door, and fell down the steps, .and Dr. Sutherland Said in all Probability her neck was broken.</p>
        <p>"Found drowned" was the verdict at the in- quest on the body of George Henry Ainsworth, aged 22, of 56, Fox-street, recovered from the reservoir at Longford Wireworks, where he was employed as a labourer. He is supposed to have slipped into the reservoir on a rough and windy night.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>LOCAL DEATHS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL DEATHS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Robert Brandieth.</p>
        <p>An old and respected tradesman, Well known in Warrington, has passed away„in Mr. Robert Brandreth, who died on .Januafy 7th, at his residence, Padgate-lane. Mr. Brandreth fol- lowed the business of a grocer, in Buttermarket- street, and afterwards at 49, Bridge-street. He was apprenticed as a grocer in early life and spent his early years in the employ of Mr. Thomas Gorst, Buttermarket-street, afterwards establishing himself in the business of his choice. lie had reached his 67th year: The funeral took place at the Warrington Cemetery op Monday and was conducted by the Rev. G. Vale-Owen. The funeral arrangements were carried out by Messrs. J. Mounfield and Sons.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>Mrs. Elizabeth Norman.</head>
        <p>Mrs. Elizabeth Norman.</p>
        <p>Many Warringtonians will regret to hear of the death of Mrs. Elizabeth Norman, widow of the late 'Mr. Ralph Norman, ,universally known in Warrington as the Inspetctor of Nuisances. After some years of depressed health and suf- fering, Mrs. Norman passed away on Sunday at her residence, Springdale, Latchford Without, in her 67th year. The funeral took place at the cemetery on Wednesday. A service,was held at Christ 'Church, Latchford, and was conducted by the Rev. G. C. Briggs. The funeral arrange- ments were carried out by Messrs. J. Mounfield and Sons.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>Mr. Ernest Waring.</head>
        <p>Mr. Ernest Waring.</p>
        <p>On Thursday afternoon the funeral took place at the Warrington Cemetery of Mr. Ernest Waring, late of Warrington, who died at his new home, Abbey-road, Rhyl, on Sunday, in his 37th year. The family were represented by his three brothers—Mr. Walter Waring, Mr. Rebind Waring and Mr. -Reginald Waring—and Mr. G. S. Millington and Mr. W. Nevins, brothers-in-law. The body was carried- to the grave by his old comrades, the members of the Wesleyan and General Assurance Society, by whom he was held in high esteem—Mr. A. Bray- shaw, Mr. J. Sherlock, Mr. F. Monks, Mr. Mc- Kinley and Mr. Harding. Mr. M. Wilcock also assisted as a carrier.</p>
        <p>Councillor R. Henshall conducted the service in the chapel and at the graveside, and the large number of friends who assembled was eloquent token of the esteem and affection in which he was held. _ _</p>
        <p>The funeral arrangements were in the hands of Messrs. J. Mounfield and Sons.</p>
        <p>Mrs. 13rocklehurst.</p>
        <p>It is with regret that we have this week to chronicle the death of Mrs. Esther Brockle- hurst, widow of the late Mr. Thomas Brockle- burst, which sad event occurred at the residence of her only son, Mr. Samuel Brocklehurst, the local manager for the Prudential Insurance Company, Moss House, Whitefield-road, Stock- ton Heath. The deceased lady, who was 81 years of age, appeared in her usual health or Wed- nesday morning, when she suddenly complained of pain in the head, died a few hours later from cerebral hemorrhage. She leaves a son and three daughters—Mrs. George Taylor (Black- burn), Mrs. Luxton (Oldham) and Mrs. Hartell (who is in Cad) to mourn their loss. Up to the day of her death the deceased .lady busied herself in knitting comforts—mittens and socks —for the soldiers at the Front. At one time the deceased was actively identified with religious work in the Winwick-road district, she and her husband being. mainly responsible for the open- ing of the old Gospel Hall in Foster-street. At their meeting on Thursday night the In- surance Committee passed a vote of condolence with the deceased's lady's son, Mr. Samuel Brocklehurst, who is chairman of the Finance Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH</p>
        <p>ELECTRIC LIGHTING.</p>
        <p>" GRFEVANCES" REVIVED.</p>
        <p>Last week the Parish Council of Stockton Heath resolved to support the Order under which the Warrington Corporation are propos- ing to supply the village with electricity, but the old "grievances" regarding the extra char's fot tram fares and gas were revived. It is only fair that our Stockton Heath friends should remember that there are two sides to these questions. One Member said that "if the Corporation were allowed to supply electricity to Stockton Heath they ought to renuce the extra + charges for gas and trams." The ratepayers of Warrington paid over £lO,OOO for widening the roads and constructing the light railway, and Stockton Heath, of course, did not contribute anything towards it. When the Corporation bought the gas under- taking in 1877 the net price charged to Stockton Heath was 4s. per 1,000 cubic feet. It was upon that basis of charges that the Corporation had to purchase; and it may be profitable to reflect what the price might have been had the works not been taken over by the Corporation. Stock- ton Heath pays now, as it paid in the olden days, qatra for the extra cost oF mains to an outlying district, but Stockton Heath pays only 25., 6d. per 'l,OOO cubic feet.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>FRIENDLY SOCIETIES.</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY SOCIETIES.</p>
        <p>dent Order of Oddfellows</p>
        <p>(.tJ.)</p>
        <p>MR. A E. SPANN MADE G.M.</p>
        <p>At the annual meeting of the Independent Order of Oddfellows (Manchester Unity), held last Saturday at the Peliian Hotel, Butter- marliet-street, Warrington, 'there being a full attendance of delegates, Presided over by Prov. G.M. Harry Lawrence, Prov. 'D.G.3,1: A. E. Spann was elected the Prey. Grand Master for the ensuing year, and P.G. Wm'. E. Clare, secretary of the "Haven of Rest" Lodge, was elected the Prov. D.G.M. It was unanimously agreed that a P.P.G.M.'S Gold Jewel, together with a P.P.G.ll.'s Certifi- cate, be presented to Bro. Harry leawyence in recognition of his services as Grand Master dur- ing the past year. The new Grand Master received 'the hearty congratulatioujm. of the delegdtes, wit:i the hope that his year of office might be a successful one. Bro. Spann, in reply,. said he highly appreci- ated the honourable position in which the brethren had placed him, and he trusted that during his tenure of office the lodges and the dis- trict generally 'would continue to prosper.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>OTHER BUSINESS.</head>
        <p>OTHER BUSINESS.</p>
        <p>Other .elections were Auditor, P.G. Walter Dennett; lodge book examiners, Bros. S. 7ones and B. Clough; county conference representa- tives, Bros. Lawrence, Spann, Webster, White- legg and Higham; examining and relieving officer and Grand Tyler, Bro. Massey. The accounts for the past year were sub- mitted by the District Corresponding Secre- tary, and showed a gain upon the year's work- ing.</p>
        <p>A donation Of £lO was made to the Warring- ton Infirmary special war fund. •</p>
        <p>THE NEW GRAND MASTER.</p>
        <p>- Bro. A. E. Spann is an enthusiast in every- thing he undertakes, and, his Tapid promotion in Oddrellowship is quite natural in the circum- stances. He is well known as the local secre- tary of the St. John Ambulance Brigade, in which he has been as active and zealous as in the cause of Oddfellowship. Having been initiated in .7une, 1897, as a member of- the LoyiT " Constitution" Lodge, he was quickly marked out for office, and passed</p>
        <p>through the different chairs with conspicuous ability. Bro. Spann has also acted- as Lodge Auditor, District Auditor, Lodge Book Exam- iner, and has served upon all the important committees of his lodge and the district. In 1909 he was honoured by being the representa- tive to the A.M.C. at Bradford, and has upon many occasions been the representative to the Lancashire and Adjacent Districts Conference. In connection with the Widows' and Orphans' Fund, Bro. Spann has acted as president. ;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>SHEPHERDS' QUARTERLY</head>
        <p>SHEPHERDS' QUARTERLY</p>
        <p>- MEETING.</p>
        <p>The quarterly meeting of the Warrington District of the Loyal Order of Ancient Shep- herds (A.U.) was held on Saturday at the head- quarters of the "Sons of Mersey" Lodge, Cen- tral Hotel, Warrington. The Prov. Chief Shep- herd (Bro. F. Taylor )presided,, and was sup- ported by the Deputy, P.C. Bro. H. Johnson, P.P.C.S. W. E. Clegg, P.P.C. Jas. Parr, Dis. Treasurer; P.P.C.S. P. L. Edgar, Dis Secretary; and delegates from the various lodges. Bro. John Ratcliffe, "Late Spring Rose" Lodge, was re-elected auditor for six months. The Secretary presented the statement' of ac- counts of the lodges and ale district for the year, which was accepted. The meeting noted with pleasure a consider- able increase in the District Funeral Fund, and also that the combined worth of the lodges had increased, ,in spite of a rather heavy year of sickness.</p>
        <p>P.P.C.S. W. E. Clegg, "Late Spring Rose" Lodge, and P.M. Robert Roughsedge "Thorn Tree" Lodge, were unanimously elected trus- tees of the district.</p>
        <p>The district officers, with Bros. Hartley, Lowe, Massey and Hough, were elected a sub-com- mittee for carrying out the duties attaching to the Convalescent Fund for the year 1915.</p>
        <p>An application on behalf of a distressed brother for a grant from the subsidiary fund was unanimously agreed to. The District Examiners (Bros. J. gr. Porn- frey and Jas. Lowe) reported that they had com- pleted their examinaticin of the books of the lodges and district and found them in order and to their satisfaction. ,The report was accepted, and a vote of thanks passed to the Examiners for their services. Bro. J. W. Pomfrey gave a very interesting report of the proceedings at the Adjourned A.M.C., held in London in October. The minutes of the Juvenile ,Section Were con- firmed, and the meeting expressed itrsatisfac- tion at the continued success of this section, nearly 200 members having been initiated into membership of the Order. A resolution was passed agreeing ythat the society join with other local Friendly Societies to welcome the Druids on the occasion of their visit to Warrington next Whit-week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>A.O.F. DISTRICT MEETING.</head>
        <p>A.O.F. DISTRICT MEETING.</p>
        <p>The 212th quarterly district meeting of the Ancient Order of Foresters was held on Satur- day'at the Masons' Arms, Mill-street, Warring- ' ton, there being present the D.C.R., (Councillor J. Clirimes, in the chair), the D.S.C.R. (E. R. Hollins, in the vice-chair), the District Trea- surer (his Worship the Mayor), the District Secretary (P.D.C.R. R. Thornley), the District Trustees (P.D.C.R. J. Walsh and P.D.C.R. H. James, sen.), and the following delegates repre- senting the various Courts of the district:— Court 138, P.D.C.R. W. Hipkiss and P.C.R. A. Morris; Court 240, P.C.R. G. Allen, P.C:R. J. pavies and P.D.C.R. G. W. R. Baines; Court 430, P.D.C.R. T. Roden, P.C.R. E. Clayton, and P.C.R. H. James, jun.; Court 431, P.C.R. J. Smith, and P.C.R. T. Crosley ; Court 461, P.C.R. J. Gatley ; Court 475, P.D.C.R. J. Grayson; Court 9,335, P.C.R. Sister R. Baines and Sister W. Knowles; assisted by P.D.C.R. G. Maybury, P.D.C.R. S. B. Healey, Sister A. Corradine, P.C.R. J. Jenns, and P.C.R. Wilding. The returns of members and the cash account were submitted and passed, and the Auditors (Bro. J. Davies, and Sister A. Corradine) re- ported having gone carefully through the dis- trict books, and finding everything in order. The matter of the Warrington- Infirmary War 'Fund was considered, ii.nd it was unanimously decided to grant £2 2s. to this worthy object. The D.C.R. then closed the meeting with the usual ritual.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>WAR STORIES: BY LETTER' AND INTERVIEW.</head>
        <p>WAR STORIES: BY LETTER' AND INTERVIEW.</p>
        <p>TALES FROM THE TRENCHES.</p>
        <p>Wounded in the Infirmary Recount</p>
        <p>Experiences.</p>
        <p>A. further batch of wounded. British soldiers arrived at the Infirmary on Friday afternoon of last week. They are comfortably quartered in the ward specially set apart for military patients, and everything is being done by the doctors and nurses not only, to aid them in making rapid and satisfactory recoveries, but also to give them the best possible, time whilst their hurts necessitate their detention in the institution. Despite their past and present sufferings the twenty-five " Tommies " are a wonderfully cheery band, and they are intensely appreciative of what is being done for them, and, of the little privileges and presents which they from time to time receive. They are allowed to smoke, and in that fact lies a hint that anybody, desirous of paying off a little bit of the debt he owes to these gallant lads, may take. They are also fond of reading, and here again is opened up a means by which some of our gratitude may be shown. An " ExaMiner " man dropped in for a chat with the wounded men the other afternoon and found that they had many interesting stories to tell.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>HAD HIS FOOT SHOT OFF.</head>
        <p>HAD HIS FOOT SHOT OFF.</p>
        <p>Private Coote, of the Northumberland Fusi- liers, went out on October 7th, joined the firing line on October 23rd, was wounded on Novem- ber 7th, and after having been six weeks in a French. Hospital, is back in England again. When he first entered the trench the next man to him had his foot shot off.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>SAW TIIE KING IN FRANCE.</head>
        <p>SAW TIIE KING IN FRANCE.</p>
        <p>Private W. King, of the Second Royal Sussei Regiment, said that all was, quiet during Christ- mas. He had seen the King, and he declared that the rousing reception His Majesty got from the men would ever live in his memory.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>"HELL ON EARTH." Lance-Corporal's Description of Shell- Fire.</head>
        <p>"HELL ON EARTH." Lance-Corporal's Description of Shell- Fire.</p>
        <p>Lance-Corporal Archer, of the Norfolk Regi- ment, said that the English had to be careful how they gave messages, because so many of the Germans understood English through having' worked here, and through it being largely taught in their day schools. Describing his experiences under shell fire, Archer said, "It is like hell on earth." He was in the trenches for 32 days. at a stretch, and had seen the good 'work done by Indian troops, who were, he declared, " hot'stuff,"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>MET BETWEEN THE TRENCHES. Infantryman's Encounter With A German.</head>
        <p>MET BETWEEN THE TRENCHES. Infantryman's Encounter With A German.</p>
        <p>Lance-Corporal Curtis, of the Somerset Light Infantry, also spoke of the Christmas friend- ship with the enemy, and said that he himself had met a Gerznan half-way between the trenches. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Speaking of the actual fighting, he said that the Gerraan snipers were very good. "If you put your face to a loop hole," he concluded, "you were nearly sure to get hit."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>FRATERNISED WITH THE GERMANS. ,</head>
        <p>FRATERNISED WITH THE GERMANS. ,</p>
        <p>Enemy's Christmas Boxes for</p>
        <p>"Tommy."</p>
        <p>Private Wall, of the First Devons, who was "Out for Christmas," said: "We had a very friendly, time all the week from the Mrd to tfie 29th. We fraternised with the Germans and found them decent fellows. We never fired a shot on either side and men left the trenches in the daylight, and had fires at night. One night the Germans called on us not to fire, and asked us ,to go across for chocolate. We would not go at night, but went 0 the morning and got cigars, chocolates, 8.;c:. German Mourners at Victim's Funeral.</p>
        <p>"One of our chaps cut a German's hair, and when we were burying one of our lads a German officer and private attended and went solemnly through the service."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>SOMETHING LIKE A "BAG."</head>
        <p>SOMETHING LIKE A "BAG."</p>
        <p>Enemy Aeroplane Brought Down By</p>
        <p>Rifle Fire.</p>
        <p>The deeds of valour of the Royal Horse Guards—the famous “Blues"—are fairly well known by now, but Trooper A. Rudduck gave a detailed account of the actions in which they have figured to our representative. He has kept an interesting little diary, and the first date worthy of special mention is October 14th, "when," writes Trooper Rudduck, "we fetched a German aeroplane down by rile fire. The officer in charge escaped, but he was captured later in the afternoon."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>Buried By Explodirig Shell.</head>
        <p>Buried By Explodirig Shell.</p>
        <p>• One of the most notable engagements in which "The Blues" figured was when 75,000 Germans were sent. back, and the estimated losses were 20,000 Germans and .500 British. "It was a hard fight," commented the Trooper, "and we were called upon to save the Second Queen's, which we did. When in the trenches we were ,shelled out by 'Jack Johnsons.' Along with four others I was buried when. one of the shells exploded near us, but we were got out." Under the heading "The Valley of Death" in the diary were several entries, and describing the encounter to which the Tennysonian description had been applied, Trooper Rudduck said: "My squadron were moved out of our trenches. We had to go over the sky line in front of the trenches to draw the enemy's fire. We ,were within 50 yards of the Germans, and' I can tell you it was 'warm work.' We lost 97 horses in less than ten minutes, but there was only one man killed and seven wounded. Our ' draw ' was successful, and the Scottish regi- ment behind took the guns and many prisoners. In the morning we were praised by the General Commanding. Whilst we were escorting the Battery,‘,R.H.A., we were followed by three German aeroplanes. It was the night of Novem- ber Ist before we could get into position, and soon afterwards the aeroplanes were brought down."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>An Historic Bayonet Charge.</head>
        <p>An Historic Bayonet Charge.</p>
        <p>"On November 6th," reads another entry in the diary, "we took part in the first bayonet charge by cavalry that has happened in history. We were awaiting orders in a wood, when about 200 of us were called upon to relieve a position. We galloped 'like mad,' and then got orders to dismount and fix bayonets.' We had to run 300 yards in the Open. We were nearly cut off, when the Cheshire Regiment came up"and saved US."</p>
        <p>"I was wet through for fen days, and did not have a dry change all the time:" was how the Trooper described one of the hardships he had endured. "This is my eighth hospital in, 14 days." Producing a round fyullet, the Trooper said: "That is a shrapnel bullet which went through my great coat, jacket, waist belt, riding pants, and through two shirts, and then stopped next to the flesh, only causing a lump about the size of an egg." There is another thing in addition to the bullet which the trooper treasures as a souvenir, and that is the time-fuse oft a Ger- man shell—one of the variety which Mr. Atkins has dubbed "Little Willies."' The fuse is' excellently made in brass, iron and lead, and is inscribed with several figures and marks indicating distances and times at which the shell may be set to explode. Oyer the figures are the marks "DOPP. Z. 96 n. 11. • . Sb. 344."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>LIFE LOST FOR A PIECE OF CAKE. Tragedy of a Christmas Parcel.</head>
        <p>LIFE LOST FOR A PIECE OF CAKE. Tragedy of a Christmas Parcel.</p>
        <p>Private Cliffe, of the Second King's Own Yorkshire Light Infantry, who is at present suffering from frost-bite, was previously wounded at Le Basses was seven weeks in a base hospital. "One of the worst sights I saw," declared Private Cliffe, "during the last three days I was out, viz., December 19th, 20th and 21st, was when a young man who had received •a parcel from home asked a friend of his if he would come over' and have a bit of Christmas cake. The other man got the cake, but as he was coming back a ' pal ' ,spoke to him. Tne man with the cake happened to put his head up above the trench and was hit with a 'dum- dum' bullet, which took half his head off."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>Gramophone Playing In German Trench.</head>
        <p>Gramophone Playing In German Trench.</p>
        <p>Describing one of his engagements, Private Cliffe said: "We could see the Germans digging a trench, and we kept sniping at them and poppinethem off.' The results were signalled by means of a shovel. Just like a 'wash-out' at home. We were only about 25 yards from the German trench and the occupants had a gramophone going. Our artillery played havoc with them, but they kept the gramophone going. The artillery kept on clearing them out, but, owing to the state of the ground, we were unable to do anything in the way of a charge."</p>
        <p>British Aviators Capture German Flying-man. "What do you think of the Flying Corps?" our representative asked. "They are doing very powerful work," was the answer. "On the 23rd December I was in tain place," slyly observed our informant, "and I saw a German aeroplane overnead. Four English machines chased it and were successful in capturing it. Our machines are very useful in finding positions for the artillery. The Ger- man machines are nowherck compared with ours.</p>
        <p>"The big 9.2 guns _which have been sent out recently have improved the Allies' position wonderfully," Private Cliffe added. "They do a lot of damage as regards shelling German transports in the roads. I think there is no doubt but that the Germans are getting short of ammunition, for when I was:in Le Bassee they fired 315 shells in one day,44but during my last three days there were only ten shells fired. When they come we laughingly remark: An- other rocket gone,.Mary !" Anxious to See -Kitchener's Army. _</p>
        <p>"We have ,heard a lot about Kit-chener's Army," concluded the cheery, private, "and nothing will please' the men who have been out there more than to see the men who have been talked about so much, and see also what they can do. . . We live very well and get plenty of food and smoking materials. We are also able to cook anything in the trenches."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>"A QUIET CHRISTMAS."</head>
        <p>"A QUIET CHRISTMAS."</p>
        <p>NA liiteeross_ Employe Writes Home</p>
        <p>From-the- Front.</p>
        <p>In one of his recent letters home, Driver Wheeler Fred Jones, formerly employed at Whitecross Works and now, of the 4th Company of the 'Army Service Corps, attached to the 18th Infantry Brigade, which is serving with the Expeditionary Force, expresses the hope that all his friends in Warrington are helping well. He acknowledges Christmas cards and apologises that he has not got one to sen.l in return. "I had a quiet Christmas," he wr;tes, "bat I had a good dinner and everything went down all right We have been at couple of months now, but, our troops having adyaniced, we are about eight miles' behind the firing line—the farthest we have been since we have' been out. 'I wish you all a happy New Year, and hope that I shall he with you by next Christmas. I also wish you all good luck, for I don't know when my time will end."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>SAW EMDEN PRISONERS.</head>
        <p>SAW EMDEN PRISONERS.</p>
        <p>Vtrarringtonian Aboard Mine-Sweeper Explains His Occupation.</p>
        <p>Mr. Will Pacey, of 38, Mill-lane, Warrington, a member of Messrs. J. Crosfield and Sons' Ambulance Brigade, was on the Royal Navy Sick Berth Reserve before the outbreak of the war and was Called up to service on August 2nd. On the R.M.F. Mooltan he then proceeded to Australia, where his company was to join a hospital ship; and it is interesting to note that another Warringtonian—Mr. F. R. Wallington— was also with them.</p>
        <p>Plans, however, were altered upon arrival at Australia, and on December 17th they returned to the Royal Naval Barracks, Devonport, where they remained until Christmas Day. At five o'clock upon that morning, Mr. Pacey was transferred to H.M.S. Lynn—which is a mine- sweeper—and upon this vessel he remained until Sunday morning, when he returned to War- rington for a ten days respite. The time of those on board the vessel, Mr. 'Pacey informed us, is fully occupied; for not only ate the men engaged on mine-sweeping-- which is a most tedious and dangerous occupa- tion—but there are, besides, never less than half a dozen sick or injured sailors on board—to whose care Mr. Pacey, with others, devotes himself; medical outfits being provided by the Govern- ment.</p>
        <p>Method of Mine-sweeping. As to the methods of mine-sweeping itself, the operationsare carried on by means of cables, which "catch" the mines and drag them along to the surface of the water. The mines are then fired at with guns or rifles, the shells or bullets exploding them—after which they sink. Of course, the mine-sweeping vessels—which, by the way, are trawlers—keep a safe distance away whilst these explosions are being caused! Day after day these operations continue; from which fact it can,readily be understood that the work—in which the greatest care has to be taken, on account of the awful risk—is most exacting. To the credit of the men it can be said that they are quite cheery and contented in their work.</p>
        <p>The crew of`tht+ 'Lynn numbers about 45—and all are amply provided for—having, in addition to excellent rations, warra winter clothing. Each man on board is supplied with everything necessary in case of accident; including rubber life-belts, which the men wear around their necks as they sleep. These belts can be in- flated, at a moment's notice, with the mouth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>Escorted by H.M.A.S. Sydney.</head>
        <p>Escorted by H.M.A.S. Sydney.</p>
        <p>Mr. Pacey's actual war experience;, however, have not been confined to the mine-sweeper, for on his return from Sydney (Australia), the troopship Militades—on which he was— brought home also the English Reserves, all of whom were highly elated at the ,prospect of serving the Motherland and the Allies! As the ship left the Torpoint landing-stage (Sydney harbour), the reserves kept up a continual round of songs, including " Xipperary " and "Old soldiers never die; they simply fade away "—the latter to the tune of the well-known hymn "Kind words." These songs roused everybody to a high pitch of enthusiasm. The Miltiades was escorted by several cruisers, and among these was the famous H.M.A.S. Sydney. This ship they one day noticed steam- ing away at full speed; but none knew whither she was bound, and much conjecture was in- dulged in among the voyagers. Soon after— with great and general rejoicing—they received the news that she had captured the notoriOns Emden, whose crew had been placed—prisoners —on the Orvieto and. the Omreh. These-ships passed quite close to the Miltiades, and some of the " boys " even obtained the autographs of the captured German captain and his officers. • • • . Altogether, that return journey proved quite an exciting time!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>Tribute to "The Examiner."</head>
        <p>Tribute to "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Mr. Pacey concluded by remarking that he would not like to let that occasion pass without paying his tribute to his favourite paper: "The Examiner." Each week, wherever he was, it had been sent to him—thus becoming such a valuable connecting-link between him, and the good old town that he would not now like to miss it on any account!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>SAVED BY HIS BANDOLIER. Warrington Trooper Tells of a Close</head>
        <p>SAVED BY HIS BANDOLIER. Warrington Trooper Tells of a Close</p>
        <p>Call.</p>
        <p>• Trooper Fred Kershaw, 3rd Dragoon Guards, who, before joining the colours, was in the office of Messrs. Rylands riros.' has sent a stir- ring letter to his mother, 66, Willis-street. In it he says that a comrade going on leave would post it upon his arrival in England, slid goes on to llescribe his experiences in the trenches ift the vicinity of Ypres. "We went to the trenches at night," he writes, and, as soon as we got in them, the Germans started. It was murder! Shrapnel bullets and 'Jack Johnsons were flying about, and next day their snipers got on the go and killed a few more of our men. We had two Maxims at work, seven men serv- ing with each.</p>
        <p>Only One of a Gun's Crew Escapes. 1 "The officer on my 'gun' was killed, and the men at the other 'gun' fared worse than we did, .only one escaping scot free, three of the others being killed and three wounded. My party went back, weary and worn, for rest the second night, and the next time we were sent to the trenches. I am glad to say we did not lose so many men; in fact, we 'went up about six times and only lost half a dozen men. Then we were sent to :the other side of Ypres —or 'what was left of Ypres, for the Germans had largely blown the place away. At this time everywhere was about a couple of feet in snow When we had got established in our new position the Germans charged our trenches. The Maxim gun was in the rear. The 'C' 'Squadron called for assistance, and the officer in charge of us asked for volunteers. About three dozen of us went. We had to run through a ditch about three hundred yards long and knee-deep in water. The end opened out into a turnip field. I was doubling along, when, all at once, I felt something splash in my face and, looking down, I saw that a pocket in n.- bandolier had been shattered by a bullet, the ten rinds in it saving my life, as. the pod; in question was just.over the heart.</p>
        <p>An 111-starred German Charge. "I lay still for a minute and thanked God for saving ray life, and then I crawled along on my stomach for about threkt hundred yards. All the While the Germans were 'sweeping with shrapnel,' pieces flytng all round ime. When reventually got to'C' Squadron's trenches the Germans were making a final charge. We let them get to within fifty .yards—right up to the barbed wire entanglements—and then we, and the Irish Guards, who were with us, let drive, mowing them down like hay. They retped pell mell, and all around us lay dead and Wounded. After we had buried the dead we were re- lieved."</p>
        <p>"I might remark," the writer concludes, "that I helped to bury young Smith. He had been shot through the head by a sniper; whilst poor Sid Taylor was killed by shrapnel. . . . I have just had a look at the leave roll, and I am not due to come home till March sth. It won't be long now !" _ _</p>
        <p>The deaths- of Messrs. Smith and Taylor, Warrington men, mentioned by the writer, have been recorded in "The Examiner."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>OUR SOLDIERS WANT MORE ZAM-BUK Remarkable Letters from the Trenches.</head>
        <p>OUR SOLDIERS WANT MORE ZAM-BUK Remarkable Letters from the Trenches.</p>
        <p>Letters received almost daily from the firing line in France and Belgium prove how indis- pensable Zam-Buk is to our gallant soldiers, not only for mending the cuts and bruises incidental to the hard life in the trenches, but also for healing their sore feet after long marches, and preventing frost-bite and rheumatism.</p>
        <p>Lance-Corporal J. P. Delaney, a Distinguished Conduct Medalist, of the 2nd Royal Irish Regt., in a letter dated November 24th, says:—" A box of Zam-Buk out here is like a loaf of bread to a starving man. On the retreat from Mons we had a forced march of 37 miles, and not a man who used 2am-Buk on his feet fell out. I can safely recommend Zam-Buk to everyone as being the best healer on the market."</p>
        <p>Private E. Westfield, No. 9896, " C " Company, 3rd Worcesters, which regiment Sir John French, singled out for special praise, writes from the trenches on Dec. 7th :—" I wish we had more Zain-Buk sent out from home—it would be much better if more Zam-Buk was sent instead of so much tobacco. We find Zam-Buk the best remedy for sore hands after trench-digging, and for frost-bite, cracks, and cold sores."</p>
        <p>Another Distinguished Conduct Medallist, Private A. Mutlow, 2nd South Staffs., writing froni "Somewhere in France" on Dec. sth, says:—" In my opinion every soldier should have a box of Zam-Buk in his kit when 'trek- king,' as Zam-Buk is the most valuable addition to his marching powers. I am a constant 'user of Zam-Buk for chafed legs (from which I suffer on long marches). I brought a supply out here from Aldershot, and it is in great demand amongst my comrades. Zam-Buk is also veil( effective for galled feet and blistered heels" Sergeant A. J. Earl, D.C.M., "C" Squadron, 15th (King's) Hussars, writing on Nov. 11th, says :—" Zam-Buk is so compact and keeps so well under the trying conditions of warfare that it is undoubtedly the best first-aid ' a soldier can carry with him. I brought four boxes of Zam-Buk with me when I left England‘ and they were soon used up by myself and iomrades, together with more boxes which they brought with them." .</p>
        <p>The above letters, which have all come through and been passed by the Censor, clearly show that our Soldiers urgently want more Zam-I3uk</p>
        <p>...;veryone of our readers, therefore cannot (To their soldier and sailor rriends a better service than by sending them gifts of one, two, or even three one and three-halfpenny boxes of Zam-Buk at once!</p>
        <p>.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>LIBERAL BODIES UNITE TO RAISE FUNDS.</head>
        <p>LIBERAL BODIES UNITE TO RAISE FUNDS.</p>
        <p>A combined meeting of the Women's Liberal Association, the Junior and Senior Liberal Associations, and the Progressive Labour League, was held in the Crosfield Memorial Hall last night, for "the purpose of organising and carrying into effect proposals for the benefit of our brave soldiers and sailors who are fighting for us."</p>
        <p>Mrs. Osborne presided and the good work which the ladies are doing in the way of making garments for the soldiers was enlarged uPon. Of course, funds are needed to provide the materials, and to raise "the necessary" it has • been decided to hold nine ward socials on the following dates: January 21st, 25th, 26th and 28th; and February Ist, 4th, Bth, 11th and 15th. A sum of £lOO has been raised already by sev- eral gentlemen, and the idea of the socials is to give everyone a chance of enjoying themselves, - and at the same time helping in providing various articles of clothing for the fighting men. The ladies have sent out 2,000 articles up to the present, and it is to be hoped that they will be still further encouraged in their noble and, self-sacrificing work.</p>
        <p>IT IS REMARERBLE</p>
        <p>What ease and helpfulness may. be derived from a Pair of Glasses which have been specially made FOR, YOU. It will take us; say, from a quarter to half an hour to test your sight, but the benefit to you will be life-long.</p>
