      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>.11 pit,MICT tipri</head>
        <p>.11 pit,MICT tipri</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH</p>
        <p>:On the Bench.—The magistrates present at the Daresbury Petty Sessions on Tuesday were Messrs. Roger C. Parr (in the chair), ll:Roberts, Hepherd, C. Jackson Holmes, T. Baxter and W. Fletcher.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>CARTERS FINED. Horses' Heavy Loads of Lightest Metal.</head>
        <p>CARTERS FINED. Horses' Heavy Loads of Lightest Metal.</p>
        <p>At Daresbury Petty Sessions (Stockton Heath) on Tuesday, Albert Hayes, of 3, Greenell- - Warrington, and James Fitzpatrick, of the lodging-house, 93, Tanner's-lane, Warring- ton, were summoned for cxuelly overloading two horses in London-road, Stockton Heath, on December 7th. The former pleaded guilty and the latter not guilty. inspector BrePze sail be noticed Hayes and Fitzpatrick driving two horses attached to lurries laden with aluminium. The horses were so exhausted that they could not go more than twenty yards without stopping, although tan drivers urged them to proceed. He ordered them to draw the horses to the roadside, as they were exhausted, and the men then fastened Fitzpatrick's horse in front of Hayes', but the lurry stopped after fifty yards. Fitzpatrick told him they had 5 tons 6 cwt. 1 qr. between them, and Hayes said his load was 2 tons 12 cwt. .1 qr. Each of the lurries weighed bewteen 25 cwt. and 30 cwt. The horses' heads were drooping and they were " blowing " a good deal. Fitzpatrick defied him, and said: "You can't stop me; you're only a bobby," whereupon, he (witness) replied that he would have him locked up if he went any further. Inspector Dee, R.S.P.C.A., Mr. J. H. T. Mor- ris and two constables also gave evidence, the Inspector stating that, apart from the lurries, the two loads weighed 5 tons 6 cwt. 1 qr. Fitzpatrick said he had driven loads ea heavy as the one in question for ten years without being stopped before. He was fined 20s. and costs, Hayes having to pay 10s. and costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>EARLY CLOSING DAY. Shopkeepers Have No Choice of Afternoon.</head>
        <p>EARLY CLOSING DAY. Shopkeepers Have No Choice of Afternoon.</p>
        <p>Margaret Clare, provision dealer, of Kimber- ley-drive, Stockton Heath, was summoned at the Daresbury Petty Sessions (Stockton Heath) on Tuesday, for keeping her shop open on the afternoon of Thursday, December 17th, con- trary to the provisions of the Shops Act. The Inspector stated that shortly before five o'clock on the day in question he was passing the shop, and noticed some qustomers inside. He saw Mrs. Clare reach down some bacon and cut it. He called her attention to the day, and she said she closed the shop on Mondays. He then told her that she had no choice of afternoon, &amp;s the Order fixed Thursdays for Stockton Heath. Mr. Clare (who appeared in place of his wife) said they had always closed their shop on Mon- days since the Act came into force. There was another shop near theirs that also closed on Monday. He pointed out that there were two holidays in the following week—Christmas Day and Boxing Day. Defendant was fined ss.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>ANTRO BUS. FAMILY IN "SHANTY." Labourer and His Wife Summoned</head>
        <p>ANTRO BUS. FAMILY IN "SHANTY." Labourer and His Wife Summoned</p>
        <p>For Child Neglect.</p>
        <p>SHORTAGE _OF COTTAGES. Some remarkable revelations were made at the Daresbury Petty Sessions, held at Stockton Heath on Tuesday, when Thomas Riley—a farm labourer—and his wife, kmily, of Murray's Farm, Antrobus, were summoned for neglecting their seven children in a manner likely to in- jure their health. Only the male defendant appeared, and Mr. A. Browne prosecuted on behalf Of the N.S.P.C.C.—RiIey pleaded guilty, but said it was not his fault. Mr. Browne stated that in this case the ob- ject of the N.S.P.C.C. was to get the man and his wife to live in proper surroundings. De- fendant worked at farming, and, twelve months ago, had been employed on piece work at a farm. He was then living at Reid's Farm Cot- tage—which was not fit to be inhabited.</p>
        <p>Children's Shocking Condition. The Northwich Inspector for the N.S.P.C.C. visited it, and found the Cottage and the chil- dren in a shocking condition. He then reported the case to Inspector Wainwright, the Warring- ton Inspector, who had had charge of it since January, 1914. The cottage was presently pur- chased—to the knowledge of some of the Bench— and Riley was ordered to quit. This, however, he refused to do—until the roof was taken off over his head. Before that time he had been repeatedly warned by Inspector Wainwright about the condition of the cottage. The family, continued Mr. Browne, could not then find a house; and by the kindness of the farmer were allowed to go into a shanty on the farm—and they all knew what shanties were like! Afterwards they were told to clear out. "Now," said the speaker, "they won't clear out, although the shanty is absolutely Unfit for occupation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>Father's Laziness.</head>
        <p>Father's Laziness.</p>
        <p>"What the ;an-4;W authorities or the Rural District Council are doing in the district I do not know— a man and his wife and seven chil- dren living in a shanty!! The Inspector has found the place filthy; and the children in a shocking, condition. The man, by his own ad- mission, can make 30s. per week, but he won't get up in the morning. Why the man won't go to his work or to a decent house I can't imagine; but if the Rural District Council won't take action, the N.S.P.C.C. !" Inspector Turpin described his visits to the place. He found the children in a shocking condition, and there were vermin everywhere. Fowls ran in and out. When he went again there was no attempt at cleanliness anywhere. The man had been cautioned.</p>
        <p>Home Dilapidated, But Unnecessarily Dirty. Mr. H. Roberts (one of the magistrates) : Was that cottage allowed to go practically to pieces?</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne: It was. Mr. Roberts: How much of the condition of the cottage was due to the family% and how much to the landlord? Could it be kept clean in that dilapidated state? Inspector Turpin : Undoubtedly. Much cleaner than it was. Mr. Roberts: Is it not a fact that most of the cottages round Antrobus are like this? Do you know of your own knowledge whether there is a decent cottage to be got round there? Inspector Turpin : There is not! Dr. Bower said he had visited the shanty and examined the children the previous Friday. They were badly clothed, and their condition would undoubtedly injure their health. In answer to Mr. Browne: He believed the sanitary authorities had visited the shanty. He</p>
