      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <p>:</p>
        <p>had advised the man to take the children to tithe Workhouse, which he indignantly refased to do.</p>
        <p>Inspector Advised the Workhouse. Inspector Wainwright, in the cour4e ,of his evidence, said the• contlition of the Jams° had varied from dirtiness to cleanliness, from cleanliness to dirtiness. He had upon three occasions asked the man and his wife to go to the Workhouse Until such time as they could ,Ocure a good house. The Chairman (Mr. Roger C. Parr) Would the parents look after the children if they had a proper house? Inspector Wainwright: No. The woman does nothing but sit about. The husband will not get up in the morning. The Chairman remarked that he had seen the farmer for whom the man worked, and had been told that many a morning they had had to send a man to knock him up. Defendant said he had tried his best to get a house, but could not get out of the shanty for love or money, and there was no room to do any housework in the shanty. Mr. Browne: That is our case. We are help- less, and want you to help us. It was decided to adjourn the ca4e for a month.</p>
        <p>The Clerk (Mr. H. Greenall) to defendant:</p>
        <p>In the meantime you had better take the ad- vice of the Inspector, if you cannot get another house!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0182" n="art0182">
        <head>HATTON.</head>
        <p>HATTON.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC APOLOGY ENDS CASE. Police Court Sequel To Boxing Day</p>
        <p>"Jollity."</p>
        <p>Thomas Britland, farm labourer, of Hatton, appeared before the magistrates at the Dares- bury Petty Sessions on Tuesday for having been violent at the Hatton Arms on Boxing Day, and for refusing to quit.—Mr. W. H. J. Jenkins, of Warrington, conducted the defence. Mr. A. Browne, who prosecuted for the</p>
        <p>licensee of the Hatton Arm- s, said that on the day in question Britland (who was in the house along with otlier men) started singing, and be- came disorderly when requested to stop. He (the speaker) had, however, been approached that morning by Mr. Jenkins, and was willing to withdraw the case if defendant would pub-</p>
        <p>fiely apologise and promise never, to enter- the house again. He might say that the licensee had been severely injured. Mr. Jenkins said the withdrawal of the case wai desirous from every point of view. There had been several soldiers in the kitchen at the time; he refrained from criticising the landlady, however, and would accept the withdrawal of the case. With regard to the injury done to the</p>
        <p>landlady, great regret had already been per- sonally expressed—although Britland had noth- ing to do with it. Defendant then apologised, and undertook never to enter the house again, whereupon the Bench consented to the withdrawal of the sum- mons.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <head>MOORE.</head>
        <p>MOORE.</p>
        <p>Wesley Guild.—A very enjoyable soiree was held in the Schoolroom on Wednesday evening in connection with the Wesley Guild. The first part of the programme took the form of a hum- orous sketch, entitled "Who's Who," in which the following lady members took part: Miss E. Dane, Miss E. Tanner, Miss R. Tanner, and Miss M. Eustance. The remainder of the pro- gramme was given by the Brooklyn Concert Party from Warrington. For all-round excel- lence in different types of singing this party would be _hard to beat. Quartets, duets and solos were in turn all excellently rendered, and Mr. W. J. Hopkins caused much mirth with his humorous items. Mr. Albert Prow officiated at the piano and during the evening delighted the audience with two musical monologues. During the interval refreshments were served by the ladies of the Social Committee. A very hearty vote of thanks to the entertainers was proposed by Miss B. Dutton and heartily carried.</p>
        <p>Red Cross Whist Drive.—A whist drive was held in the Milner Church Institute on Thurs- day evening, in aid of the local Red Cross organ- isations. About a hundred people attended. Light refreshments were served in the interval. The prim-winners were :—Ladies: 1, Mrs. Roberts 2, Miss N. de Prez. Gentleman: 1, Mr. H. Tickle; 2, Mr. John .Cartwright. The prizes and refreshments were kindly given by lady friends.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>Wesley Guild.—The monthly devotional meet- ing of the Wesley Guild was held on Monday evening, and was presided over by the Rev. A. J. Morton. A very interesting address was given by the Rev. Hugh Williamson, his subject being "The Romance of the Gospels." Miss Mason gave a sweet rendition of the solo "Light after Darkness."</p>
        <p>The Vacant Chaplaincy at the Homes.—The Rev. E. E. Chamberlain, the curate-in-charge at Padgate, has been nominated by the Bishop of Liverpool for the chaplaincy at the Padgate Cottage Homes, and at the fortnightly meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians on Wednesday, testimony was paid to the abilities of the curate, and Mr. Mather gave notice that he would move his amintment at the next meeting.</p>
        <p>Wesleyan Tea Meeting.—The annual congre- gational tea meeting and entertainment were held in the Wesley Hall on Thursday evening, being presided over by Mr. Lunt. A very interesting and instructive address was given by the Rev. A. J. Morton, in which he pointed out the importance of home influence on the chil- dren, in addition to the Sunday School teach- ing, as the time spent in Sunday School was so short that it was most important that parents should follow up the instruction in the home life. The following artistes provided a very attractive programme: Mrs. Cottrell, the Misses D. London, Eileen Morton, A. Tomlin, and Duckworth, and Messrs. J. Hannan, Nurse, Cook, Angus and Ashton, Miss L. Duckworth being at the piano.</p>
        <p>Sunday Scholars Entertained.—On Friday evening the children attending the Wesleyan Sunday School had their tea meeting in the Schoolroom, after which came a concert and distribution of prizes. The Rev. A. J. Morton was in the chair. The primary children, who had been carefully trained by Miss L. Wright, gave an excellent rendering of the "Flag Song," , and the nursery rhyme, "Where are you going 1 to ray pretty maid?" Recitations were given ' by the following: The Misses M. Tunstall, E. Wilcox, A. Meredith, M. Sutton and E. Angus, and Master J. Davies. The elder scholars, who had been trained by Miss Wright and Mrs. Dakin, also gave a few interesting items, the boys singing "Off to Klondyke," and the girls singing the French, Japanese, Belgian, Russian and English National Anthems. The soloists were Miss A. Ball, who sang Elgar's "Land of Hope and' Glory," and Miss E. Burnett, who contributed "Rule, Britannia!" Recitations were also given by Master B. Stables and Miss L. Sykes and Mr. H. Monks. The prizes were presented by Miss H. Bennett, who has been connected with the Sunday School for a large number of years.</p>
        <p>Death of Mrs. Harriett Ward.—We regret to announce the death of a well-known Pad- , gatonian in the person of Mrs. Harriett Ward,l who passed peacefully away at her residence, Barrowby, Green-lane, on Tuesday morning. Born hr 1845 at Kelsall, near Chester, the de- ceased lady's early days were spent in that vil- lage. In 1869 she was married, at the Parish Church, Delamere, to Mr. Henry Ward, who predeceased her. Following her marriage she resided in Liverpool for a number of years, after- wards removing to Manchester. In 1880 she came, with her family, to Padgate, where the remainder of her lire was spent. She was a strong believer in Nonconformity all her life, and during the last thirty-four years was a regular and devout worshipper at the Wesleyan Church at Padgate. She led a very quiet, life, but her generosity and nnassuminemanner won fot her a large oirc4o of friends la the Pulsate</p>
        <p>distritt, NAtere she was held in high es4V'em,by ttli ?with whom she came in contact. She leaves</p>
        <p>vale son and four daughters to mourn her loss. 'The funeral will take place this afternoon. A short service will be held in the Wesleyan Church at 1,30, followed by the interment at the Warrington Cemetery at 2.30.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>FOR THE SIDESMAN'S "BAG."</head>
        <p>FOR THE SIDESMAN'S "BAG."</p>
        <p>Rev. A. M. Mitchell Suggests Offertory</p>
        <p>Pennies for Homes' Children.</p>
        <p>"That the Church of England children at the Padgate Homes be supplied with offertory money when attending Sunday service at church." Such was the resolution proposed by the Rev. A. M. Mitchell at the fortnightly meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians, held under the chairmanship of Mr. A. J. Jolley at the. Workhouse on Wednesday. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell said the offertory was by way of compensation for the £l3 which the church appeared to have had formerly, and as a substitute for pew rents. Before sending in the notice of motion he inquired from the Superintendent whether or not what he had in mind was feasible. The Superintendent said it would be quite easy to give the children a penny or a halfpenny as they were marshalled for church. He (the speaker) was of the opinion that the Padgatites would benefit. When it was worked out, the amount would be, perhaps, something varying between £lO or £l5, suppos- ing each child attending the morning service at church received a penny. If they did not agree to a penny they might make it a halrpenny. So long as the principle of helping the church was recognised, he would be satisfied, and he thought those who had the interests of Pa.dgate Church at heart, as evidently had their brother Mr. Mather--</p>
        <p>Mr. Knowles: Christian brother. er.)</p>
        <p>(Laugh-</p>
        <p>Guardians in Loco Parentis.</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: They would be well advised to support that particular form of monetary aid. Considering the question from the standpoint of justice to the children, he thought it was a recognised principle, upon which they were proud to act, that they took the place of the fathers and mothers. The Guardians ought to try and deal with them as they would with their own children. He did not know whether Mr. Mather had a family, but then it was never too late to mend their ways—(laughter)—but, if he had a family, and knew as much about little children as he (the speaker) knew, he would quite understand how the youngsters from the homes like to be put on terms of equality with other children. They did not like to be sent to church without their copper, and he knew children in his own parish —poor and despised as it was—who would not go to church if their father or mother did not give them a penny or a halfpenny to contribute to the offertory. Mr. Knowles: To buy toffee with on the way. (Laughter.)</p>
        <p>A Point "of Supreme Importance."</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: The point of equal- ity is of supreme importance. Our children at the homes are not equal on the floor of God's house with other children from the parish of Padgate. They are passed over with the offer- tory plate, or bag, and if a stranger came into the church he might naturally inquire what was the reason that the children had been passed over. He would be told, "Oh ! they are chil- dren of the Poor Law." Perhaps, someone who does not treat' the word " pauper " as I treat it, would say, "These are pauper children." Just think what the children will feel! It is a slight upon them, and children are very sensi- tive and would feel it very much. If you adopt the offertory system, which is the true Christian principle of church support, you are training the children in the good habit of giving. At the present time we all know the difficulties about religion, and religious work, and we are complaining at all the churches about shortage, and resort to peculiar devices in order to make money—raffles, &amp;c. Mr. Knowles: Illegal—law-breaking! The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: We do these things instead of teaching our children to give system- atically every Lord's Day. Mr. Knowles rose to speak, but The Chairman pointed out that the resolu-</p>
        <p>:ion was not seconded. Mr. Brooks: Before a ventilation of opinion is given, can we get to know if the resolution is Legal?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>Would It Be Legal?</head>
        <p>Would It Be Legal?</p>
        <p>Mr. Knowles: The Local Government Board will not sanction it.</p>
        <p>Mr. Brooks: It is useleps to second it, then. The Chairman: If this is seconded, I will settle whether it is in order or not. If no one seconds it, the resolution falls to the ground. Mr. Knowles: I wanted to tell you a tale about the offertory system, of three men- The Chairman: Order, order. (Much laugh- ter.) The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: You have no spiri- tual faculty. (More laughter.) Mr. Knowles: Plenty of spirits. I had some last night. (Laughter.) 'Piero was no, seconder to the resolution. Mr. Mather: I shall give notice "that a con- tribution be paid to the wardens of Padgate Church towards the heating, lighting and clean- ing expenses." I object to Mr. Mitchell calling me his brother. Anyone looking round would think I had had the big spoon and he had had the little spoon. (Laughter.) The Rev. A. M. Mitchell brother? I am so sorry.</p>
        <p>Are you not a</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>PRETTY WEDDING AT GREAT SANKEY CHURCH.</head>
        <p>PRETTY WEDDING AT GREAT SANKEY CHURCH.</p>
        <p>At St. Mary's, Great Sankey, on Saturday, the marriage took place of Mr. Thomas Edward Carrington, eldest son bf Mr. Thomas Carring« ton, of Park-view, Penketh, and Miss Mary Alice Haslam, of Belmont, Whittle Hall-lane, Great Sankey, the Rev. J. R. Jones (vicar) offi- ciating. The bride was given away by her father, Mr. Edwin Haslam. She wore a charm- ing dress of cream corduroy silk, with a hat to match, and carried a sheaf of lilies and carna- tions. The bridegroom's present to the bride was a dress ring. The bridesmaids were the Misses Lillie Ditchfield and Florence Carring- ton, and they were dressed in pretty shades of blue, and carried bouquets composed of white chrysanthemums and lilies of the valley. The duties of best man were discharged by Lance- Corporal F. Haslam, only brother of the bride. Mr: T. F. Wallington was at the brgan. The wedding breakfast was served at the bride's home, about sixty relatives and friends attend- ing. The Vicar proposed .the health of the newly-mariied couple. Numerous presents were received.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>QUEER NAMES OF BOOKS.</head>
        <p>QUEER NAMES OF BOOKS.</p>
        <p>These are some of the odd titles of old Eng- lish books published in the time of Cromwell : A Most Delectable Sweet perfumed Nosegay for God's Stains to Smell At. Biscuit Baked In the Oven of Charity, Care- fully Conserved for the Chickens .of the Church, the Sparrows of the Spirit and the Sweet Swallows of Salvation. ,</p>
        <p>A 'Sigh of Sorrow for the Sinners of Zion Breathed Out of a Hole in the Wall of an Earthly Vessel Known Among Meh by the Name of Samuel Fish.</p>
        <p>Eggs of Charity Layed, for the Chickens ol the Covenant and Boiled with the Water o: Divine Love. Take Ye Out and Eat. The Spiritual Mustard Pot to Make. the Sou Sneeze with Devotion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>CZAR'S CROWNS.</head>
        <p>THE CZAR'S CROWNS.</p>
        <p>The Czar has as many crowns as a fashionable lady has hats. He is regarded by his people as a religious as well as a secular monarch, and therefore has crowns cfor every possible State occasion. The Russian imperial crown is modeled after a patriarchal miter. Five magni- ficent diamonds, resting on a huge glowing ruby, form the cross at the summit. Diamonds and pearls of utmost perfection render this crown unrivalled among all others and there is one sapphire in it which is said to be the finest gone of its kind 'ever mined.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
