      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <head>LYIVIM.</head>
        <p>LYIVIM.</p>
        <p>United Prayer Meeting.—On Saturday even- ing, in the Primitive Methodist Church, a united intercessory prayer meeting was con- ducted by Mr. J. Waterworth.</p>
        <p>Footballers Help Reci Cross.—A contribution of X 4 7s. 9d. has just been handed to the Lymm Red Cross Hospital as a result of the football match held on Christmas Day.</p>
        <p>Vicar of The!wall Resigns.—We understand that the Rev. J. Tiarks, vicar of Thelwall, is resigning his charge and is leaving the parish at once, having accepted a preferment in the South. It will be remembered that Mr. Tiarks succeeded the late Rev. T. Pym Williamson in the incumbency of Thelwall, a few months ago.</p>
        <p>Recruiting and Lymm Football.—Owing to the recruiting from the ranks of the Lymm footballers, only four members of ffie Wednes- day Club remain—three more (Messrs. G. Cope- land (captain), G. F. Readitt and J. Cheetham) having this week joined the colours. So many have now gone from the ranks of the Wednes- day League clubs that the League is preparing a roll of honour.</p>
        <p>Free Church Council's Annual Meeting.—The annual meeting of the Lymm Free Church Coun- cil was held on Friday evening of last week in the Primitive Methodist Church. The President (Rev. E. E. Lovell) was in the chair. The Hon. Secretary (Mr. J. D. B. Smith) gave the treas- urer's statement„in the unavoidable absence of Mr. J. Waterworth, also his own report, both of which were duly adopted. The Rev. A. Rip- pener (of Manchester) then gave a very inter- esting lecture en "Our Heroic Forefathers," and he was followed by the Rev. John Heath (secretary of the Manchester Federation of Free Church Councils), who spoke on the work and aims of Free Church Councils. After the passing of a hearty vote of thanks to the speakers, accorded •on the motion of Mr. R. Hewitt, seconded by Mr: S. Moss, the meeting was brought to a close by the singing of the Doxology.</p>
        <p>not willing to come forward. First there was the selfish man: "Why should I go? I can get another man's work: perhaps he may get killed, and then I shall get all his job.' But he (the speaker) did not believe there was anything such as real cowardice. No man was really afraid.</p>
        <p>"Think It Out."</p>
        <p>The worst districts for recruiting, he was told, were the farming districts. This was disgrace- ful. Some of the men—who told him that they occasionally saw a weekly paper—had said : "What does it matter to us whether the King or the Kaiser rules over us? We should still be here doing our work." He always told young men to think it out, come to a conclusion, and then stand by their decision. It was for every- body to think. He was not going to appeal to men's feelings, but to their reasons. The need of men was very great. They wanted to save the lives of the men in the trenches by relief, relief, relief. Every life was a valuable one, and they' needed a constant change of the men in the trenches. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>Again, this war was an expensive one. It was costing ten millions every week, and that would continue as long as the war lasted. They would have to pay the taxes in the future; so let them have the men for the sake of economy! If there were any men who were wavering, let them come; let them" think of these things." He was perfectly sure that if very man would only "think of these things," he would not linger! (Loud applause.)</p>
        <p>DR. FLETCHER AND THE INSURANCE POINT OF VIEW. Dr. F. S. Fletcher said he thought there was hardly anyone in this country or the world who did not now know that for years Germany had been preparing to annihilate us. It had been proved up to the hilt that she had long been our bitter enemy, only waiting for the day to come when she could strike the blow. And for the men who had heard and seen everything and yet would not do th6r share, ..he had nothing but the most vital and bitter contempt. He asked them to look at the war from an insurance point of view. We had a grave and real calamity looming up before vs. Was it not worth while to sacrifice something now as a premium to insure themselves, their wives, children, pro- perty, possessions—everything they had worth having? All the soldiers who had seen anything said that this war was not going to end up in a hurry. Everything depended upon one thing alone for our ultimate success—men to fall back upon. If they enlisted, they only left a small family to become members of a larger one: and, speaking on behalf of his own regiment and by the command of Colonel Crawford, he could say they would all receive a hearty welcome— and, apart from the hard work, they did not have a bad time. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>CAPTAIN GOSSETT TELLS OF "THAT</p>
        <p>MOST GALLANT REGIMENT."</p>
        <p>Captain Gossett pointed out that, since the Battle of ions, the Cheshires had been known as "that most gallant regiment." (Applause.) From the mobilisation, up to five o'clock that evening, the county of Chester had—irrespec- tive of the Territorials—given 15,359 men to Lord Kitchener. If they wanted to make his heart happy, they would give him fifty more men before the following Saturday evening. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>VICE-CHANCELLOR SMITH AND THE POWER THAT COMPELS.</p>
        <p>Vice-Chancellor Smith proposed a .vote of</p>
        <p>thanks to the speakers. -.Eiery recruit there that night must feel a proud man to take part in this war. There had been wars before this— wars caused by differences/ of religion, by dif- ferences of race, by the ambitions of rulers, by the desir6 for territory, and other causes that moved men's hearts—but there had never been a war like the present one. Between the 'Sixties and 'Seventies, Germany waged three of the wars- he had mentioned, and then—there was a pause! After Bismarck's death, when the strong . _ .</p>
        <p>hand that had restrained the Kaiser was re- moved, he entered upon a plan which he was pursuing to-day. Germany had got to the sea: she must get to the sea again! She Vowed she would push to all the Continents. It was taught in the schools; the whole Civil Service had been organised to that end; its Army had been ron the drill; and its Kaiser had spoken. Those were the ends that Germany had pursued; those were the aims she had given to the world. And she had chosen Britain for her foe! She had chosen well, for Britain was her foe—be- cause Britain was free! (Applause.)</p>
        <p>Compulsory Service?</p>
        <p>Now, we were "up against a big proposition." %We had not been prepared for war on the 4th August; we were better prepared for it now. But we had millions of trained men to beat, and we wanted every man in this country to do his share. Was it, then, to be by compul- sory training? Compulsory military service was in the air, and we were not in ordinary times now. But there was a greater and more powerful compulsion—the power that came from the heart of a man; his conscience and his duty. The power which compelled was the power of one's own conscience.</p>
        <p>He had then in the House of Lords the pre- ceding Thursday, listening to Lord Kitchener when he made his third statement on the pro- gress of the war; and one of the things His Lordship said was that, since, August, 69,100 commissions had been given to young men serving in His Majesty's Forces. These were all drawn from the better classes. Let them think of,their own district ! Was there a house within a mile from which one man bad not gone? There were many from which all the men had gone! The Army was a great family, and all social distinctions were lost in it. If they thought they had a snug little job, and they did not like to leave it—where was that job going to be at the end of the war?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <head>LOYAL LYNEVI.</head>
        <p>LOYAL LYNEVI.</p>
        <p>Stirring Patriotic Scenes At Recruiting Meeting. STRONG APPEALS.</p>
        <p>Scenes of great enthusiasm were witnessed at the Lymm Drill Hall on Wednesday evening, when a monster meeting was held for the pur- pose of prevailj-ng upon those young men of Lymm and district, who had not already taken up arms on behalf of King and country, to do so without delay. A considerable time before the commencement of the meeting the hall was packed; and in the meantime the Manchester Royal Field Artillery Band gave several rous- ing patriotic airs, until at length the speakers mounted the gaily-decorated platform to the stirring refrain of "The Marseillaise "—being accorded a splendid ovation. Mr. A. Watkin presided, and the speaker was His Honour Judge Mellor, B.C. Other speeches were given by Vice-Chancellor Dudley Stewart Smith; Surgeon-Captain F. S. Fletcher (16th Battalion Manchester Regiment), Captain Gossett (recruit- ing, officer for the Chester Division), the Hon. J. E. Cross, Mr. A. de C. Parmiter, and Mr. Arthur Newton; whilst there were also upon the platform Captain Linaker (recruiting officer for the Aitrincham Division), Mr. F. E. Evans (Lymm recruiting officer), and Mr. W. N. Moles- worth. Mr. W. Peter Rylands wrote apologising for his inability to be present.</p>
        <p>At the front Of the audience were several re- cuperating soldiers from Brookfield, who were in the charge of the Red Cross nurses. Detach- ments of the Lymm and Heatley Boy Scouts— under Scoutmasters Farrar, Nash, Timperley and Hatton—also attended.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <head>MR. A. WATIGN AND LY.MM'S SHARE IN</head>
        <p>MR. A. WATIGN AND LY.MM'S SHARE IN</p>
        <p>RECRUITING.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said he believed that that was the first public meeting which had been held in that hall since the outbreak of hostilities; and so lie thought it well to tell them that—in addition to twenty men from that district who had been in,the Regfular Army for some yea/14— over two hundred and seventy men it ho lived at Lymm and Oughtrington had now answered the call, and were serving their King and country. (Applause.) They applauded their patriotism. Their thoughts and prayers went with them, and they might rest assured that, during their absence from home, those they left behind would be well looked after. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>Call To Arms.</head>
        <p>The Call To Arms.</p>
        <p>That night they were there to ask others to einulate their soldiers' good example. Unfor- tunately there were, in all walks of life, some people who were always willing to let others fight their battles for them and undergo all the privations which war entailed upon those who went. But he did hope that' there was no man in Lymm who would forsake his duty in that time of national need. If, however, there were any present who hesitated between patriotism on the one hand, and selfishness and ease on the other, he would like to ask them if, when they were eligible for service and saw other men wearing the King's uniform, they were satisfied tr) stay at home. _</p>
        <p>"Men of Lymm !" said the speaker, "your King and country need you! Never were the words of the great Nelson truer than they are to-day : England expects every man to do his duty!" (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <head>JUDGE MELLOR AND SACREDNESS OF OUR NATIONAL WORD.</head>
        <p>JUDGE MELLOR AND SACREDNESS OF OUR NATIONAL WORD.</p>
        <p>Judge Mellor, who had a splendid reception, said he was not going to speak about the war— for he was not a military man, and he was not even a military arm-chair critic. He knew nothing about war, and had no criticisms to pass upon the conduct of those who were re- sponsible for it. He was there to ask those who had not gone why they had not gone? He could only suppose that the real reason why so many people were still keeping back was because they did not realise the great danger the country Was in, and because they did not realise what the war meant We were fighting really for a great principle—the sacredness of our national word; and he was one of those who thought that that was the highest thing we could fight for. He remembered travelling abroad, some years ago, with a friend—a student like himself. One night they lost all their money. Of course, there were linguistic diffi- culties—as, having been brought up in an Eng- .. _ _ _</p>
        <p>lish University, they knew n tongue but their own. (Laughter.) However, they went to the money-changer, who said he would cash any cheque they -liked. He (the speaker) happened to express his astonishment at such willingness, but the man replied: "Oh, it's all right; you're Englishmen, and I know that your word is as good as your money." (Applause.) A friend of his had an experience much the same in Damascus—although he was dressed like a tramp rather than like the wealthy man he was! Did they, then, not think it a great thing to belong to a country that had such a reputa- tion? And what had given them that, reputa- tion? England owed her greatness to being true to what she said she would do. Of course, we had not conquered all our Empire by our own efforts, but we had kept it because we , had al- ways been true to our word. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>An International Tribunal.</head>
        <p>An International Tribunal.</p>
        <p>After referring to the making of the "Belgian neutrality" treaty and how Germany broke it, the speaker went on to say that he had had friends among the Germans, and could not un- defstand how, when they wanted Anything, they could say that Might was Right. To do away with these 'awful wars the only thing required was that the nations should have some tribunal to which they could go. If such a tribunal had been in existence to-day we should not have the frightful state of things that now existed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>Interest and Existence.</head>
        <p>Interest and Existence.</p>
        <p>But there was another thing we were fighting for. Our interest and existence was wrapped up in the war, and he did not think that was realised by the people. What would be the state of England if it dame under the rule of the Germans, judging by what they had done in Belgium and France? There was no doubt now that the Germans had meant to take country by country. It was all very well to say that they could not have conquered our Fleet. Per- haps they could not, but they could have dam- aged it very severely. What would it mean to them if a million men went to the bottom?— they could bring up 'many more millions. He did not really think that was realised by the men of this country, because he had heard the piost extraordinary reasons given why men were</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
        <head>Downtrodden German Worker.</head>
        <p>Downtrodden German Worker.</p>
        <p>They had got to beat Germany, or England would become a little German province. And would they be content with German wages? If not—. Trade unions were illegal in Ger- many, and a strike was a riot, when men were shot down, not in ones or twos, but in hun- dreds. It had happened often enough already! The conditions of to-day Would not be the con- ditions of to-morrow, and certainly not the conditions of six months hence. And if they wanted to earn 'their country's gratitude, let them enlist to-day! That little place had sent nearly three hundred men, but it could send more. These were the men who had been brought up with them, and they were calling to them now! (Prolonged applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0201" n="art0201">
        <head>MR. A. NEWTON'S ADVICE TO THE</head>
        <p>MR. A. NEWTON'S ADVICE TO THE</p>
        <p>LADIES.</p>
        <p>Mr. Arthur Newton seconded. He stood before them, he said, as one who had served fOr over thirty years in His Majesty's Forces, and would advise the men to join the sth Cheshire Batta- lion, for there they would be amongst their own "pals." To the ladies he would say: "Don't hold them back! And if you haven't a Sweetheart at present, wait until the boys come back; and then you will have one to be proud of!" • (Laughter and applause.) _ _</p>
        <p>The motion was carried with acclamation. The Chairman announced that Captain Linaker would take the names of recruits if they would stay behind at the close of the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0202" n="art0202">
        <head>HON. J. E. CROSS AND THE FARMER'S</head>
        <p>HON. J. E. CROSS AND THE FARMER'S</p>
        <p>DIFFICULTY.</p>
        <p>The Hon. .r. E. Cross then said that before they dispersed he would like to thank their Chairman for presiding over the meeting. They had met with one common object—to do good to Lymm, to their country, and to their King; and nobody in Lymm had done more for these,- during the war, than Mr. Watkin. (Applause.) With reference to the difficulty in recruiting from the farms, the labourers would make good soldiers, it was true, and the farmers could help them to go by employing those who had passed the age limit. They could all go to the farina and help to gather the crops in—which could not be don i without labour, and they would then have the satisfactory feeling that they had "given their mite" to the country. (Applause.) Mr. A. de C. ParmiterAwho seconded, said Mr. Watkin evidently felt it his duty to do all he could for the country. The motion was unanimously carried, and Mr. Watkin suitably replied, paying also a tri- bute to the valuable services of Mr. F. E. Evans, the Lymm recruiting officer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0203" n="art0203">
        <head>RECRUITING RESULT.</head>
        <p>RECRUITING RESULT.</p>
        <p>After the meeting. several men who had at- tended added their names to their country's roll of honour—their example -being followed the next day by many more.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0204" n="art0204">
        <head>CONTRACTOR'S "FUSE."</head>
        <p>CONTRACTOR'S "FUSE."</p>
        <p>Mr. Simpson's Successful Application</p>
        <p>to the Council.</p>
        <p>The application of Mr. Henry Simpson, sani- tary contractor, for a "rise" in salary, which was discussed at the Rural District Council meeting last week and referred to the Parish Council, came before that body on Monday even- ing. The letter of application stated: "Owing to the advance in wages and other expenses, I can- not do the sanitary contract at the present rate. I want to call your attention to the extra work caused by the new houses which have been built during the last eighteen months, which number 32. I therefore require an advance of £l5 per annum. Failing this, my contract with your Council will expire at the end of January." The Clerk :. He had an increase of £lO about 18 months ago. The Chairman (Councillor Furness) : Of course, what he states about 32 houses is not correct. We gave him a rise some 18 months age on the ground that the population had in- creased. His salary was then brought to 02100 per year. I think the number of houses built during the 18 months will be about 18. Councillor Taylor: I think he does his Work satisfactorily. The Chairman: They pay £lO5 in Penketh, and they have a bigger population than us. The Clerk :',They may have 50 or 80 houses moie, but they have not such an extensive area. Mr. Simpson attended, but before be was called in the Council decided to offer him an increase of £lO. -</p>
        <p>_ Mr. Simpson stuck out for the Xl5, and it was granted to him on condition that his agreement should be made to read: "Three months' notice on either side to terminate the appointment."' Previously Mr. Simpson was on a month's notice..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0205" n="art0205">
        <head>DANGEROUS POLES. Should Be Moved "In the Interests of Safety."</head>
        <p>DANGEROUS POLES. Should Be Moved "In the Interests of Safety."</p>
        <p>With reference to the alleged dangerous posi- tion of the telegraph posts at Barnes' Bridge, further letters were read by the Clerk at the Parish Council meeting on Monday. At the outset the Chairman said he thought. the Telegraph Department Rad moved the posts. He had seen men working there the previous week. _ 4 _</p>
        <p>The Clerk: If that is so, it is unknown to me. I have not had a communication either- way. Mr. W. Compton Hall, county bridge-master, wrote stating that he did not consider the. County Council had any responsibility in the matter. 'Re Was of the opinion that the Parish Council had done the right thing in writing to the Postal Telegraph Department. He also stated that he would go further into the ques- tion of widening the bridge. Mr. Wright, who wag; concerned in a recent accident near the posts, wrote: "I trust that in the interests of public safety your efforts will meet with success:" The , Postal Telegraph Department wrote: "You? letter shall receive attention." Councillor Pownall : The probability is that it will be taken in hand. _</p>
        <p>The Clerk: Oh, yes, but they don't say any- thing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0206" n="art0206">
        <head>SUNDAY LETTERS. Should- There Be A Sabbath Day Delivery?</head>
        <p>SUNDAY LETTERS. Should- There Be A Sabbath Day Delivery?</p>
        <p>"That this Council is of the opinion that the delivery of letters might be extended to Sundays and the office kept open for the sale of stamps, if Mr. Knowles (in charge of the sub-post office) has no objection." This resolution was, passed at a meeting of the Sankey Parish Coun- cil, held on Monday evening, Councillor Furness. presiding. The Clerk read the following letter from Mr. Chambers, the Warrington Postmaster: "I am directed to mention that the present arrange- ments entail keeping open Great Sankey sub.. Post Office for the purpose of callers. In case a complete delivery is made, of course, there will no longer be the necessity to keep the office Open except for the casual sale of postage stamps. Will you inform me whether, in your o.pinion, the closing of the office :on Sundays will cause any inconveniences?" . .</p>
        <p>The Clerk: A representative from the Post Office came to see me with respect to the appli- eaton , and I gave him certain information. T. have since made .further inquiries at the Post Office and Mr. Knowles tells me it might reduce the emoluments of one or two who have had the sorting and taking of letters to the Post Office. He is of the opinion, however, that if the sale of stamps is continued it will not interfere with the little allowance 4e gets. The Chairman: He does not object to the sale of stamps? • The Clerk: Oh, no. The Chairman: If the postmaster is willing to sell stamps as usual, and we get the letters as welt it will be' a decided improvement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0207" n="art0207">
        <head>s• • • ;,4i. V 13160,.... 41,„*". . • , •1:0, !$, _.. • .4,100 P • • .„1,, ;;;. o • . , . . • gee- • " • ; , ;,•• •"</head>
        <p>s• • • ;,4i. V 13160,.... 41,„*". . • , •1:0, !$, _.. • .4,100 P • • .„1,, ;;;. o • . , . . • gee- • " • ; , ;,•• •"</p>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>OLD FOLK'S TREAT.</p>
        <p>Tea, Supper and Concert for 120 of the</p>
        <p>Aged.</p>
        <p>On Friday of last week the annual Penketh, Sankey and District Old Folk's Treat was held in the Wesleyan Schoolroom, Penketh. Mr. James Woodward had made the room look gay with decorations. There were 158 invitations issued, and 120 old folk were able to respond, and were entertained to tea and supper, with a first- class concert in between, to which friends and supporters of the fund were cordially invited. The programme was a long and most enjoyable one, and was arranged by the Bold-street (War- rington) Wesleyan Choir, who gave their ser- vices free, Mr. A. Greenhalgh, an old resident, being mainly responsible for the arrangement. Mr. Hickman, who had trained the choir, which consisted of about 40 voices, acted as accompan- ist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0208" n="art0208">
        <head>Entertainers.</head>
        <p>The Entertainers.</p>
        <p>There was a large and keenly appreciative audience, and they heartily applauded each item of the programme, the contributors to which, in addition to the choir, were Mrs. War- burton, the Misses M.-- Whitmore, Blackburne, Plumpton and E. Taylor, and Messrs. Dutton', A. Greenhalgh, Warburton, J. Clayton, E. H. Pearce, Mills, H. M. Ashton. an 1. Han'Tali.</p>
        <p>Glad the Treat Was Held "As Usual."</p>
        <p>At the end the President of the Committee responsible for organising the treat (Dr. Mur- ray) said that at one time it had been suggested that under the circumstances it would be better to drop the Old Folk's Treat for this year, but he was very glad that they had been enabled to carry it through as usual, for, going in and out of the homes of the old people as he did, he saw what a very great disappointment it would have keen to them in the worry of present events, they had been put on one side even for one year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0209" n="art0209">
        <head>Thanks and Gifts.</head>
        <p>Thanks and Gifts.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. T. Ambler, who presided over the even- ing's proceedings, then moved a vote of thanks to the artistes who had given those present such a pleasant evening and also to the friends who had made it possible. He then presented Dr. Murray with a pipe and tobacco, similar to those given to the old men. Mr. Hickman responded on behalf or the artistes to theYote of thanks accorded them, and said that if the audien6 had had as much pleasure in having them there as they had had in c,.ming. they were quite satisfied. The old ladies each received a parcel of oceries as they left.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0210" n="art0210">
        <head>VILLAGE MATTERS. Considered By Parish Council.</head>
        <p>VILLAGE MATTERS. Considered By Parish Council.</p>
        <p>• The statutory meeting of the Parish Council was held on Tuesday evening in the Council Schools, Stocks-lane. The councillors present were Dr. Murray (chairman), Messrs. J. G. Withinshaw, R. Routledge, J. Woodward, J. Ditchfield, and E. Foster (clerk). The State of the Main !load. , A letter was received from the County Sur- vleyor at Preston re the state of the main road through the village, stating that the matter should receive •attention. Dr. Murray proposed that the Clerk write again, repeating the previous complaint, and drawing attention to the abominable state of the road through the village at the present time.</p>
        <p>Mr. Withinshaw remarked that the road .was getting worse every week. The proposition was carried. The Proposed Electricity Order. A letter was read from the Electricity Depart- ment of the Warrington Corporation re the pro- posed Electric Lighting Provisional Order, which is contemplated for the district, and ask- ing for the approximate number of houses and population in the area under the Parish Council of Penketh. Mr. Withinshaw proposed that a precept be sent for £5. The Chairman seconded, and it was carried. Lamplighters' Leggings. Mr. Davies proposed that the• Clerk write to the Gas Department and ask if they would pro- vide leggings and a storm hat for the lamp- lighter. Mr. Woodward seconded. Mr. Withinshaw moved, as an amendment, that the Clerk write and inquire how the men were usually provided. - The Chairman seconded, and the amendment was carried. Concerning a Contract. A letter was read from the nightsoil contrac- tor asking for an increase of £2O peg year on his present pay, which stands at £lO5. Dr. Murray proposed that the District Coun- cil be written to, suggesting an advance of £lO. Mr. Davies seconded, and it was carried. A rider was added to the effect that if any more increases be asked for a new contract will be necessary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0211" n="art0211">
        <head>GREAT SANKEY.</head>
        <p>GREAT SANKEY.</p>
        <p>Present at the Council Meeting.—There were present at the Parish Council meeting on Mon- day, Councillors Furness (in the chair), Pow- nail, Taylor and Ireland, with Mr. Smith (clerk).</p>
        <p>Sankey's Bridge Trouble.—At the Parish Coun- cil meeting on Monday, councillor Pownall com- plained about the bridge and the manner in which the alterations are being carried out. "It seems to me," he declared, "that the work is being conducted in a slow, careless sort of manner. I crossed the other day and saw two men working on it, and there were four offi- cials present. That kind of procedure will never do, for traffic is .held up regularly. A constable has also to spend his time there.—The Clerk: Have you seen .as many as 100 vehicles held up by it?—Councillor Taylor: I have seen it.—The Clerk: I was told about it happening whilst they were putting new timber on the " relief " bridge.—lt was decided to send the following resolution to the authorities: "This Council regrets that the construction of the bridge is proceeding so slowly, and begs to call attention to the frequent congestion of .traffic on both sides of the road, as a consequence."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0212" n="art0212">
        <head>COUNCILLOR LONG RESIGNS.</head>
        <p>COUNCILLOR LONG RESIGNS.</p>
        <p>Will Mr. Lowe Accept the Position?</p>
        <p>Councillor Long has resigned his position. as parish .councillor. At the Parish Council meet- ing on Monday, the Clerk read the following letter from him: "As my business engagements necessitate my absence from the district, I beg to resign my seat on the Council, and also my seat on the Council School Management Com- mittee. Please convey my thanks to the mem- bers for their courtesy to me during my term of office. The Chairman (Councillor Furness) : Council- lor Long has accepted a position on a large firm in Ireland, and under those conditions he felt he would not be at liberty to come over as fre- quently as he wohld like, and would not be able to discharge his duties. I suggest that, in accepting his resignation, we express regret that he finds it necessary to resign and tender to him our expression of appreciation of the valuable services he has rendered during liis tenure of office.</p>
        <p>The Council adopted the Chairman's sugges- tion.</p>
        <p>The question of appointing a successor was then raised, and The Chairman asked who was the next in order on the list. The Clerk: There were seven candidates nomi- nated for election and the seventh unelected member was Mr. Lowe, from Houghton's works. It has been impressed on us by the Local Gov- ernment Board that these gentlemen nominated at the annual meeting have no claim whatever in the event of a vacancy, and that we must take into consideration the qualifications of any and every individual, irrespective of who was nominated for election.</p>
        <p>Another gentleman's name was mentioned in connection with the election, but the Chair- man said it was the usual mode of procedure for a Parish Council to avoid the trouble of an elec- tion or even to go away from the list. One reason why they should do that was that the next man on the list had been before the elec- tors and had attended at the Pariah Meeting. Of course, the matter rested entirely with the Council. Atsuraing that they had an-election,</p>
        <p>which gave them two or three men over and above, what they wanted, he did not think they would attempt to go outside those three men. As matter-of common equity, they would take one of the men on the list. Personally, how- ever, he had no feeling in the matter, either one way oe:the other. Councillor Pownall : I propose that the Clerk call upon Mr. Lowe and see if he is prepared to accept the position. The Chairman: I think he ought to be asked first. If he declines, then we have a free hand in the matter.</p>
        <p>After further discussion it was decided that Mr. Lowe be appointed, subject to his approval</p>
        <p>New School Manager.</p>
        <p>For the position of School Manager, Councillor Pownall was appointed, on the proposition of Councillor Ireland, seconded by Councillor Taylor.</p>
        <p>- - The Clerk explained that he himselt was one of the two managers whom the Council had the</p>
        <p>right to elect.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0213" n="art0213">
        <head>FIELD OF SPORT</head>
        <p>FIELD OF SPORT</p>
        <p>WILDERSPOOL "WATER CARNIVAL"</p>
        <p>Warrington Beat Barrow by a Narrow Margin.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0214" n="art0214">
        <head>GAME OF GOALS ONLY.</head>
        <p>A GAME OF GOALS ONLY.</p>
        <p>Notes by "Centre."</p>
        <p>The ground at Wilderspool on Saturday was in such a water-logged condition that ' the opinion was freely expressed that the game ought to have been declared off, anything like serious, high-class football • being impossible under the terrestial conditions prevailing. The heavy rains of the previous few days had studded the pitch with puddles, especially ilia in front of the pavilion-end goal, where pretty near- half of the turf was under water; whilst the rest of the ground was dangerously' slippery and deplorably muddy. Play had not. been in progress .many minutes before the men reserabed. mud-larks, bespattered from head to foot with the sticky, clayey soil., whilst most .of them Were drenched through- as the result of taking involuntary headers into the miniature lakes which abounded. Were the times normal there • can be little doubt that the match would have been declared off, but. no doubt the referee (Mr. Webster) 'felt that, in these days of strait- ened circumstances, the Clubs could not wel; afford to lose what promised to be a goocish sort of "gate," especially as Barrow had al- ready incurred the expense of journeying down to Warrington; and so he ordered the game to proceed. And proceed it did, with results that tan be better imagined than describe.l.. The play may have been amusing from the specta- tors' point of view, but it certainly was not football of the class that Northern tinicii sup- porters are accustomed to witness; indeed, the game may be fitly described as a mud-and- water carnival, full of laughable incidents and with- out many unduly serious moments from I,egin- ning to end.</p>
        <p>into the home territory was of brief duration, and Brookes, dribbling strongly, took the ball back into the lake-like area fronting the visi- tors' goal, only to be brought down by Narey just as he was becoming dangerous. Some de- sultory work near the mid-way line was 'followed by 4 Barrow attack, during the progress of which a knock-on by Tranter, right in the home goat, looked menacing. Warrington, however, managed to avert the threatened, danger, and Cox and Fearnley- transferred operations to the other end of the field. Half-time was near at hand when theequalising points came to Bar- row, Corns pufting on a penalty goal with a free-kick, awarded for "feet up."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0215" n="art0215">
        <head>WHY WARRINGTON WON</head>
        <p>WHY WARRINGTON WON</p>
        <p>Warrington won, and, after all, that is the main consideration from the Warrington .view- point. They wol4 because they• were better opportunists. Early on they realised that orthodox tactics were .useless, and adapted themselves to existing conditions, in so far as it was possible to do so. Barrow, on the other hand, clung to the usual methods, and, as a result, the story of the game, so far as tlify were concerned, is a long tale of soon-cnrrailed rushes and of passing movem:mits "nipped in the bud." The first minute's play yielded War- rington two points. Straight from the kick-off. the Wilderspool lads 'forced the ball into the quagmire fronting the visitors'. goal, and Pearnley made a mark. Brookes took the kick and put the ball nicely between the uprights. Barrow replied with a .determined attack, headed by Narey, but the ball went dead. It was some time, however, before Warrington could repel the invaders, and though they were never really very dangerous, they kept the defenders just nicely busy. At length a free- kick, awarded for off-side, enabled the home team to get the -ball away, and some mud- skating and wading was witnessed near the visitors' goal, during which many a player lueasured his length in the water-logged turf, and many a shirt and, pair of shorts lost the Pristine hue imparted by the laundress.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0216" n="art0216">
        <head>BARROW'S EQUALISER.</head>
        <p>BARROW'S EQUALISER.</p>
        <p>narrow were awarded a free-kick for "feet near the home goal. Narey took it, and Missed by inches, the shot being an excellent one, considering the sodden, and, consequently, heavy condition .of the leather. Warrington took the ball down to the other end of the field and kept it there for some time, during which the splashing and tackling in the big puddles afforded much merriment to the onlookers, and, in inverse ratio, much discomfort to the players. Barrow defended doggedly. and, though the contest was waged in dangerous proximity to their citadel, they managed to hold their oppon- ents at arms' length, and, presently, Spencer managed to clear his line and play was trans- ferred to the Warrington half, but not before Brookes had made an unuccessful attempt to kick another penalty goal. Barrow's incursion</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0217" n="art0217">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION Third QtialitAing Round.</head>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION Third QtialitAing Round.</p>
        <p>RYLANDS' RECS. v. ANCHOR CABLE CO. This match was played on the lir-street ground, Leigh, before a good gate. The Anchor Won the toss and elected'to play with the wind. Whittaker kicked off for llylands'. Higson, re- turning, the Cable pressed from the start, and Lyons scored at the corner. Allison failed at goal. This reverse seemed to rouse the "Wire- men," who, from the re-start, at once began to press. Potter charged a kick from Higson, but failed to reach the ball, before it went dead. Griffiths, in tackling Lyons, was badly kicked on the head, and had to leave the field. Both teams tried hard to score,4each end being visited in turn. liylands' again bad hard lines, Gallop kicking too far. The Anchor next worked their way to the home goal and from a scrummage, formed in nylands' "2.5." Gallop passed to Warren, who transferred to Potter, that player giving H. Povey a nice pass. H. Povey, in turn, passed to his brother, who raced in with a fine try from nearly half-way down the field. Warren goaled. Half-time Arrived with the score: Rylands',l goal 1 try (5 points); Anchor Cable, 1 try (3 points).</p>
        <p>The Anchor commenced the second half, Hor- rocks finding touch well 'down the field. Rylands' were' pressing hard at this point,. the Brothers Povey indulging in a paing movement, which resulted in H. Povey getting over, but the try was disallowed by the referee, for knocking on - over the line. Horrocks dropped a lovely goal. Warren retired hurt. Being now a man short, the Anchor gave the " Wiremen " a hot time, but the defence held good, the game ending with the score: Rylands' Recs., 2 goals 1 try (7 points); Anchor Cable, 1 try (3 points). Horrocks, the Brothers tiovey and Warren played well for the visitors, whilst, of the home team, Allison:Lyons and Higson were the pick.</p>
        <p>INTERMEDI FE 11l GBA LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>TO-DAY'S MATCHES. At Wilderspool. .. . __</p>
        <p>Heathside frogliall O.H. v. Beamont -United 0.B.; referee, Mr. W. Jones. In Victoria Park. Kick-off 2.15 p.m. St. Ann's 0.8. v. Arpley-street 0.B.; Mr. W. Bibby. --</p>
        <p>St. Barnalms' 0.8. v. Beiithside Bewsey 0.B.; Mr. J. Cooper. Wycliffe 0.8. v. St. James' Juniors 0.B.; M. J. Beardsell.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0218" n="art0218">
        <head>SATURDAY'S RESULTS:</head>
        <p>SATURDAY'S RESULTS:</p>
        <p>, - St. James' 0.8., 10 points; Bolton Council, 5, St. .Ifaiy's 0.8., 0; Beamont Athletic 0.8., 0, St. Alban's 11; St. Alban's "A*," 0. Heatliside Froghall, 15; .acred Heart, 3.</p>
        <p>The Prisoner's Challenge. The burly prisoner stood unabashed before the Judge. "Prisoner at the bar," asked the clerk, "do you wish to. challenge any of the jury?" The prisoner looked them over carefully. Well," he replied, " I'm not exactly wot you'd call in training. but I wouldn't mind a round or two with that there fat old josser in the corner."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0219" n="art0219">
        <head>HOW THE WINNING POINTS CAME.</head>
        <p>HOW THE WINNING POINTS CAME.</p>
        <p>When the teams crossed over with two (Ants each to their credit, the game was anybody's. Bad as it was at the outset, the state of the ground had now become .Iwful. and enything like scientific football was completely out of the question. Out of consideration for the drenched and mud-bespattered players, no in- terval was called. Barrow commenced the moiety with an attack, but it availed them nothing, and soon they were busy defending, Cox getting possession, from a bunch of oppon- ents, dribbling through and rushing to within a yard or two of the line before Dobson nipped up and dispossessed him. Continuing to press, Warrington were awarded a free-kick for ob- struction, but Brookes made poor use of the opportunity, mis-kicking and sending the ball to an opponent only two or three yards off. Warrington continued to press, but found that they could not prevail against the Barrow defence, though keeping play in the visitors' half of the field. Once a scoie seemed almost inevitable, a. scrum being forced near the Bar- row line; but Spencer got the ball away and play centred, for the next few minutes, in War- rington's territory. Eventually, however, the brothers •Cunliffe got going, and took the ball back into Barrow's quarters, and Warrington instituted a determined attack. Several times they were repulsed, but they came again each time and eventually their persistence met with reward, a penalty falling to their lot, and Dainteth, who took the kick, turning it to good account. Nothing further was scored, and when the long whistle sounded, the score-board read: Warrington, 2 goals (4 points); Barrow 1 goal (2 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0220" n="art0220">
        <head>DESERVED WIN.</head>
        <p>A DESERVED WIN.</p>
        <p>Warrington deserved their w iii. Their for- wards worked hard, undeterred by the mud and the water; indeed there were 'times when they appeared positively to. revel in the novel condi- tions under which the game was played. The whole sextet did their best, and to them is the major share of the credit for the victory—to them and to Dainteth, that is; for the half- back, temporarily transferred to the rear, put .up a first-rate game, all things considered. It is questionable whether the redoubtable Jolley —who is still busy recovering from his recent -injury, and who, it was hoped, would be able to play on Saturday, but, at .the last moment, was found to be unable to do so—could have done much .better than did his understudy. Tranter was the best of the home threequarters, who all did fairly well—everything being taken into account—and Laws and Brookes were 4 useful pair of halves. Like his vis-a-vis, Spencer played a sterling game at full-back for Barrow, and Corns, Dobson and Doyle got, in some sterling work on the threequarter line, whilst Ilickton was of great service at half-back. The Barrow front-rankers worked hard, but they were not the equals of the Warrington bunch, handling poorly and lacking initiative and re- source. Barrow and Warrington have now met three times this season, the rubber resting with the latter by the odd trick out of three. In the three matches Warrington have scored 17 points to Barrow's 11.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0221" n="art0221">
        <head>TO-DAY'S VISITORS.</head>
        <p>TO-DAY'S VISITORS.</p>
        <p>Will the Leeds Debacle Be Repeated?</p>
        <p>Leeds—from whom (on December 28th) War- rington received the worst beating they have experienced this season, the score being 35 Points to nil in favour of tlie Yorkshiremen— will be the visitors at WilderspoOl to-day, when it is to be, hoped that Warrington will be able to yedeem the reputation so recently and so seriously damaged at Headingley. The teams selected for to-day are : '</p>
        <p>Warrington. 1 Jolley. 5 Bradshaw, 3 Tranter, 4 Brookes, 2 Taberner</p>
        <p>7 Young, 6 Dainteth. 8 Cox, 9 Fearnley, 12 W. Cunliffe, 10 Mather,</p>
        <p>11 Green, 13 T. Cunliffe.</p>
        <p>8 F. Harrison, 9 F. Webster, 14 ,T. Chileott, 10 Godward, 11 G. Rees, 12 F. Carter, 17 F. blirfield 6 J. Sanders, 7 I. Jones.</p>
        <p>, 5 A. Jenkinsou, 4 W.A.Davies, 3 J. D. Campbell, 2 B. Ward. 1 D. D. Lewis: Leeds.</p>
        <p>The game will be refereed by Mr. E. Tonge (Swinton), and the touch-judges will be Messrs. R. Pye (Salford) and T. Riley (Leigh).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0222" n="art0222">
        <head>" A " TEAM AT ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>" A " TEAM AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>Warrington "A" will journey to St. Helens to-day to fulfil the return_ fixture with St. Helens Recs., who •visited ,Warrington on Box- ing Day and beat the locals by 10 points to 3. Warrington will be represented by the follow- ing :—Bramhall; Baker, Highani, Cherrett and Waywell ; Laws and Nicholls;. Harmer, Thorn- ton, Fairhurst, Porter, Logan and Hart.</p>
        <p>SALFORD SHIELD COMPETITION. Rylands' Recs. v. !Ham and Cadishead. At Cadishead, in the preliminary round of the above competition:—</p>
        <p>Rylands' : F.-Horrocks; J. Griffiths, G. War- ren, 11. Povey and C. Povey; 3. Gallop and A. Horrocks; F. Potter, E. Smith, W. Shaw, F. Baguley, A. Penn and J. Dumhell; team to meet at the Central Station at 1.30 p.m.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0223" n="art0223">
        <head>FIGHT AGAINST "-PRACTICAL ATHEISM."</head>
        <p>FIGHT AGAINST "-PRACTICAL ATHEISM."</p>
        <p>Sir Oliver Lodge, in an address at Bradford, referring to the war, s.aid it was a revolt against Christianity we were witnessing, and the deifi- cation of force and materialism. To say that there was no power above the State, and that if the State was only strong enough it had the right to everything that would conduce to its own benefit was practical atheism. This was no time for depression. We were living in great times, and this nation was allowed fo serve in a contest for the highest ideals of man. The re- sult of the war would ,be more and more to carry the world back to Christ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0224" n="art0224">
        <head>ANGLING.</head>
        <p>ANGLING.</p>
        <p>NVarrington Working Men's</p>
        <p>Association.</p>
        <p>N.A. CENTRE.</p>
        <p>Owing to the wretched weather that still con- tinues around this district angling is almost out of the question. The only stream where sport may be obtained is the Gowy. This waterway is more subject to a rise and fall than any other water of which we know, but local anglers will not take the risk of tramping the swampy meadows, and, after all, not catching the stream in fishing condition. We all know that in this most favoured spot the water can .be up and down again inside twenty-four hours, but is the risk worth it?</p>
        <p>The remainder of our fisheries are really not worth the time and trouble to pay them a visit in present conditions. It is generally known that all canals take a considerable time to re- cover, after such heavy rains. Canal fishing is now, and always has been, all the better after a spell of dry weather, and it is not much use going just because you catch a fine day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0225" n="art0225">
        <head>SPECIMENS GALORE.</head>
        <p>SPECIMENS GALORE.</p>
        <p>All streams have not been affected, as the wateis around Warrington have been. A writer in the "Fishing Gazette" of January 2nd speaks of the wonderful takes on the Avon. Mr. Wallis fished eight days, Mr. Beal six days, Mr. Woolley six days, or twenty days' fishing in all. The result was: 810 roach over 11b.; 250 roach over 1-1-Ib.; 100 roach over nib.; or an aver- age of 681 b. per day's fishing. They also took 145 chub, averaging 21b.; 25 grayling, averaging 11b.; 86 perch, averaging 11b.; 425 dace, averag- ing 60z.; 12 tench, averaging 21b.; making a total of 1,9451 b., or an average of just 97f1b. per day of fishing, After leaving out roach over 21b., and the pike, this works out at about 331 b. per rod per day. Other writers go on to say that this is a very meagre total in a classical river like the Avon, with anglers who know the game. We have had members who have taken 601 b. per rod per day on the Broads, but the conditions were far different, and their takes were at lea gt 50 per cent. brertin. In the con- ditions we have had these last six weeks, these results go to prove that the beautiful Hamp- shire Avon is the anglers' paradise. We should like to hear of a better, as we in War- rington have a few members who would no doubt spend their summer holidays in the dis- trict.</p>
      </div>
