      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0234" n="art0234">
        <p>White Star's 4-Nil Win over Elmwood's Injury-</p>
        <p>Weakened Team.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0235" n="art0235">
        <head>MORE BROKEN ENGAGEMENTS.</head>
        <p>MORE BROKEN ENGAGEMENTS.</p>
        <p>On Saturday the clubs in the Warrington and District League resumed their normal course of games, and at Paddington there was a fairly good crowd to witness the fixture between the local club and Longford.</p>
        <p>There was a great improvement in the weather and the game was contested under very agree- able conditions. In the first portion the play was very even, with a possibility of the home team gaining the lead as the interval drew near, but the visitors prevailed and no score was made. The second half opened more keenly, and a chance of gaining an early lead was thrown away. Then some strong raids were made on the home goal, but the defence proved equal to the attack until they had to yield their goal to a penalty kick. Following this the game was hotly contested and several times the home- sters were within an ace of equalising, but always failed at the critical moment. A few minutes before the finish the homesters were lucky in that their goal was not captured with a strong shot which hit the cross-bar. The game was won by the visitors by a goal from a penalty kick, a result which is always very unsatisfac- tory to the losers.</p>
        <p>The White Star had their neighbouring club, Elmwood Cons., as opponents, but on visiting the ground it was found to be in a flooded state and unfit to play upon. After a short consulta- tion between the officials of both clubs they de- cided to play the game on the Cons.' ground. Owing to a very strong wind, which greatly in- terfered with the play, neither side exhibited their usual form, and as the Cons. at the pres- ent time cannot Put their strongest, team in the field owing to players being on the Casualty list, it was not surprising that they were defeated. The White Star half-back line proved too good for the Cons.' forwards, and it was only on rare occasions that they threatened any danger. They had chances of scoring, but failed to accept them. Still, although the visitors gained the verdict by four goals to nil, their display did not warrant such a margin.</p>
        <p>St. Barnabass could not play their game with the Y.M.C.A. on their own ground owing to its flooded condition, but they were successful in obtaining a playing pitch in Victoria Park. However, there must have been an oversight on the part of some of the Saints' officials, as the appointed referee turned up at the Lovely-lane ground instead of the Park. The Y.M.C.A. had only nine players out, and I have been informed that it was decided to play the game as a friendly Exture, and that the Bank Quay secre- tary deputised for one of the absent-Young Men.</p>
        <p>The Bank Quay Recs. failed to travel to Earlestown, and, I am informed, did not advise the Mission that they were unable to come.</p>
        <p>Hollins Green failed to put in. an appearance at the Fairfield United ground to play their re- turn fixture with the last named. From in- formation to hand the excuse of the Greenites is very much similar to that of the man who wanted a day off. The man it may be remem- bered, went to his employer and told him he was not coming to work that day, and then asked him if he could have a day off. Several of the HollMs Green players came to the United dress- ing-rooms and said they could not raise a team to play, as some of their players had gone to Cadishead to play in a North Cheshire League match. I understand that Hollins Green were also represented in both teams engaged in that fixture, viz., Cadishead Wesleyans and the Lino- type. If this is correct it was not a very sports- manlike action on the part of the players con- cerned to go and leave their own club, because it does not appear to have a good chance of win- ning top place in the League in which it is entered.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath had Altrincham St. Mar- garet's as visitors on Saturday, and proved themselves superior at all points in the matter of attacking. Had it not been for the good defensive work of I the visitors' backs—who phowed sterling form throughout—the score against the Saints would have been much greater.</p>
        <p>The Heathens are very well pleased with their draw in the Altrincham Amateur Cup Competi- tion. The tie has to be decided on the 23rd inst. on the West-avenue ground. Their oppo- nents are the Cadishead Wesleyans, and as the Heathens have played them drawn games in both their League matches, there should be a keen contest when they oppose each other in the Cup fixture. The Heathens, having choice of ground,- may be regarded as favourites.</p>
        <p>Notes by "Linesman."</p>
        <p>hall, whose shot was only a foot wide of the Posts. At the opposite end, following an attack by the visitors, Fearnley hit the cross-bar with a great shot. For the home team, Holmes and Bramhall played a sound game, and Harrison, in goal, dealt with the shots that came his way very creditably. Spencer, at half-back, was a hard worker, and foiled the opposing front line many times. Wainwright, at outside right, would have done better had he been more liber- ally served, and Derbyshire improved the attack when he went to the centre position in the second half. Chamberlain did not uphold his form. For the visitors, Sturgess,, in goal, did what was required of him, but his backs were not as safe in their kicking as usual. Fearnley was the best of the half-backs, and Stevens did not come up to his recent form. Hands- and Hughes did' better in the later stages. Hough- ton had few chances. The result was: Long- ford, 1 goal; Paddington, niL ORFORD WHITE STAR. v. ELM WOOD. These teams should have met on the ground of the former club, but owing to the recent heavy rains their ground was partly flooded and unfit to play upon, and it was mutually ar- ranged to play on the latter club's ground. Thus the start was delayed a matter of fifteen min- utes. The players lined out as follows : Elm wood: Holt; J. Phoenix and Mellor; Pritchard, Hazeldine and Wallington; W. Phcenix, J. Smith, Kendrick, Hall and Lamb. White Star: Miller: Pilling and Evans; Gif- ford, Denny and Caldwell; Turner, Hankinson, Meachin, Stubbs and Gregory. The referee was Mr. J. Wright, The visitors won the toss and elected to de- fend the Cemetery goal, having the sun and wind at their backs. Kendrick set the ball roll- ing, and the visitors at once assumed the ag- gressive and made a raid into the -home terri- tory, where Pritchard returned the leather with a timely clearance. However, the ball was sent back by a hard drive, which, unfortunately, struck W. Phoenix in the face with such force that he had to retire for a short tima. For a while the game was contested in mid-field, but gradually the visitors' half-backs proved their superiority and forced the Cons. to act on the defensive, and it was only on rare occasions that .their forwards were able to get into the visitors' half, and when they did then their efforts were easily frustrated by the visitors' backs. It was from one of these breakaways that the ball was returned to the visitors' left wing, and, follow- ing a good centre, a melee ensued in the home goalmouth, during which Mellor handled the ball. Meachin was entrusted with the kick and Milt was successful in preventing the ball going between the uprights. But from the clearance, Meachin gained possession and drove the ball past Holt, thus scoring the visitors' first goal, after twenty-five minutes' play. From the centre kick the homesters rushed towards the visitors' goal, Smith being prominent in this movement. Hesitation, coupled with bad shoot- ing, enabled the visitors' defence to clear, and the White Star forwards, by good ,combination, transferred play to the home end again, where Meachin registered a second point for the visit- ors. The remaining portion of the first hall was tamely contested, and when the interval arrived the visitors were leading by 2 goals to none. On the teams resuming, the wind had abated somewhat, and the Cons. did not gain the same advantage from it as their opponents had done. But they managed to niake an attack on the visitors' goal. However, owing to bad finish- ing at the critical moment, they failed to avail themselves of the opportunities offered, and the visitors, giving an improved forward display, Hankinson and Turner combined well together, and the former notched the visitors' third goal. Following this reverse there was a spell of even play in mid-field, and then the homesters fell off in their play, and their citadel was again at- tacked. Caldwell, with a good pass, set Turner going on the extreme right, and his centre was returned up the field, when Gifford sent in a long shot which to all appearances was well over the cross-bar, but the wind caused the ball to curve, and Holt was completely deceived, the leather going through at the opposite end, of the goal. The remainder of the game was of a scrappy nature, and no, further goals were scored, the result being: Orford White Star, 4 goals; Elmwood Cons., nil. ST. BARNABAS' v. EAR LESTOWN Y. M C A.</p>
        <p>The above fixture was set down for decisio‘n at Lovely-lane, but owing to the unfit state of that ground, the game was played at Victoria Park. The visitors arrived with only nine players, and substitutes had to be found. The Saints won the toss and, played with the incline in their favour. The opening exchanges were in favour of the Saints, and they were soon attacking vigorously at the visitors' end, but met with a stubborn resistance, and were eventually re- pulsed. Darlington was brought down inside the penalty area, and Devonport, taking the kick, easily scored for the Saints. he visitors now contested the game more keenly, and by smart forward play they succeeded in equalis- ing the score. Encouraged by their success they again forced the Saints to defend their goal, and again captured the opponents' citadel. The half-time score was: 2 goals; St. Barnabas', 1 goal.</p>
        <p>The second half opened very taniely, and play was not interesting until Illidge gained posses- sion in mid-field and by a splendid individual effort succeeded in making the scores equal. A few minutes later a mistake by Long at full- back enabled the visitors to take the lead again. After this set-back the homesters roused up and made strong efforts to get on level terms once more, but the visitors defended very stoutly, and it was close upon time before Illidge again beat the backs, and equalised the score. The re- sult was: St. Barnabas', 3 goals; Earlestown Y.M.C.A., 3 goals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0236" n="art0236">
        <head>PADDINGTON'S NEW SECRETARY.</head>
        <p>PADDINGTON'S NEW SECRETARY.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Paddington Club, the Chairman of the Committee read a letter from Mr. H.-Range resigning the position of secretary. After consideration the resignation was accepted and Mr. A. Marsh, of the Dog and Partridge- row, Paddington, was elected to fill the vacancy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0237" n="art0237">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>PADDINGTON v. LONGFORD UNITED.</p>
        <p>The re-arranged fixture between the above clubs was played on the ground of the former, Mr. J. 'Smith having control of the following players : Paddington; Harrison.; Holmes and Brim- hail; Griffiths, Derbyshire and Spencer; WiVn- wright, Pritchard, Garnett, Brown and CVm- berlain.</p>
        <p>Longford: Sturgess; Mason and Lawrance;• Sherratt, Fearnley and Shelmardine; Houghton, Finch, Stevens, Hands and Hughes. • The visitors won the toss and elected to play with the sun at their backs, and against a strong wind. The opening exchanges were fairly even. Then the visitors, by long kicking, got near the home goal, but were easily repulsed by Holmes and Bramhall. Play was transferred to the visitors' end, where Mason and Lawrance were not so sure in their tackling, and Sturgess was called upon to clear, Several attempts were made by the visitors to invade the home terri- tory, but good play on the part of the home backs, who were aided by the wind, withstood all the attempts that were made. Stevens, on one occasion, broke away on the right and cen- tred across the goalmouth, but his colleagues failed to accept the opening. Following thia. the hontesters asserted themselves anti continu- ally kept the visitors on the defensive. Sturgess was called upon several times, but had no diffi- cult shots to contend with, while, at the oppo, site end, Harrison played the part of spectator. The interval arrived without any Score being made. .</p>
        <p>On the resumption, the wind was not so strong as in the first portion and the game was more keenly contested. Owing to a miskick by the visitors' backs, Derbyshire got through, but, when in front of goal and well inside the penalty area, he shot high over the bar. From this "let off" the visitors quickly made tracks for the home citadel, 'where Harrison effected several good clearances from long shots. Un- fortunately for the home defenders, who were playing a sound game, Spencer, of the half-back line, gave a penalty away by pushing an oppo- nent down. Stevens took the kick and was a trifle lucky in beating Harrison, as the latter just reached the ball, but failed to put it round the post. Following this reverse, the homesters played in a more determined fashion and made several strong attacks on the visitors' goal, but their efforts lacked finish, and Sturgess was not seriously troubled. However, the goal had a lucky escape from a free kick, taken by Bram-,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0238" n="art0238">
        <head>NORTH CHESHIRE LtAGUE. STOCKTON HEATH v. ALTRINCHAM ST. MARGARET'S.</head>
        <p>NORTH CHESHIRE LtAGUE. STOCKTON HEATH v. ALTRINCHAM ST. MARGARET'S.</p>
        <p>The above teams met on the West-avenue ground before a fair number of spectators. The, home team won the toss and played toward i the farm, with the wind at their backs; and, straight from the kick-off, made an attack on the visitors' citadel, but the custodian cleared in fine style. The home team continued to have the best of the exchariges, and kept up a bom- bardment on the visitors' goal, but their efforts met with a stubborn resistance from Shaw, Cotterill and Cowsill. It was only on rare occa- sions that the visiting forwards got into the home half and then their efforts were easily repulsed by the defenders. The wind proved a great factor in the game, and it was not .until a few minutes before the interval 'that the "Heathens" opened their account, and half-time arrived with the score one goal to nil in their favour.</p>
        <p>Upon changing ends it was expected that the visitors would improve upon their display, but their forwards' efforts were very weak and the home half-backs soon forced them to defend, and, after ten minutes, increased their lead. This reverse appeared to dishearten the visitors and had it not been for the splendid display-of their backs their citadel would have been cap- tured oftener. At the call of time the home team were winners by four goals to nil, Lloyd, Noden and Bates (2) being the goal scorers. Mr. A. Clare, of Lyrnm, was the referee. CADISHEAD WESLEYANS. v. LINOTYPE.</p>
        <p>On Saturday the prospective champions were visited by the Linotype. The home captain won the toss and allowed the visitors to have the aid of a strong wind. The game opened very evenly, but, aftk the visitors became more accustomed to the conditions, they made a sharp attack on</p>
      </div>
