      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0254" n="art0254">
        <head>DESERTERS AND ABSENTEES.</head>
        <p>DESERTERS AND ABSENTEES.</p>
        <p>SOLD AND PAWNED HIS KIT.</p>
        <p>When James Potts was charged at the Borough Police Court on Tuesday with being a deserter from the Birkenhead Bantam Bat- talion since January 3rd, it was stated that upon being arrested he admitted that he was a run-away, and said that he had sold some of his kit and pawned part of the remained.—He was remanded to the cells to await an escort. A FEW DAYS OVERDUE.</p>
        <p>George Bray and George Pownall admitted to the magistrates, at the Borough Police Court on Tuesday, that they had absented themselves from the 4th Battalion Of the South Lancashire Regiment, stationed in the South of England, and they were ordered to be handed over to the escort, which, it was stated, was on the way to fetch them back. The men had absented themselves since January 9th.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0255" n="art0255">
        <head>GAVE HIMSELF UP.</head>
        <p>GAVE HIMSELF UP.</p>
        <p>Joseph Howarth was charged on his own confession, at Tuesday's Police Court, with being an absentee from the 4th South Lanca- shire stationed in the South.—Superintendent Williamson said the man surrendered himself at the Police Station that morning, and the police had not yet had time to communicate with the military authorities.—Councillor Roberts (to prisoner) : Are you willing to go back straightaway?—Prisoner: Yes, sir; that is what I want to do.—lt was stated that he was fonr days overdue and he was remanded until arrangements were made for sending him back to his regiment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0256" n="art0256">
        <head>BETTER EUROPE. Peace to be an Abiding Presence.</head>
        <p>A BETTER EUROPE. Peace to be an Abiding Presence.</p>
        <p>Addressing on Sunday a meeting of men at the Leeds Coliseum, the Archbishop of York dis- cussed the moral and spiritual issues of the war. He said his only fear was that anything should occur that might make the spirit of the people flatter when the pinch of sacrifice began to press. He supported the Archbishop of Canterbury in the suggestion that we should abstain as an act of self-control from the use of alcohol during the continuance of the war, and he expressed the hope that if our armies were to be used as an instrument of Divine judgment it should be kept clean, and that the stern righteousness of our cause should not be soiled by personal malignity and hate. He looked finally for a better Europe, in which peace was not merely an uncertain guest, but an abiding presence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0257" n="art0257">
        <head>BANK QUAY MISSION'S GUESTS. Belgian Refugee Family Settled In Their New Abode.</head>
        <p>BANK QUAY MISSION'S GUESTS. Belgian Refugee Family Settled In Their New Abode.</p>
        <p>The Belgian Refugee family who arrived last Friday and who are the guests of the Bank Quay Working Men's Mission, !in a house in Golborne-street, have expressed themselves de- lighted with all they have seen. in Warrington. They have visited their friends at Penketh, and are getting quite used to the district: • They manage, their own affairs ai Golborne- street, and do their own shopping. Four of the ladies from the Mission constitute a Visiting Committee ,and attend at the house daily. On Wednesday the house was open for inspection to those who had contributed .in any way to the furvishing or upkeep of it. - The little Belgian boy who forms one of the family of refugees is attending Arpley-street School and is quite happy there.</p>
        <p>TIM E R FECTLY healthy children I are generally happy; but a very flight stomach trouble is often enough to make them miserable. ' °‘ That is the time to try a Steedman's Powder. You will find that it acts like magic. Steedman's Powders contain no poison or narcotic. '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0258" n="art0258">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY. JANUARY 16, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY. JANUARY 16, 1915.</p>
        <p>LEAGUE OF HELP.</p>
        <p>Services Rendered by Members of</p>
        <p>Workingmen's Mission.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0259" n="art0259">
        <head>NO INDISCRIMINATE GIVING.</head>
        <p>NO INDISCRIMINATE GIVING.</p>
        <p>The sixth annual meeting of the League of Help in -tonnection with the Working Men's Mission, Bank Quay, was held on Tuesday evening, the President (Mr. G. Dale) 'seing in the chair.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0260" n="art0260">
        <head>YEAR'S WORK.</head>
        <p>THE YEAR'S WORK.</p>
        <p>The annual report set forth that the work of the League during the past twelve .months had been exceptionally quiet, notwithstanding the outbreak of war. Fifty-two grants and twelve Christmas gifts were made, the total thus disbursed being 3s. less than in the previous year. The fact that the League was not called upon more than in the previous twelve months, the retort opined, spoke well for trade in War- rington, its condition being something for the inhabitants to be thankful for, when they con- sidered the state of the people of some other towns. '4 •</p>
        <p>The balance sheet appaided to the report showed a total income of ,21.5 17s. 10d., and a balance in hand, at the end of the year, of 43 58. 9d.</p>
        <p>Report and balance sheets were passed.</p>
        <p>ABOUT £lBO DISTRIBUTED IN GRANTS. In the course of a brief address to the mem- bers present, The President said that the League had been. in existence a little over six years, and during thalt time had paid out in grants about £lBo— average of .Y. 30 per year. With the exception of about £lO, the whole of the money had been subscribed by members of the Mission. In addi- tion to the money, clothing, shoes, bedding, &amp;c., to the value of ..t3O or so had been given away, most of the. articles having been contributed by Mrs. John Crosfield, and Mr. and Mrs. Tanner, whose kindness they much appreciated. Apart from their own members, there were few people who recognised the importance of the ,great work which the League was doing. They were sometimes told by the ministers and members of other churches that they looked upon the Working Men's Mission as one of the most suc- cessful churches in Warrington. The members themselves ought not, perhaps, to express an opinion on that point, but there was one thing of which they might be sure, and that was that a church which tried to do its duty' to the poor would receive the Divine approval.</p>
        <p>Splendid Support.</p>
        <p>The President" went on it; thank the various sections of the Mission for the splendid way in which they had supported the League. There were people, lacking experience in such matters, who expressed the opinion that work of the kind done by the League had aipauperising ten- dency, and that it attracted to the church which took it up a number of people who only came for the "loaves and fishes." However, six years' experience of League of Help work had placed him and the other officials in a position to disprove that allegation. Slipshod methods would no doubt produce such results, but when a well-constituted committee, consisting Of workers who knew the district; took up the in- vestigation work on proper lines, the results would, in nearly every instance, be satisfactory. An organisation dealir g efficiently with the pro- blems that confronted them, as did the League of Help, would never attract persons of the un- desirable class.</p>
        <p>" Undesirables " Steer Clear.</p>
        <p>On the contrary, as a rule, people of that class did 'their best to avoid such organisations, as was proved by the experience of the Warring- ton Citizens' Guild of Help. During the first year of the existence of that Guild, cases of attempted imposture were frequent, but now they were very rare. Of course, where churches or societies gave financial help indiscriminately, without the aid of a committee, and with no means of making the necessary investigations, the results would be disappointing. An import- ant feature of the work of the Lague of Help was its freedom from narrowness and bigotry. People of other churches had been helped, in- cluding the Roman Catholics and Protestants of various denominations. When the committee were satisfied, help was given without any ques- tion as to the religious tendencies of the person in need. It must not be imagined that the League Committee gave financial help when some other course could be adopted. Fre- quently, financial help was given whilst a family was passing thrangh some crisis, or where some- one was temporarily out of work, but in every case they tried to adopt the best and quickest means of again placing those concerned under normal conditions. In conclusion, the President trusted that in their work during the forthcom- ing year, the League would continue to cultivate high ideals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0261" n="art0261">
        <head>Our Duty to _Serve.</head>
        <p>Our Duty to _Serve.</p>
        <p>They should realise that it was their duty to serve. No man, however wealthy, had a right to speak of himself as independent. The richest man in the world was a very dependent person, and was a debtor to his fellows.</p>
        <p>prietors o.</p>
        <p>THE EUPHEiVIISTS. _ KAISER: "I say, how are you going to explain away the surrender of your Army Corps in the Caucasus?" SULTAN OF TURKEY: "Nothing simpler. I shall say, Our gallant troops determined to embarrass the enemy's commissariat, out their object with overwhelming success.'" KAISER: "Splendid t Couldn't hava put it better myself." SULTAN: "My dear boy, we were in &amp;the business, ages and ages before you Wete thought of."</p>
        <p>BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>Dr. Peacocke's " Rise " Discussed.</p>
        <p>SUPERANNUATION.</p>
        <p>Important Questions Raised by</p>
        <p>ftlenabers.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians was held at the Work- house on Wednesday, lr. A. J. Jolley presiding.</p>
        <p>ANOTHER £2O A YEAR FOR THE CHILDREN'S DOCTOR.</p>
        <p>It was reported that at a meeting of the Padgate Homes Committee a letter was read from the doctor to the homes, Dr. Peacocke, asking for an increase in salary. It was moved by Mr. Mather, and seconded by Mr. Bent, that the doctor's salary be increased from £3O to „t. 50 per annum. The proposition was carried by six votes to four, and there were two mem- bers who did not vote.</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. M. Mitchell said the amount was not sufficient now, having regard to the fact that the 'doctor had such a long record of service at his back, and that he had been underpaid for a great number of years. Other reasons why he should • have an increase were that so much more was required of a medical man at the homes than elsewhere, and that so much more scientific knowledge was demanded. He (the speaker) proposed that the salary be doubled, and that, the doctor should have £6O instead of £5O. If the proposal was not in order he would support the £5O. lle thought fhat when they were recognising the services of a medical officer at the homes, who was an independent medical officer and had no superior over him, he ought to have that amount of recognition which would be an encouragement to him in the future. It would also be a little expression of thankful- ness for what he had done in the past. The doctor had done his work very well indeed, and he (the speaker) was of the opinion that the Board ought to encourage to the utmost of their power officers who did their duty. By granting an additional £lO to the proposed £5O, and making the figure £6O, they would be act- ing wisely, and would have the approval of the outside public. ,</p>
        <p>Mr. Knowles Are we allowed to speak on this without proposing or seconding? The Chairman: You can speak to the minutes of the committee. Does a6-one second Mr. Mitchell? Miss Broadbent: I second. What About the Ratepayers?</p>
        <p>. Mr. Knowles said he knew Dr. Peacock° very well, and had the greatest admiration for him, but one had also to have some regard for rate- payers' money. Whilst he never liked to go against a. committee who had gone into all the facts, he would say that if he had been on that committee he would not have been a party to increasing any man's salary by more than half of what he was getting. "The present increase to ..e5O is larger than I should have felt.justified in supporting," continued Mr. Knowles. "At these times ratepayers are not all standing at the bottom of a pipe which is running with sovereigns. Men have to work hard in differ- ent businesses in these days to make ends meet, and these are not times when money should be thrown away by people—l would say more if there were not a lady seconder--who are ` fads ' on certain subjects. I think the committee have gone far enough in what they have done, and I don't propose anything to better that." On a vote being taken, the Rev. A. M. Mitchell's amendment was lost, and the Chair- miln declared the minutes of the committee con- fined</p>
        <p>Mr. Mather: There is one question the Board ought to discuss, and that is that when a com- mittee met, like the Padgate Homes Committee did yesterday, and come to an almost unanimous decision on a matter, their decision is not treated IN ith respect- here, where a member brings the matter up, and has it debated again. He had his chance of saying everything yester- day. To bring the matter up again and take up our time—well, I think there ought to be some expression of opinion on the action.</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: It is quite in order, Mr., Chairman?</p>
        <p>The Chairman: Yes. It is not a question of "ir_ order," but a question of propriety.</p>
