      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0262" n="art0262">
        <head>MIDNIGHT SUN.</head>
        <p>MIDNIGHT SUN.</p>
        <p>Philomaths Listen To Lecture on Norway.</p>
        <p>GLORIES OF GLACIER AND FJCRD.</p>
        <p>Switzerland is generally regarded as the happy hunting ground for the Continental tourist in search of the beautitil ..and the majestic in Nature, but that he had sampled both Conntrks and- ,preferrecr- Norway, with its magnificent fjords. awe-inspiring glaciers, and wonderful waterfalls, was the statemeht miile to the mem- bers of the Philontathic Society--iasseinbled at their headquarters i goodly .- numbers on Wednesday -evening—by Mr. L. Robinson, who lectured in interesting fashion on the land of the midnight sun, his remarks, being illustrated by a beautiful series of lantern slides, made by, him- self from photographs taken on the occasion of his comparatively recent visit to the land from which came the hardy Vikings, in their ungainly galleys,, to the conquest of Britain.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0263" n="art0263">
        <head>AN UNFATHOMABLE FJORD.</head>
        <p>AN UNFATHOMABLE FJORD.</p>
        <p>- Having described the voyage across the North Sea, the lecturer gave his audience photographs of the first fjord glimpsed—one near Stavanger and drew attention to the precipitous slopes descending almost from the clouds, and to the fact that their sides were but sparsely covered with vegetation. That, he said, was typical of Norway, and it was said—it was one of the pretty fancies of the Norwegians—that when the angels were distributing the earth they overlooked the rugged Scandinavian coast line, subsequently scraping up handfuls of soil and placing them where it would stick. (Laughter:, The fjord into which the vessel first sailed was so deep that anchor could not be dropped, but the vessel had, so to speak, to be tied up to the shore. The sounding With the lead recorded 78 fathoms (468 feet) without finding bottom. Near to this fjord innumerable cascades and fall abound, the best of them being the Laatefos a wonderful sight—hard by which is a tablet, carved in the rock, to the memory of a German officer, who is said to have lost control of his bicycle and to have fallen into the ravine. The local story, however, was to the effect that the officer was, with others, invited to join the Kaiser at champagne—his Majesty being then on one of his numerous cruises to Norway—and that the Emperor, having said something 'dis- paraging about the officer's mother, the officer struck his Majesty and then committed suicide. Anyhow, the Kaiser had the memorial tablet erected, and every year he placed a wreath upon it. It might be that his conscience pricked him—if he had one! (Laughter.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0264" n="art0264">
        <head>EUROPEAN NIAGARA.</head>
        <p>THE EUROPEAN NIAGARA.</p>
        <p>Having described the waterfall at Skieggadal- fos—" the Niagara of Europe "—and having spoken of a five-hour railway journey, during which 84 tunnels are negotiated, the lec- turer went on to describe the voyage from Bergen to Trondjhem, and to speak of the curious little vehicles—in which the driver sits behind his passengers—known as stolkjerries, and the ponies attached to which are blinker- less and invariably walk up an incline and generally consider it undignified to even trot down a slope. (Laughter.) "The reason," ex. plained, the lecturer, "why these ponies do not wear blinkers is that there are no railways along the sides of the roads, so that they can see for themselves what sort of a drop there is if they happen to go over the edge." (Laugh- ter.)</p>
        <p>Trondjhem, the lecturer reminded his audi- ence, is the largest city in so high a northerly latitude. It was founded by the first Christian king of Norway in 996 A.D., and after his fall at the battle of Stilkstad, where he was vanquished by Canute the Great, his body was taken there and a church was erected over his grave. After many alterations and restorations, this churcb became the famous cathedral and metropolitan church of Norway. The original church has been burnt down, and the present restoration was commenced in 1869, and it is calculated that the work will be finished in 1925. Accord- ing to the Constitution, all Kings of Norway shall be crowned in the Cathedral of Trond jhem, and the last coronation was that of Kim Haakon and Queen Maud, which took place in 1906.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0265" n="art0265">
        <head>NATURE'S NIGHTLIGHTS</head>
        <p>NATURE'S NIGHTLIGHTS</p>
        <p>Talking of Trondjhem, the lecturer recalled to the minds of his auditors the fact that in summer it never goes dark in that part of-Nor— way, and told how that a few minutes after midnight he had seen members of his party reading newspapers without the aid of artificial light. Norwegian farmers, listeners were in- formed, have a, to us, peculiar way of drying their hay. They do not stack it in bundles, as the people of this country do, but spread it on to a framework, the idea being that the wind and sun dried it much quicker. The lecturer pictured the glories of Lake Loen, the scenery surrounding which affords, the greatest variety of views; fine mountain ranges, crowned with eternal snow and ice, being intersected by beau- tiful green valleys. Half-way across the lake</p>
        <p>Continued in next column.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0266" n="art0266">
        <head>GUARDIAN'S ELECTION.</head>
        <p>GUARDIAN'S ELECTION.</p>
        <p>Candidates for Bewsey Ward Vacancy. .</p>
        <p>Mrs. Simpson having, in consequence of leav- ing the district, resigned her seat on the War- rington Board of Guardians, the question rela- tive to the appointment of a new member for Bewsey was raised at the fortnightly meeting of the Board on Wednesday, Mr. A. J. Jolley pre- siding. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell asked if the notices for the election Were out.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: The notice is posted up here at the Workhouse., The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: The notice I read stated "any time .in 1915," which is certainly a little "off."</p>
        <p>The Clerk: The notice the reverend gentleman 'read was the statement as to the persons nom- inated, and that is exactly a copy of the legal form. If he will read the notice of election before him he will find the whole information there. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: I have not received it. Where is it to be seen outside the Board Room?</p>
        <p>The Clerk: On the gate here, at the office in Bewsey-street, and on all the hoardings in Bewsey Ward.</p>
        <p>TWO CANDIDATES NOMINATED. Two nominations have been received for the 'vacant seat, the candidates being :</p>
        <p>Mr. Wiliam J. Shaw, master plumber, 89, DaHam-lane; and</p>
        <p>Mrs. Mary Roberts, widow, 2,,Kendrick-street.</p>
        <p>Mr. Shaw is ,proposed by James Partridge; Imperial Hotel; John Hankinson, 21, Folly-lane; Arthur W.-Baker, 174, Winwick-road; Margaret Brophey, Farmers' Arms, Dallam-lane; jai* John Holden, 35, Folly-lane. His seconders are Fred: Wilkinson, Old Ball Hotel, Winwick- street ; Reuben ,Iligham, 19, Winwick-road ; Michael Flynn, 79, Dallam-lane; Emily Powell, Vulcan Inn, Dallam-lane; and Horace Penketh Brandwood, 22, Pierpoint-street. '</p>
        <p>Mrs. Roberts is proposed by James Kenyon, 47, Hoyle-street, and seconded by John Blake, 93, Bewsey-road. Notice of the poll will be issued on January 21st, and the election itself will take place on Thursday, January 28th.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0267" n="art0267">
        <head>LADY CANDIDATE.</head>
        <p>THE LADY CANDIDATE.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Roberts is a widow, whose late husband was prominently identified with the Liverpool Victoria Society, which was established in War- rington by his father. Mrs. Roberts is well known in the town, and for 20 years kept a restaurant in Tanner's-lane, retiring from busi- ness 14 years ago. She is a prominent member of the Women's Liberal Association, and takes a keen and practical interest in every organisa- tion which has for its object the helping of those who, by circumstances, are unable to look after themselves. She is a member of the League of Help and is on the Distress Committee, the Sol- diers' and Sailors' Families' Association Com- mittee, and the Insurance Committee. For many- years she has been a regular worshipper 'at the Wycliffe Congregational Church.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0268" n="art0268">
        <head>MIDNIGHT SUN—continued.</head>
        <p>MIDNIGHT SUN—continued.</p>
        <p>the grand, rolling glacier, Halseth, is constantly pushing enormous masses of blue ice down the steep side of the mountain, to an accompani- ment of loud cracking noises, followed by the thundering of the immense avalanches as they are 'loosened from the glacier. In the spring of 1905 there was a big fall of rock into the lake which caused a wave a hundred feet in height and lifted the lake steamer over an island, itseli 45ft. above the water, ultimately leaving the boat a quarter of a mile inland. At the same time two villages were submerged, with terrible loss of life. At Bergen, an interesting place to visit, is the fish market, where the tourist may see all kinds of beautifully coloured fishes swim- ming about the tanks. A purehaser desiring a fish points the particular one out, and it is im- mediately captured for him, weighed, cleaned and handed over.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0269" n="art0269">
        <head>PEOPLE THRIFTY AND HONEST.</head>
        <p>PEOPLE THRIFTY AND HONEST.</p>
        <p>The Norwegians, said the lecturer by way of conclusion, are a pleasant people. They are not effusive, but they make visitors welcome. They liked to sell their goods, but they did not try to cheat the foreigner in the matter of his change. They are honest and self-reliant. thrifty and frugal, and have immense patience and a kind of stolid courage which nothing can shake. In some of the outlying districts the party came across people who were so poor that they clubbed together to buy a cabbage. The beer of the country, added Mr. Robinson, amid laughter, is considered excellent by the natives, but from an Englishman's point of view it is no stronger than ginger-beer. , The lecturer was accorded a hearty vote of thanks, on the proposition of Mr. E. G. Burgess Mr. Norman Nield occupied the Uair.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0270" n="art0270">
