      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0271" n="art0271">
        <head>RETURN OF SUPERANNUATION CONTRIBUTIONS.</head>
        <p>RETURN OF SUPERANNUATION CONTRIBUTIONS.</p>
        <p>Au important point with regard to the super- annuation payments of the Poor Law officials Was raised when Mr. WKinley proposed the fol- lowing resolution: "That the Guardians, act- ing under powers granted by Section 7 of the Poor Law Officers' Superannuation Act, 1896, return to the Rev. J. Walmsley, late Roman Catholic instructor at the Cottage Homes and Schools, the sum of £1 4s. Bd., being the amount contributed by him under the Act." Mr. M'Kinley said that the Rev. J. Walmsley had not resigned his position, but had been called away by the Archbishop to take the place of a dead priest. The letter which had been re- ceived from the. Archbishop seemed to have mis- led some members. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: Is the word "com- pulsory" used?</p>
        <p>The -Chairman : In My opinion it will be necessary for the ,Clerk to communicate with the Local Government Board to ascertain if this payment can be properly made. The question is: "Has Father Walmsley resigned or not?" His letter was undoubtedly a letter of resigna, tion. If that is so we cannot legally pay him anything. Was it a volintacy resignation, in the strictest sense, or was it compulsory, seeing that he is under the Archbishop, and has to obey orders? The amount is small, but there is an important principle behind the matter. Mr. M'Kinley : It has beeu granted befo:e iu si ii!iar cases</p>
        <p>Tho Chairman: If an officer resigns vti tarity, the Guardians' cannot repay him any- thing.</p>
        <p>An Objection on Principle.</p>
        <p>Mr. Mather: I object to it on principle. The, time is coming when we ought to review these things. When we Come to consider what super- annitotion is for, and what it means in case of accident, disablement or ill-health, let us think of what public bodies like insurance societies would do. They would never give back to an insured person any contribution he had paid if he left the society. Here we take the risk; as ratepayers, of giving these people the superan- nuation, and I must say that public bodies, such as Guardians, treat their servants who do become disabled in a very liberal manner. I say review the whole circumstances, and stop veying, back any superannuation money that ought, not to be paid. . The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: That is the point: " Ought not to be." Mr. Mather: I say "that ought not to be." The Rev. A. At. Mitchell: That is begging the question. The Chairman: Excuse Me! No! It is a point that has been raised before, but, never fully discussed by the Boai d. because in each individual case anyone who takes the line of principle is bound to give offence to someone. Mr. Mather is quite right in saying that this is, in effect, like a policy in insurance or that it is like a person asking for his premium back again. The Board have repeatedly_ paid sums of this kind. Mr. M'Kinley's motion is in accordance with what has been done before. On the last occasion I objected, on the lines taken by Mr. blather. I do not 6reak on this resolu- tion, but on the broader lines of the principle of the thing, Mr. Mather did not want to pass a censure, but if we are going to take the feeling of the Board as to whether we ay back all superan- nuation money under any conditions, I should vote against the resolutim.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0272" n="art0272">
        <head>What An Insurance, Company Would Do.</head>
        <p>What An Insurance, Company Would Do.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. Knowles said he was bound to support Mr. Mather. If they went to an ordinary r.,- surance company and said they had .paid for a raan who was 75 years of age; and had not died, and therefore they wanted their money back the officials would laugh. Commonsense was corn monsense, and when they went to the Boar meeting they ought to go with . the feelings which would be dictated to them in the conduct 44- business outside. (Hear, hear.) They to return the premiums, and seeing . present amount was so small, they had -tunity of refusing without injuring . • ;nd of establishing the principle for • • • • ,•lication. I think otherwise. (Hear, hear.) - rhy we should take this course when • am like this under consideration, have done the same thing before, circumstances. Make a question</p>
        <p>OU SU</p>
        <p>.-Th</p>
        <p>'</p>
        <p>of the matter and decide for another case that might come under consideration. It would Ie an injustice to do what Mr. Knowles suggests now, when we have made the grant under similar circumstances before. Decide on the broad question when there is no case before yoa. The Chairman: But if you think it is vi rang now, it is wrong every time. Mr. Furness said he considered the time had arrived when the matter ought to be gone into carefully by the Board. They paid public money for the purpose of insuring their ser- vants. They could not say to an insurance company, "There is no claim. We want our money back." It was not just to the ratepayers, neither was it their duty as Guardians to pay a man back his contributions, because he had not drawn anything. it was a case of "Heads I win, tails you lose." Mr. Blum les : Exactly so. Mr. Furness: I don't oppose the present reso- lution, but I say we ought to establish the line we shall take in the future.</p>
        <p>The Law on the Point.</p>
        <p>The Clerk: All that you have paid has been legal, for the officers had a proper claim on you. The Act states that "In all cases except voluntary resignation, or dismissal for miscon- duct," you have to pay the contributions back. Are you satisfied that the case before you is one of a voluntary resignation or not? The cir- cumstances are such that if the Local Govern- ment Board were asked they would give their sanction to the repayment. It would not be in order to say you would not do this sort of thing in future.</p>
        <p>Mr. Flanagan: Father Walmsley had no option. He had to do as he was told. Mr. M'Kinley said he did not think there would have been so much controversy over the matter, and it was rather unfortunate that there had been any. When cases of a similar character had been brought before the Board, cases in which larger amounts- The Chairman: Not similar. Mr. M'Kinley : Practically similar. money was returned.' The Chairman: Do you mind giving the name? Mr. M'Kinley : Mrs. French.</p>
        <p>The</p>
        <p>The Clerk: It was on similar lines. The Board were informed that they need not pay the amount without the sanction of the Local Government Board, and I was instructed to ask them for their sanction. I did, and you got it. Mr. M'Kinley : Then why make an exception in this case?</p>
        <p>The resolution was carried by eight votes to six. Two members did not vote. Mr. M'Sinley : Now is the time for someone to give notice of motion on the matter for future cases.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0273" n="art0273">
        <head>SINGINC TO THE POOR.</head>
        <p>SINGINC TO THE POOR.</p>
        <p>Clara Butt's Concert Party At Dutton Worßhouse.</p>
        <p>When, soon after the outbreak of war, it became clear that among the first of the pro- fessions to suffer financially would be the musical, Madame Clara Butt, the world-famous songEltress, and her no, less famous husband, Mr. Kennerley Rumford, cast around them to see what they could do to help their less-fortunate brethren of the concert platform, and they hit upon the happy' idea of laying • the matter before the profession as a whole, and before the public, coupling the statement of the fact that. the European con- flict has hit the musician very hardly with an appeal to the gener- osity of the long-pursed public. The result has been the raising of a large sum of money, but instead of it being doled out in-the shape of charity, it is being paid to a large number of excellent artistes in the shape of fees, in return for which they are asked to give their services at a number of concerts to be given to the inmates of workhouses and such like institu- tions, Mr. and Mrs. Kennerley Rumford being themselves big donors to the fund. Last week one of the parties visited the Dut- ton Workhouse, and treated the officials and inmates—to say nothing of many members of the Board of Guardians and the Ladies' Visit- ing Committee and their friends—to a splen- did concert, the like of which, as the Chairman (Mr. C. E. Linaker, jun.) phrased it, could only in normal times have been heard by going to some big town and paying a big price for a seat. The artistes were Miss Edith Kirkwood (soprano), Miss Grace Ivell (soprano), Mr. Hur- bert Baker (tenor). Mr. Tom Fussell (violinist), and Mr. Edward Brightwell (pianist).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-16_WidnesExaminer_art0274" n="art0274">
        <head>GUARD!ANS' VOTE OF THANKS.</head>
        <p>GUARD!ANS' VOTE OF THANKS.</p>
        <p>At the meeting of the Guardians on Tuesday a hearty ATpte of thanks was accorded the party. The Chairman : I attended, expecting to hear. a good concert, but when I had heard it I made up my mind tlrat it Was the best I had heard outside London. To speak in "slang'," they aro " top-hole " artistes and cannot be beaten. Those who did not attend missed a treat. Mr. Spruce: All the Guardians who came were delighted.. -</p>
