        <p>MEIMMINIOL '... g A NEW. ViefLUME 0F,.. ......._ P ...„ ,„. ..t ..„ : , ri , COMMENCES ON JAN. 6• cl. , ..,... i NOW is tile time to take t . in regularly and provide a weekly joy for those at home, or to enter a subscription for a . •• friend or relative. Tell your Newsagent to deliver "Punch" to you. Send orders for its delivery by post to the Secretary, "Punch" , • --"- e" 7 ) Offices, Bouverie Street, London, E.C. One year's supply of "Punch" in Great Britain (or to a Member or the Forces during the War to any address throughout the World) costs 16/-. Outside the U.K. (other than to a .1 . ;-I Member of the Forces) the cost is 18/6. A 1-N 1426</p>
        <p>the home val. The ball was passed out to Atkinson, who was well placed, and he had not much difficulty in placing it between the up- rights. This reverse spurred the home team to more strenuous efforts, and Roberts, in the visitors'. goal, had to handle. Not to be denied, the home forwards attacked again, and this time Stokes made the scores equal. The home team now warmed to their work, and, despite the splendid defence of the visitors, goals were added by Lowe and Arstall, the half-time score being: Wesleyans, 3 goals; Linotype, 1 goal. Upon changing ends, the home team had the aid of the wind, and were soon in front of the visitors' goal, and, after a few minutes' play, Taylor scored a fourth goah The visitors were only able to make occasional raids on the home goal, but from one of these Bradbury scored the visitors' second point, the home goalkeeper being at fault. From this, on to the close of the game, the home team had the lion's share of the play, and further goals were added by Stokes, Taylor and Lowe. At the call of time the hEme team were easy winners by 7 goals to 2.. The referee was Mr. G. Knight, of Stock- ton Heath. MATCHES FOB TO-DAY. WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. Grappenhall v. Newton Cors. Hollins Green v. Longford United: Orford White Star v. Earlestown St. John's. Warrington -Central v. Elmwood Cons. NORTH CHESHIRE LEAGUE. Altrincham St. George's v. Stockton Heath. FRIENDLIES. Paddington v. Fairfield United. Paddington v. St. Barnabas', on the Fairfield ground. THE ENGLISH CUP. Hovers Beaten By Second League Team. The first round in the Association Cup. foot- ball tournament was played on Saturday. The highest attendance was at the Chelsea-Swindon Town match at Stamford Bridge, where 28,000 people were present. Last year there were nine' gates of over 20,000 in the first round, but this year there were only two, though at many matches there were over 18,000 persons present. The great surprise of the round was the defeat of the holders of the English League Cham- pionship, Blackburn Rovers, by a second divi- sion Southern League team, Swansea Town. Leeds City beat Derby County at Derby, but Sunderland fell to the Tottenham Hotspur team at Tottenham. There were five Matches drawn, and the game between Bristol Rovers and Southend could not be played at Bristol because of the floods LORD ROSEBERY AND COMPULSION. Lord Rosebery, speaking at Dalkeith, said he thought that if sufficient recruits were not ob- tained by the voluntary system some system of compulsion would very soon be inaugurated. He believed this would not be unwelcome to the 'great mass of his fellow-countrymen, who, while they would be willing to serve under some Im- perial mandate, did not consider it fair that they should serve while others iemained at home. •</p>
